window.WALKBCN_I18N["en"] = {"stops":{"sagrada-familia":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"In 1882 a modest neo-Gothic church began here. A year later young Antoni Gaudí took over, threw out the plan, and spent 43 years on it. Asked why it was so slow, he said: my client is not in a hurry. Look up at the columns, branching like trees in a stone forest.","normal":"In 1882 a modest neo-Gothic church began here. A year later a young architect named Antoni Gaudí took over, and threw out the plan. He spent the next 43 years on it, living in his workshop near the end. When asked why it was taking so long, he said: my client is not in a hurry. Look up at the columns — they branch like trees, because Gaudí wanted the inside to feel like a forest.","long":"In 1882, builders started work right here on a modest neo-Gothic church. The first architect was Francisco de Paula del Villar. A year later, the project passed to a young architect named Antoni Gaudí, and he did something bold: he threw out the plan and started again. He spent the next 43 years on it. In his final years he gave up other commissions and devoted himself to this church alone, and near the end he was living in his workshop on the site. People kept asking why it was taking so long. His answer has become famous: my client is not in a hurry. He was talking about God. Gaudí died in 1926, after being struck by a tram, and he is buried in the crypt beneath the building. The work carried on for generations after him, paid for largely by donations and, these days, by visitors' tickets. Now look up at the columns. They don't rise straight like ordinary pillars. They lean and branch, splitting the way a tree does, because Gaudí wanted the inside to feel like a forest. Let your eye follow one from the base to where it divides, and see if you can spot where trunk becomes branch. Before you move on, step aside from doorways and keep the pavement clear. People live and work all around here, and this is their neighbourhood too.","new_facts":["The first architect was Francisco de Paula del Villar.","In his final years Gaudí gave up other commissions to work only on the Sagrada Família.","His workshop was on the building site.","By 'my client' Gaudí meant God.","Gaudí died in 1926 after being struck by a tram.","Gaudí is buried in the crypt of the Sagrada Família.","Construction has been funded largely by donations and, today, by visitors' tickets.","The columns lean rather than standing straight."]},"architecture":{"short":"Gaudí wanted a church that felt like a forest. Look up at the towers, then imagine stepping inside, where columns split and branch like trees holding up a canopy. It wasn't the original plan: Gaudí scrapped an ordinary neo-Gothic design and shaped this one for 43 years. Please keep the pavement clear for neighbours.","normal":"Here's something daring: Gaudí wanted a church that felt like a forest. Look up at the towers rising above you, then imagine stepping inside, where the columns split and branch like trees, holding the roof the way trunks hold a canopy. That idea wasn't in the original plan. Work began in 1882 on a fairly ordinary neo-Gothic church, and when a young Antoni Gaudí took over a year later, he set that design aside and started again. He shaped it for 43 years. Take your time, and leave the pavement clear for the neighbours who live here.","long":"Here's something daring: Gaudí wanted a church that felt like a forest. Start by looking up at the towers rising above you. Notice their rounded, tapering shapes, more like something that grew than something that was built. Now imagine stepping inside. The columns split and branch like trees, holding the roof the way trunks hold a canopy. Many of them lean slightly, too, and that's not just for looks. By tilting the columns to follow the weight, Gaudí could do without flying buttresses, the external stone props that hold up so many Gothic cathedrals. You can see that love of nature outside as well. On the Nativity façade, the part built under Gaudí's own direction, the stone is crowded with carved plants and animals, as if the wall were alive. Inside, stained glass fills the forest with coloured light that shifts through the day. None of this was in the original plan. Work began in 1882 on a fairly ordinary neo-Gothic church, and when a young Antoni Gaudí took over a year later, he set that design aside and started again. He shaped it for 43 years, and builders have carried his ideas forward ever since. Take your time, and let your eye travel up the towers. And as you do, leave the pavement clear for the neighbours who live here; this is their everyday street.","new_facts":["Many of the interior columns are inclined.","The inclined, branching columns let Gaudí avoid the external flying buttresses used in Gothic cathedrals.","The Nativity façade was built under Gaudí's own direction.","The Nativity façade is carved with plants and animals.","Stained glass windows fill the interior with coloured light.","Construction continued after Gaudí's death."]},"history":{"short":"Picture 1882: builders laying the first stones of a modest neo-Gothic church. A year later young Antoni Gaudí took over and threw out the plan. Asked why it took so long, he said: my client is not in a hurry. Look up at the towers; the work outlived him by generations. Keep your voice low.","normal":"Picture this spot in 1882: builders laying the first stones of a modest neo-Gothic church. Just a year later, the project passed to a young architect named Antoni Gaudí, and everything changed, because he threw out the plan. For the next 43 years this building was his life. Near the end he was living in his workshop. People asked why it was taking so long. His answer: my client is not in a hurry. Look up at the towers: the work went on for generations after him. This is a real neighbourhood, so keep your voice low.","long":"Picture this spot in 1882. The streets of the Eixample, the city's grid-planned extension, were still filling in, and builders were laying the first stones of a modest neo-Gothic church. Its first architect was Francisco de Paula del Villar. The idea was an expiatory temple, a church paid for entirely by donations. Just a year later, the project passed to a young architect named Antoni Gaudí, and everything changed, because he threw out the plan. For the next 43 years this building was his life. In his last years he turned down other work to focus on it, and near the end he was living in his workshop. People asked why it was taking so long. His answer: my client is not in a hurry. He was speaking of God. In 1926 Gaudí was struck by a tram while walking through the city, and he died a few days later. He lies buried in the crypt of the church he never saw finished. Now look up at the towers. The work went on for generations after him, through war and decades of slow fundraising, with new architects working from his models and drawings. In 2010 the church was consecrated as a basilica. So when you look up, you're seeing many lifetimes of work, not just one. This is a real neighbourhood, with families living in the apartments around you, so keep your voice low as you take it in.","new_facts":["The Eixample is the city's grid-planned extension and was still being built up in 1882.","The first architect was Francisco de Paula del Villar.","The church was conceived as an expiatory temple funded by donations.","In his last years Gaudí turned down other work to focus on the Sagrada Família.","By 'my client' Gaudí meant God.","In 1926 Gaudí was struck by a tram and died a few days later.","Gaudí is buried in the crypt of the Sagrada Família.","Construction continued through war and decades of fundraising, with later architects working from Gaudí's models and drawings.","The church was consecrated as a basilica in 2010."]}},"tip":"Walk around to the Passion façade side — it's calmer and the sculpture is stark and striking."},"recinte-modernista-de-sant-pau":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This fairytale town was a working hospital until 2009. Architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty helped people heal, so patients got gardens, light and colourful tiled pavilions. Look down at the paths and picture the tunnels beneath, moving patients and supplies so the gardens stayed peaceful. Please keep it quiet for neighbours.","normal":"This looks like a fairytale town, but it was a working hospital until 2009. Architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty helped people heal, so he gave patients gardens, light, and colourful tiled pavilions. Underneath, a network of tunnels moved patients and supplies, so the gardens stayed peaceful above.","long":"This looks like a fairytale town, but it was a working hospital right up until 2009. When patients moved out, they didn't go far: hospital care continues in a modern building just to the north of these old pavilions. The story begins with a gift. A Barcelona banker named Pau Gil left money in his will for a new hospital, and the architect chosen was Lluís Domènech i Montaner. He believed beauty helped people heal, so instead of one huge block he spread the hospital across separate pavilions, each surrounded by gardens and filled with light. Look at the rooftops and domes around you, covered in coloured tiles, and at the warm exposed brick, carved stone and mosaic details on the walls. Nothing here was meant to feel cold or frightening. Now notice the way the site sits. The whole complex is turned at an angle to the neat street grid of the Eixample, and Domènech i Montaner chose that orientation so the wards would catch better sun and air. Then picture what you can't see. Underneath the gardens, a network of tunnels linked the buildings, moving patients and supplies out of sight, so the paths above stayed peaceful places to rest and recover. Domènech i Montaner didn't live to see it all finished; his son Pere completed the work. Today the site is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. As you wander, remember that people live all around this neighbourhood, so enjoy it calmly and quietly.","new_facts":["Hospital care continues in a modern building on the north side of the site","The new hospital was funded by a bequest from Barcelona banker Pau Gil","The pavilions are decorated with exposed brick, carved stone and mosaics, and some have domes","The complex is set at an angle to the Eixample street grid, an orientation chosen for better sunlight and ventilation","Domènech i Montaner died before completion; his son Pere Domènech i Roura finished the work","The site is a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]},"architecture":{"short":"Look at the rooftops glowing with coloured tiles. This fairytale town was a hospital, and that was the point: Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty helped people heal. Beneath these gardens, tunnels moved patients and supplies so life above stayed peaceful. People live all around here, so enjoy it quietly.","normal":"Look at the rooftops around you, glowing with coloured tiles. It looks more like a fairytale town than a hospital, and that was the point. Architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty helped people heal, so he spread the hospital across separate pavilions, full of light and set among gardens. Now picture what you can't see. Beneath these gardens, a network of tunnels moved patients and supplies between buildings, so life above stayed peaceful. That calm still matters. People live all around here, so enjoy it quietly.","long":"Look at the rooftops around you, glowing with coloured tiles. It looks more like a fairytale town than a hospital, and that was exactly the point. Architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty helped people heal, and he was one of the great names of Catalan Modernisme, the same architect behind the Palau de la Música Catalana. Here he rejected the idea of one enormous building. Instead he spread the hospital across separate pavilions, so each ward had its own light, its own fresh air and gardens outside the windows. Let your eyes travel slowly over one pavilion. Notice the warm red brick, the carved stone, and the way ceramic tiles and mosaics turn domes and towers into patterns of colour. Look closely for flower and plant shapes worked into the decoration; very little here is plain. Then step back and notice how the whole site sits. It's set at an angle to the straight streets of the Eixample, a choice meant to give the wards better sunlight and ventilation. Now picture what you can't see. Beneath these gardens, a network of tunnels moved patients and supplies between buildings, so the paths above could stay calm, green and quiet. Together with the Palau de la Música, this complex is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. That calm still matters. People live all around here, so enjoy it quietly.","new_facts":["Domènech i Montaner was a leading architect of Catalan Modernisme","He also designed the Palau de la Música Catalana","The pavilions use red brick, carved stone, ceramic tiles and mosaics, with domes, towers and floral and plant motifs","The complex is set at an angle to the Eixample street grid to give the wards better sunlight and ventilation","The complex and the Palau de la Música Catalana together form a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]},"history":{"short":"Here's something surprising: this fairytale town was a working hospital until 2009. For generations, anxious patients arrived to find gardens, sunlight and colourful tiled pavilions, because Lluís Domènech i Montaner believed beauty could help people heal. Look along these paths and imagine the nurses and families who walked them. Please keep El Guinardó peaceful.","normal":"Here's something surprising: this fairytale town was a working hospital right up until 2009. For generations, sick and anxious patients arrived here and found gardens, sunlight and colourful tiled pavilions waiting for them. That was the vision of architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner, who believed beauty itself could help people heal. Underneath, tunnels carried patients and supplies, so the gardens stayed a place of rest. As you look along these paths, imagine the nurses, doctors and families who once walked them. El Guinardó is a living neighbourhood, so please keep it peaceful.","long":"Here's something surprising: this fairytale town was a working hospital right up until 2009. Its roots go back much further. The hospital grew out of the Hospital de la Santa Creu, founded in 1401 in the old city, in what's now the Raval. By the late nineteenth century that old building was too small for a growing Barcelona, and a banker named Pau Gil left money in his will for a new hospital. That's why its full name honours both: the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau. Work here began in 1902, to the vision of architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner, who believed beauty itself could help people heal. He didn't live to see it finished; his son Pere completed it. For generations, sick and anxious patients arrived and found gardens, sunlight and colourful tiled pavilions waiting for them. Underneath, tunnels carried patients and supplies between buildings, so the gardens above stayed a place of rest. As you look along these paths, imagine the nurses, doctors and families who once walked them, some hurrying, some simply sitting in the sun while someone they loved recovered. In 1997 the complex was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and in 2009 hospital care moved into a modern building on the north side of the grounds, where it continues today. So this is still a place connected to illness and healing. El Guinardó is a living neighbourhood, so please keep it peaceful.","new_facts":["The hospital grew out of the Hospital de la Santa Creu, founded in 1401 in what is now the Raval","By the late 19th century the old hospital had become too small for the growing city","Banker Pau Gil left a bequest for a new hospital, and 'Sant Pau' in the full name honours him","The full name is the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau","Construction began in 1902","Domènech i Montaner died before completion; his son Pere Domènech i Roura finished it","The complex was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997","In 2009 hospital care moved to a modern building on the north side of the grounds, where it continues"]}},"tip":"Sagrada Família is a straight 10-minute walk down Avinguda de Gaudí."},"casa-mila":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Locals mocked this building when it opened in 1912, calling it La Pedrera, the stone quarry. Look along the façade: there isn't a single straight wall. Then raise your eyes to the roof, where chimneys stand like helmeted warriors. Some say they inspired the look of Darth Vader.","normal":"Locals mocked this building when it opened in 1912, calling it La Pedrera — the stone quarry. There isn't a single straight wall on the façade. The wrought-iron balconies look like seaweed, and up on the roof stand chimneys shaped like helmeted warriors. Some say they inspired the look of Darth Vader.","long":"Take a moment here on the pavement and look up at this rippling wall of pale stone. When it opened in 1912, locals mocked it. They called it La Pedrera, the stone quarry, and the nickname stuck so well that most people still use it today. Its official name is Casa Milà, after the couple who commissioned it, Pere Milà and his wife Roser Segimon. The architect was Antoni Gaudí, and this was the last private home he designed before giving himself over to the Sagrada Família. Now try to find a straight wall on the façade. Follow the curves around the corner, along each floor, up to the top. You won't find one. The whole front swells and dips like a cliff worn down by the sea. Look next at the balconies. Their wrought iron twists and tangles like seaweed caught on rocks. Then let your eyes travel up to the roofline. From down here you may glimpse the tops of the chimneys and ventilation towers. Up close they look like helmeted warriors standing guard, and some say they inspired the look of Darth Vader. Whether or not that's true, they're among the strangest guardians any rooftop has ever had. Since 1984, it has been on UNESCO's World Heritage List as part of the works of Antoni Gaudí. But it's not only a monument. People still live in some of these apartments, so enjoy the view quietly and leave room on the pavement for neighbours passing by.","new_facts":["The official name is Casa Milà, after the couple who commissioned it, Pere Milà and Roser Segimon","It was the last private residence Gaudí designed before devoting himself to the Sagrada Família","The rooftop has ventilation towers as well as chimneys","It has been on UNESCO's World Heritage List since 1984 as part of the Works of Antoni Gaudí"]},"architecture":{"short":"Try to find a straight wall on this façade. You won't. Gaudí's stone front ripples like a weathered cliff, and the trick is hidden: inner columns carry the weight, so the walls could curve freely. Locals mocked it as La Pedrera, the stone quarry. People live here, so admire quietly.","normal":"Run your eyes along this façade and try to find a straight wall. You won't. Antoni Gaudí's stone front ripples like a weathered cliff, which is why locals mocked it as La Pedrera, the stone quarry, when it opened in 1912. The cleverest part is hidden. The stone doesn't hold the building up. Columns inside carry the weight, so the walls were free to curve. Now look up to the roofline, where chimneys rise like helmeted warriors. People still live here, so admire it quietly from the pavement and leave room for neighbours to pass.","long":"Stand back far enough to take in the whole corner, and run your eyes along this façade. Try to find a straight wall. You won't. Antoni Gaudí's stone front ripples like a weathered cliff, floor after floor, which is why locals mocked it as La Pedrera, the stone quarry, when it opened in 1912. Gaudí built it between 1906 and 1912 for Pere Milà and Roser Segimon, which is where its official name, Casa Milà, comes from. The cleverest part is the part you can't see. That heavy-looking stone doesn't hold the building up. Columns inside carry the weight, along with a frame of iron beams, so the façade is really more like a curtain hung on a skeleton. That freedom let Gaudí curve the walls however he liked. It also meant the floors inside didn't need fixed load-bearing walls, so the apartments could be laid out freely. Look at the balconies, too. Their iron twists like seaweed, softening the stone. Inside, out of sight from here, the building wraps around large open courtyards that bring light and air into the flats. Now look up to the roofline, where chimneys and ventilation towers rise like helmeted warriors guarding the top. Near the top of the façade you may spot carved lettering, a Latin prayer to the Virgin Mary. People still live here, so admire it quietly from the pavement and leave room for neighbours to pass.","new_facts":["It was built between 1906 and 1912","It was commissioned by Pere Milà and Roser Segimon, hence the official name Casa Milà","The structure uses iron beams along with the columns","Without load-bearing walls inside, the apartments could be laid out freely","The building is arranged around large open courtyards that bring light and air inside","The rooftop has ventilation towers as well as chimneys","Carved lettering near the top of the façade is a Latin prayer to the Virgin Mary"]},"art":{"short":"Look at the tangled iron balconies. They look like seaweed washed up on a rock. Gaudí's collaborator Josep Maria Jujol is credited with much of this wild, sculptural ironwork. Up on the roof, chimneys stand like helmeted warriors. Enjoy them quietly; this is a lived-in neighbourhood.","normal":"Notice the tangled iron balconies. They look like seaweed washed up on a rock. Gaudí worked here with his collaborator Josep Maria Jujol, who is credited with much of this wild, almost sculptural ironwork. Together they turned an apartment building into something closer to a giant artwork. When it opened in 1912, locals laughed and nicknamed it La Pedrera, the stone quarry. Up on the roof, chimneys stand like helmeted warriors, and some say they inspired the look of Darth Vader. Enjoy them quietly, because this is a lived-in neighbourhood.","long":"Find a spot on the pavement where you can see a few balconies at once, and notice the tangled iron. It looks like seaweed washed up on a rock, or kelp drying in the sun. Gaudí worked here with his collaborator Josep Maria Jujol, who is credited with much of this wild, almost sculptural ironwork. Jujol was an architect in his own right. He also worked with Gaudí on other projects, including the famous serpentine bench in Park Güell, with its broken-tile mosaic. Look closely and you'll see the iron isn't just bent into neat curves. It's twisted, knotted and frayed, as if it grew rather than was made. Now step back and take in the whole front. The pale stone swells and folds like a cliff, and the iron seems to cling to it the way seaweed clings to rock at low tide. Together Gaudí and Jujol turned an apartment building into something closer to a giant artwork. When it opened in 1912, though, not everyone was charmed. Locals laughed and nicknamed it La Pedrera, the stone quarry. Today the old insult has become an affectionate name. Up on the roof, chimneys stand like helmeted warriors, and some say they inspired the look of Darth Vader. Some of them are covered in broken tile and glass, the same mosaic technique you'll find elsewhere in Gaudí's city. The building is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, but it's also home to residents. Enjoy it quietly, because this is a lived-in neighbourhood.","new_facts":["Josep Maria Jujol was an architect in his own right","Jujol worked with Gaudí on the serpentine bench mosaic in Park Güell","Some of the rooftop chimneys are covered in broken tile and glass mosaic","Casa Milà is a UNESCO World Heritage Site"]}},"tip":"Even from the street, check the iron balconies up close — each one is different."},"casa-batllo":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Look up at the roof: it rises like the scaly back of a dragon, and the tower is the sword of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, plunged right into it. Below, the balconies resemble skulls, which is why some call this the House of Bones. Gaudí rebuilt an ordinary house into this legend.","normal":"Look at the roof: it rises like the scaly back of a dragon, and the tower is the sword of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, plunged into it. The balconies resemble skulls and the columns look like bones, which is why some call this the House of Bones. Gaudí rebuilt an ordinary house into a legend.","long":"You're standing on Passeig de Gràcia, in front of one of Antoni Gaudí's most famous works. Start at the very top. Look at the roof: it rises like the scaly back of a dragon, its glazed ceramic tiles shifting colour as the light changes. Now find the tower, crowned with a cross. That tower is the sword of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, plunged into the dragon's back. Sant Jordi is said to have slain a dragon, and Catalans still celebrate him every year on the twenty-third of April, when the city fills with books and roses. Let your eyes come down the facade. The balconies resemble skulls, with their empty eye sockets, and on the lower floors the columns between the windows look like bones. That's why some people call this the House of Bones. Between them, the wall sparkles with fragments of coloured glass and ceramic, a mosaic technique Gaudí loved, called trencadís. Here's the surprising part: this wasn't built from scratch. Gaudí took an ordinary house and rebuilt it for the textile industrialist Josep Batlló, between 1904 and 1906, turning it into a legend in stone and glass. Now look along the street to the neighbouring buildings. This stretch is nicknamed the Block of Discord, because several of Barcelona's great modernista architects built here side by side, each in a strikingly different style. Next door is Casa Amatller, by Josep Puig i Cadafalch. Before you move on, please keep to the side of the pavement. This is a busy street where people live, work and shop every day.","new_facts":["Casa Batlló stands on Passeig de Gràcia","The roof is covered in glazed ceramic tiles","The tower is crowned with a cross","Sant Jordi's Day is celebrated every 23 April with books and roses","The facade is decorated with fragments of coloured glass and ceramic","Gaudí used the broken-tile mosaic technique called trencadís","Gaudí rebuilt the house for the textile industrialist Josep Batlló between 1904 and 1906","This stretch of street is nicknamed the Block of Discord, where several modernista architects built side by side in different styles","Casa Amatller, next door, is by Josep Puig i Cadafalch"]},"architecture":{"short":"Notice how this facade seems to ripple, as if the house were breathing. Gaudí didn't start from scratch: he rebuilt an ordinary building and reshaped its front into this living skin. Look at the lower windows, where the columns are shaped like bones. Please keep to the side; people live here.","normal":"Notice that this facade seems to ripple, as if the whole house were breathing. Here's the daring part: Gaudí didn't start from scratch. He took an ordinary building and rebuilt it, reshaping its front into this living skin. Look at the lower windows, where the columns are shaped like bones, and the balconies above, curved like skulls. Then let your eyes climb to the roof, arched like a dragon's scaly back, pierced by a tower that stands for Sant Jordi's sword. Please keep to the side of the pavement; this is a busy street where people live and work.","long":"Notice that this facade seems to ripple, as if the whole house were breathing. There's hardly a straight line on it. Here's the daring part: Gaudí didn't start from scratch. Between 1904 and 1906, working for the textile industrialist Josep Batlló, he took an ordinary building from the 1870s and rebuilt it, reshaping its front into this living skin. Start low. Look at the lower windows, the big openings of the main floor where the Batlló family lived. The columns between them are shaped like bones, slender and swelling at the joints, and the stone around them flows as if it had been moulded rather than carved. Above, the iron balconies curve like skulls or masks. Now look at the wall between them. It's dotted with fragments of coloured glass and round ceramic discs, mostly in blues and greens, so the surface shimmers as you shift your position on the pavement. Try taking a step or two and watch it change. Then let your eyes climb to the roof, arched like a dragon's scaly back and covered in glazed ceramic tiles. It's pierced by a tower crowned with a cross, which stands for Sant Jordi's sword. Compare all this with the straighter, stepped gable of Casa Amatller next door; the two sit side by side on a stretch of Passeig de Gràcia nicknamed the Block of Discord, where rival architects built in strikingly different styles. Please keep to the side of the pavement while you look; this is a busy street where people live and work.","new_facts":["Gaudí rebuilt the house between 1904 and 1906","The client was the textile industrialist Josep Batlló","The original building dated from the 1870s","The Batlló family lived on the main floor with the large lower windows","The balconies are made of iron","The facade is dotted with coloured glass fragments and round ceramic discs, mostly in blues and greens","The roof is covered in glazed ceramic tiles","The tower is crowned with a cross","Casa Amatller next door has a stepped gable","The building is on Passeig de Gràcia, in a stretch nicknamed the Block of Discord where architects built in contrasting styles"]},"art":{"short":"Imagine a painting you can walk past. The shimmering front is dotted with coloured glass and ceramic that catch the light like scales underwater. Look up at the roof: it's the scaly back of the dragon slain by Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, and the tower is his sword. Enjoy it quietly.","normal":"Imagine a painting you can walk past: that's what Gaudí made of this house. The shimmering front is dotted with fragments of coloured glass and ceramic that catch the light like scales underwater. But the real artwork is a story. Catalonia's patron saint, Sant Jordi, is said to have slain a dragon, and here that tale is told in a building. Look up at the roof, the dragon's scaly back, and the tower, his sword driven in. Some see the skull-like balconies and bone-shaped columns as the dragon's victims. Enjoy it quietly; neighbours live all along this street.","long":"Imagine a painting you can walk past: that's what Gaudí made of this house. Stand still for a moment and let the facade work on you. The shimmering front is dotted with fragments of coloured glass and ceramic that catch the light like scales underwater. Gaudí loved working with broken pieces like these, a mosaic technique called trencadís, and here they're scattered over a background of blues and greens, so the wall seems to change as clouds pass or as you move along the pavement. But the real artwork is a story. Catalonia's patron saint, Sant Jordi, is said to have slain a dragon to save a princess, and here that tale is told in a building. Look up at the roof, the dragon's scaly back, its glazed tiles shading from one colour to another along the ridge. The tower, crowned with a cross, is his sword driven in. Some see the skull-like balconies and bone-shaped columns below as the dragon's victims. The legend still matters in this city. Every twenty-third of April, on Sant Jordi's Day, Barcelona fills with stalls of books and roses, and couples and friends exchange them as gifts. Gaudí created this between 1904 and 1906 for the industrialist Josep Batlló, transforming an existing house rather than building a new one. Notice how the story reads from top to bottom, like a picture you're meant to take in slowly. Enjoy it quietly; neighbours live all along this street.","new_facts":["Gaudí used the broken-piece mosaic technique called trencadís","The facade background is in blues and greens","In the legend, Sant Jordi slays the dragon to save a princess","The roof's glazed tiles shade from one colour to another along the ridge","The tower is crowned with a cross","Sant Jordi's Day is 23 April, when Barcelona fills with stalls of books and roses exchanged as gifts","Gaudí transformed the existing house between 1904 and 1906 for the industrialist Josep Batlló"]}},"tip":"Next door is Casa Amatller, by a rival architect — this block is nicknamed the Block of Discord."},"mercat-de-la-llibertat":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Gràcia was its own town until 1897, and it still behaves like one. This iron-and-glass market from 1888 is where the neighbourhood really shops. Watch how stallholders greet customers by name. If you buy something, try a paper cone of olives, and start with a friendly bon dia.","normal":"Gràcia was its own town until 1897, and it still behaves like one. This iron-and-glass market from 1888 is where the neighbourhood actually shops. Notice how stallholders know their customers by name. If you buy something, try some local cheese or a paper cone of olives — and say bon dia.","long":"Gràcia was its own town until 1897, and it still behaves like one. You can feel it in these narrow streets and little squares, and you can feel it most of all right here. This iron-and-glass market dates from 1888, and it's where the neighbourhood actually does its shopping. Take a moment to look up first. The iron frame and the glass let daylight fall across the stalls, so the whole hall feels open and bright, more like a covered square than a building. Then bring your eyes back down to the counters. Notice how the stallholders know their customers by name. You might catch a quick greeting, a question about family, a word about what's good today. These conversations happen while things are being weighed and wrapped, and nobody seems in a hurry. The market shares its name with the square it stands on, Plaça de la Llibertat, and llibertat is simply the Catalan word for freedom. You'll hear plenty of Catalan around you, which is the everyday language for many people here. Remember that this is a working market, not a museum, so keep to the side of the aisles and leave room for people with shopping bags and trolleys. If you do want to buy something, it's a lovely way to take part. Try some local cheese, or a paper cone of olives to nibble as you carry on walking. And when you reach the counter, start the way locals do, with a warm bon dia. It means good morning, and it opens doors.","new_facts":["The market stands on Plaça de la Llibertat and shares its name","\"Llibertat\" is the Catalan word for freedom","Catalan is the everyday language for many people in Gràcia","\"Bon dia\" means good morning in Catalan"]},"food":{"short":"Breathe in: cured cheese, briny olives, fresh fruit, all under an iron-and-glass roof that has sheltered this market since eighteen eighty-eight. This is where Gràcia buys its dinner. Notice what's piled highest on the stalls. Fancy a taste? Point, smile, say bon dia, and ask for a paper cone of olives.","normal":"Breathe in for a moment: cured cheese, briny olives, fresh fruit, all under one roof of iron and glass that has sheltered this market since eighteen eighty-eight. This is not a showpiece for visitors. It's where Gràcia buys its dinner. Stroll past the stalls and notice what's piled highest, and what's being wrapped up for regulars. If you'd like a taste, ask for a little local cheese, or a paper cone of olives to nibble as you walk on. Point, smile, and start with bon dia. Good morning goes a long way here.","long":"Breathe in for a moment. Cured cheese, briny olives, fresh fruit, all mingling under one roof of iron and glass that has sheltered this market since eighteen eighty-eight. Look up and you'll see how the ironwork holds the glass above you, letting in the light that makes the fruit stalls glow. But this is not a showpiece for visitors. It's where Gràcia buys its dinner, and the rhythm here follows the neighbourhood's kitchens, not the tour buses. Stroll slowly past the stalls and let your eyes do the work. Notice what's piled highest, because that usually tells you what's in season and what people are cooking right now. Watch what's being wrapped up for regulars, and how carefully. At the cheese counters, look for the big wheels and wedges waiting to be cut. At the olive stalls, you'll often see tubs of different colours and sizes, some plain, some dressed with herbs or garlic. If you'd like a taste, this is the perfect place for a small one. Ask for a little local cheese, or a paper cone of olives to nibble as you walk on. You don't need perfect Spanish or Catalan. Point, smile, and start with bon dia, which is Catalan for good morning. It goes a long way here. Keep in mind that people around you are doing their real shopping, so let regulars take their turn, step aside if someone needs to reach a counter, and ask before taking a close-up of anyone's stall. Then carry your little treat back out into Gràcia's streets.","new_facts":["\"Bon dia\" is Catalan for good morning"]},"local":{"short":"Here's a surprise: until eighteen ninety-seven, Gràcia wasn't part of Barcelona at all. It was its own town, and it still feels like one. Under this iron-and-glass roof, listen for stallholders greeting customers by name. Join in with a warm bon dia, and keep photos discreet. These are neighbours shopping.","normal":"Here's a surprise: until eighteen ninety-seven, Gràcia wasn't part of Barcelona at all. It was its own town, and in many ways it still behaves like one. Pause for a moment under this iron-and-glass roof, built in eighteen eighty-eight, and listen. You may hear stallholders greeting customers by name, chatting as they weigh and wrap. For the people who live on these streets, this is everyday life, not a sight. So join in the simplest local ritual: a warm bon dia when you reach a stall. And if you take photos, be discreet. These are neighbours doing their shopping.","long":"Here's a surprise: until eighteen ninety-seven, Gràcia wasn't part of Barcelona at all. It was its own town, and in many ways it still behaves like one. Its streets are full of small squares where people stop to talk, and this market is one of the places where that village feeling is strongest. Pause for a moment under this iron-and-glass roof, built in eighteen eighty-eight. Look up at the ironwork and the light coming through, then bring your attention back down to the stalls, and simply listen. You may hear stallholders greeting customers by name, asking after someone's family, chatting as they weigh and wrap. Much of what you hear will be in Catalan, the everyday language for many people in this neighbourhood. Even the market's name is Catalan. It shares it with the square it stands on, Plaça de la Llibertat, and llibertat means freedom. For the people who live on these streets, this is everyday life, not a sight. So the best way to experience it is to fit in rather than stand out. Join in the simplest local ritual: a warm bon dia when you reach a stall. It means good morning, and it's how a conversation starts here. Keep the aisles clear, let regulars go first if they're in a hurry, and if you take photos, be discreet. Ask before photographing a stallholder, and avoid pointing your camera at shoppers. These are neighbours doing their shopping, and that ordinary rhythm is exactly what makes this place special.","new_facts":["Gràcia's streets have many small squares","Catalan is the everyday language for many people in Gràcia","The market stands on Plaça de la Llibertat and shares its name","\"Llibertat\" means freedom in Catalan","\"Bon dia\" means good morning in Catalan"]}},"tip":"Mornings are busiest with locals; keep the aisles clear and buy something if you linger."},"placa-del-sol":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This square has been Gràcia's meeting point for generations. Here's the surprise: during the Spanish Civil War, a bomb shelter was dug right beneath your feet. Look around at the sunny terraces where people now gather after work. The balconies above are homes, so enjoy your evening quietly.","normal":"This square has been a meeting point for Gràcia's neighbours for generations. During the Spanish Civil War, a bomb shelter was dug beneath it. Today it's where people gather after work for a drink in the sun. Gràcia's squares are its living rooms — and it's people's home, so evenings here are best enjoyed quietly.","long":"Welcome to Plaça del Sol, the Square of the Sun. For generations, this has been one of the places where Gràcia's neighbours come together. It helps to know that Gràcia was once a separate town, and it only became part of Barcelona in 1897. You can still feel that village spirit in the way people use squares like this one. Here, squares are the neighbourhood's living rooms, places to meet friends, chat and watch the evening go by. Take a moment to look at the open sky above you. That sunshine is what gives the square its character, and its name. After work, people drift in to sit on the terraces with a drink and let the day wind down. Now here's something you'd never guess from all that brightness. During the Spanish Civil War, Barcelona was bombed from the air, and a shelter was dug beneath this square. Imagine neighbours hurrying underground to take cover, right below where you're standing. Barcelona's residents built many shelters like this across the city to protect themselves. Look down at the ground for a moment and let that sink in, then look back up at the life around you. Finally, look at the balconies that frame the square on every side. Behind them are real homes, with families who have to get up in the morning. So enjoy the square the way locals do, relaxed and unhurried, and keep your voice low as the evening goes on. That's how this living room stays welcoming for everyone.","new_facts":["Plaça del Sol means 'Square of the Sun'.","Gràcia was a separate town until it became part of Barcelona in 1897.","Barcelona's residents built many air-raid shelters across the city during the Civil War."]},"local":{"short":"Think of this square as a living room without a roof. Look up at the open sky: that sunshine draws Gràcia's neighbours here after work for a drink on the terraces. The balconies all around are real homes, so join in the way locals do, relaxed and unhurried.","normal":"Think of this square as a living room without a roof. In Gràcia, squares are where neighbours actually spend their evenings, and Plaça del Sol has been a meeting point for local people for generations. Notice the open sky above you. That sunshine is exactly what draws people here. After work, neighbours drift in to share a drink on the terraces, catch up with friends and let the day wind down. Now look up at the balconies all around you. Those are real homes, so join in the way locals do: relaxed, unhurried and easygoing.","long":"Think of this square as a living room without a roof. In Gràcia, that's not just a figure of speech. Squares are where neighbours actually spend their evenings, and Plaça del Sol has been a meeting point for local people for generations. Gràcia was its own town until it joined Barcelona in 1897, and it still keeps a strong neighbourhood feel. Start by noticing the open sky above you. In a district of narrow streets, a space like this, open to the sun, is precious, and that sunshine is exactly what draws people here. After work, neighbours drift in to share a drink on the terraces, catch up with friends and let the day wind down. Watch for a moment and you'll feel the rhythm of it: people greeting each other, conversations picked up from yesterday. If you happen to visit in August, you may catch the Festa Major de Gràcia, when residents decorate their streets and the whole neighbourhood celebrates together. Now look up at the balconies all around you. Notice the plants, the shutters, the small signs of daily life. Those are real homes, and the people inside live with the sound of this square every night. So join in the way locals do: relaxed, unhurried and easygoing. Find a seat, order something, enjoy the light, and keep the volume gentle, especially later in the evening. Treat it like you've been invited into someone's living room, because in a way, you have.","new_facts":["Gràcia was a separate town until it joined Barcelona in 1897.","Gràcia is a district of narrow streets.","The Festa Major de Gràcia takes place in August, when residents decorate their streets."]},"history":{"short":"Here's something you'd never guess from the sunny terraces. During the Spanish Civil War, when Barcelona was bombed from the air, a shelter was dug right beneath your feet. Look down, then look around: the same square where neighbours once took cover is where Gràcia gathers today.","normal":"Here's something you'd never guess from the sunny terraces. During the Spanish Civil War, when Barcelona was bombed from the air, a shelter was dug right beneath your feet. Picture neighbours taking cover below the very ground you're standing on. Now look around at the square today, the same place where Gràcia gathers for a drink in the sun. It has been the neighbourhood's meeting point for generations, through the darkest years and the brightest. And that history still belongs to the families living in the homes all around you.","long":"Here's something you'd never guess from the sunny terraces. During the Spanish Civil War, which lasted from 1936 to 1939, Barcelona was bombed from the air, and much of that bombing came from Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side. To protect themselves, people across the city dug shelters, and one of them was dug right beneath your feet. Pause here and picture it. The sirens sounding, neighbours leaving their homes and hurrying down below the very ground you're standing on, waiting in the dark for the danger to pass. Now look down at the ground, and then slowly look up and around you. This is the same place where Gràcia now gathers for a drink in the sun. It has been the neighbourhood's meeting point for generations, through the darkest years and the brightest. That continuity runs deep here. Gràcia was its own town until it became part of Barcelona in 1897, and its squares have long been the heart of local life. The people who sheltered underground came back up to the same streets, the same balconies, the same square, and carried on living here. Take a look at those balconies now, framing the square on every side. Some of the families behind them may carry stories of those years, passed down from parents and grandparents. That history still belongs to the families living in the homes all around you. So as you enjoy the sunshine, enjoy it gently, with respect for the people whose square this has always been.","new_facts":["The Spanish Civil War lasted from 1936 to 1939.","Much of the aerial bombing of Barcelona was carried out by Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side.","People across Barcelona dug air-raid shelters to protect themselves.","Air-raid sirens warned residents of bombing raids.","Gràcia was a separate town until it became part of Barcelona in 1897."]}},"tip":"Residents live right above the terraces: keep voices down late at night."},"palau-de-la-musica-catalana":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Built in 1908 for a working-class choir, this concert hall celebrates Catalan identity. Look up at the façade for the busts of Bach, Beethoven and Wagner. Inside, a huge stained-glass skylight glows like a sun, and by day this is the only concert hall in Europe lit entirely by natural light.","normal":"Built in 1908 for a working-class choir, this concert hall is a celebration of Catalan identity. The façade features busts of Bach, Beethoven and Wagner, and a sculpture of Catalan folk song. Inside, an enormous stained-glass skylight glows like a sun above the audience. It's the only concert hall in Europe lit entirely by natural light during the day.","long":"You're standing in front of the Palau de la Música Catalana, built in 1908 for a working-class choir, the Orfeó Català. Its members were ordinary working people who came together to sing, and this hall was built as their home. That's why the whole building is a celebration of Catalan identity, from its brick to its mosaics. Take a moment to look up at the façade. You'll find carved busts of great composers, among them Bach, Beethoven and Wagner, gazing down on the street. On the corner, a sculpture bursts out of the stone. It's a tribute to Catalan folk song, the music these singers loved, and if you look to the very top you'll spot Saint George, Catalonia's patron saint. Notice too the columns on the upper balcony, wrapped in colourful mosaic that seems almost to flower. Inside, the surprise is light. An enormous stained-glass skylight hangs above the audience and glows like a sun. During the day, this is the only concert hall in Europe lit entirely by natural light. Around the stage, sculpted muses emerge from the wall behind the musicians, half in mosaic and half in relief, as if the music itself had taken shape. In 1997 the Palau was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, but it's still very much a working concert hall, and these narrow streets are where people live. So please keep to the side, leave room for neighbours passing by, and enjoy the view quietly.","new_facts":["The choir it was built for is the Orfeó Català","Saint George, Catalonia's patron saint, appears at the top of the corner sculpture","The columns on the upper balcony are covered in colourful floral mosaic","Muses emerge from the wall behind the stage, half in mosaic and half in relief","The Palau was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1997","It is still a working concert hall"]},"architecture":{"short":"In 1908 this concert hall was a daring idea: almost a glass box. Lluís Domènech i Montaner built it around a steel frame, so the walls could open up to coloured glass. Look up at the façade, where brick, stone, mosaic and sculpture crowd together. And please step aside for neighbours passing by.","normal":"Here's a daring idea for 1908: a concert hall that's almost a glass box. The architect, Lluís Domènech i Montaner, built the Palau de la Música Catalana around a steel frame, so the walls didn't have to carry the building's weight and could open up to coloured glass instead. Look up at the façade and notice how brick, stone, mosaic and sculpture all crowd together, a proud showcase of Catalan Modernisme. Inside, a huge stained-glass skylight pours daylight over the audience. These are narrow, lived-in streets, so do step aside to let neighbours pass while you look.","long":"Here's a daring idea for 1908: a concert hall that's almost a glass box. The architect, Lluís Domènech i Montaner, built the Palau de la Música Catalana around a steel frame. Because that frame carries the weight, the walls didn't have to, and large stretches of them could open up to coloured glass instead. It's a modern engineering trick dressed in very traditional craft. Look up at the façade and notice how brick, stone, mosaic and sculpture all crowd together. The red brick is plain and everyday, but see how it's set against carved stone and glittering tiles, as if the building were showing off everything Catalan workshops could do. That's the spirit of Catalan Modernisme: nothing left plain where it could be beautiful. Now find the columns along the upper level, wrapped in mosaic flowers, each one patterned a little differently. Then let your eyes rise to the big mosaic high on the main façade, made by the artist Lluís Bru. Inside, out of sight from here, the steel frame really pays off. A huge stained-glass skylight hangs over the audience like an upside-down dome, pouring daylight onto the seats, so this is a concert hall where you can hear music by natural light. In 1997 the building was added to UNESCO's World Heritage list. As you take it all in, remember these are narrow, lived-in streets. Do step aside to let neighbours pass, and keep the pavement clear while you look.","new_facts":["The columns on the upper level of the façade are covered in floral mosaic, each with a different pattern.","The large mosaic on the main façade is by the artist Lluís Bru.","The stained-glass skylight is shaped like an inverted dome.","The Palau de la Música Catalana was added to UNESCO's World Heritage list in 1997."]},"art":{"short":"Imagine sitting beneath a sun made of glass: that's the stained-glass skylight inside, glowing gold and blue. Out here, look up at the corner and find Miquel Blay's big sculpture celebrating Catalan folk song, crowded with everyday people, young and old, swept up in music.","normal":"Imagine sitting beneath a sun made of glass. That's the great stained-glass skylight inside this hall, glowing in golds and blues above every concert. Out here, look up at the corner and find the big sculpture celebrating Catalan folk song, carved by Miquel Blay, full of everyday people, young and old, swept up in music. Then let your eyes travel along the façade to the busts of Bach, Beethoven and Wagner. Sculptors, mosaic makers and glass artists all worked side by side here, turning a choir's home into one great work of art.","long":"Imagine sitting beneath a sun made of glass. That's the great stained-glass skylight inside this hall, glowing in golds and blues above every concert, made in the workshop of the glass artist Antoni Rigalt. You can't see it from the street, so start with what you can. Look up at the corner of the building and find the big sculpture celebrating Catalan folk song, carved by Miquel Blay. Take your time with it. It's full of everyday people, young and old, swept up together in music. Above them rises Saint George, Catalonia's patron saint, as if guarding the song itself. Now let your eyes travel along the façade to the row of busts. Bach, Beethoven and Wagner are there, joined by the Renaissance composer Palestrina: great names of European music watching over a hall built for a Catalan choir. Between them, notice the columns wrapped in mosaic flowers, each one a little different, and the shimmer of broken tile catching the light. Inside, the sculpture carries on in a startling way. Around the back of the stage stand female figures, the spirits of music, their lower halves in flat mosaic and their upper halves bursting out as sculpture, the work of Eusebi Arnau. Sculptors, mosaic makers and glass artists all worked side by side here, turning a choir's home into one great work of art. Stand back a little from the doorway and give passers-by room as you look.","new_facts":["The skylight was made in the workshop of glass artist Antoni Rigalt.","Saint George, Catalonia's patron saint, appears at the top of Miquel Blay's corner sculpture.","A bust of the Renaissance composer Palestrina also appears on the façade.","The façade columns are covered in floral mosaic, each with a different pattern.","Behind the stage are female figures representing the spirits of music, part mosaic and part sculpture, with the sculpted upper halves by Eusebi Arnau."]},"culture":{"short":"In 1960, with ministers of Franco's government in the audience, people here stood and sang the Cant de la Senyera, a Catalan song removed from the programme. Look up at the corner sculpture honouring Catalan folk song. Families live in these streets, so keep your voice gentle.","normal":"Some of the most famous moments in this hall came from the audience, not the stage. In 1960, at a concert attended by ministers of Franco's government, people here stood and sang the Cant de la Senyera, a Catalan song that had been removed from the programme. It became a celebrated act of quiet defiance. That suits a building made for a choir, the Orfeó Català, so ordinary people could sing together. Look up at the corner sculpture honouring Catalan folk song. Concerts still fill this hall, and families live in the streets around you, so keep your voice gentle.","long":"Some of the most famous moments in this hall came from the audience, not the stage. In 1960, Franco's regime was still restricting public use of Catalan, and a concert here marked the hundredth anniversary of the birth of the poet Joan Maragall. Ministers of Franco's government were in the audience. One piece had been removed from the programme: the Cant de la Senyera, a Catalan song with words by Maragall himself. People here stood up and sang it anyway. It became a celebrated act of quiet defiance, remembered today as the Fets del Palau, the events of the Palau. Some of those involved were arrested afterwards, among them a young Jordi Pujol, who decades later became president of Catalonia's government. That moment suits a building made for singing. The Palau was built as the home of the Orfeó Català, a choir founded in 1891 by Lluís Millet and Amadeu Vives, so that ordinary people could sing together. Millet also wrote the music for the Cant de la Senyera, so the song the audience chose was, in a sense, coming home. Look up at the corner sculpture honouring Catalan folk song, with its crowd of everyday people caught up in music. It says plainly who this hall was meant for. Concerts still fill the Palau, and the Orfeó Català still sings here. Families live in the narrow streets around you, so as you stand and listen, keep your voice gentle.","new_facts":["In 1960 Franco's regime was restricting public use of the Catalan language.","The 1960 concert marked the centenary of the birth of the poet Joan Maragall.","The lyrics of the Cant de la Senyera were written by Joan Maragall.","The episode is known as the Fets del Palau.","Jordi Pujol was among those arrested afterwards and later became president of Catalonia's government.","The Orfeó Català was founded in 1891 by Lluís Millet and Amadeu Vives.","Lluís Millet composed the music of the Cant de la Senyera.","The Orfeó Català still performs at the Palau."]}},"tip":"Look for the mosaic columns on the corner — each has a different floral pattern."},"mercat-de-santa-caterina":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Look up at that wavy, colourful roof. It's made from over 300,000 ceramic tiles, in the colours of the fruit and vegetables sold below. This is the oldest covered market in the city, built where a medieval convent once stood. Take a moment to trace the waves from one end to the other.","normal":"That wavy, colourful roof is made from over 300,000 ceramic tiles, in the colours of fruit and vegetables sold below. The market is the oldest covered one in the city, built on the site of a medieval convent. When it was renovated in 2005, archaeologists found the convent's foundations — you can still see them inside.","long":"Stop here and look up. That wavy, colourful roof rolls over the market like a sea of tiles, and there are more than 300,000 of them, all ceramic. The colours aren't random. They're the colours of the fruit and vegetables sold underneath, so the roof is a kind of giant shop sign for what's inside. Follow the curves with your eyes and you'll see how the colours shift from one patch to the next, a bit like stalls laid out side by side. This is the oldest covered market in Barcelona, but the ground beneath it is far older. In the Middle Ages a Dominican convent stood on this spot, and the market took its name, Santa Caterina, from it. When the building was renovated in 2005, archaeologists dug down and found the convent's foundations. They weren't covered over again. Step inside and look for them. You can still see those old stones, a reminder that the market sits on top of centuries of the city's history. The renovation was the work of the architects Enric Miralles and Benedetta Tagliabue. Miralles died before it was finished, and Tagliabue saw the project through. Notice how they kept the old market walls at street level and set the new roof floating above them, the old and the new sharing one building. Before you go in, remember this is still a working market where people from Sant Pere do their daily shopping. Give them room at the stalls, keep your voice down, and enjoy it as they do.","new_facts":["The medieval convent on the site was Dominican.","The market takes its name, Santa Caterina, from the convent.","The 2005 renovation was designed by the architects Enric Miralles and Benedetta Tagliabue.","Miralles died before the renovation was finished, and Tagliabue completed the project.","The renovation kept the old market walls and set the new roof above them."]},"food":{"short":"Look up: that rippling roof is a giant still life, more than 300,000 ceramic tiles coloured like the fruit and vegetables below. Then step inside to see the real thing piled on the stalls. Locals shop here every day, so let them pass, and maybe grab some ripe fruit to go.","normal":"Look up first: that rippling roof is a giant still life. Its colours, spread across more than 300,000 ceramic tiles, were chosen to echo the fruit and vegetables sold underneath. Now step inside and see the real thing, piled high on the stalls. This is the oldest covered market in Barcelona, and it's still where people from Sant Pere do their everyday shopping, so let them pass and keep your voice low at the counters. Notice what's on display: whatever is in season is what the neighbourhood is eating this week. A piece of ripe fruit makes a perfect walking snack.","long":"Before you go in, look up. That rippling roof is a giant still life, laid out in more than 300,000 ceramic tiles. Its colours were chosen to echo the fruit and vegetables sold underneath. Think of the reds of tomatoes and peppers, the greens of leaves, the warm oranges and yellows of citrus and squash. It's as if the stalls had been painted onto the sky. Now step inside and see the real thing, piled high on the counters. This is the oldest covered market in Barcelona, and it carries the name of Santa Caterina, a medieval convent that once stood on this very spot. But it's no museum. People from Sant Pere still come here for their everyday shopping, bags on their arms and lists in their heads, so let them pass, wait your turn, and keep your voice low at the counters. Take a slow lap and notice what's on display. Whatever is in season is what the neighbourhood is eating this week, so the colours on the stalls change through the year, even if the roof stays the same. Look at how the stallholders build their displays, the care in a stack of fruit or a tray of fresh greens. Listen to the quick exchanges in Catalan and Spanish between sellers and their regulars. If you'd like to join in, buy a single piece of ripe fruit. It makes a perfect walking snack, and it's a small way of taking part in the market's daily life rather than just watching it.","new_facts":["The market is named after Santa Caterina, the medieval convent that stood on the site.","Catalan and Spanish are both spoken at the market."]},"architecture":{"short":"Look up at that roof, rolling like a wave above the market and covered in more than 300,000 ceramic tiles. Its colours echo the fruit and vegetables sold below, so the building wears its contents on the outside. Enric Miralles and Benedetta Tagliabue designed it for the 2005 renovation. Mind the shoppers as you look.","normal":"Look up at that roof. It rolls like a wave above the market hall, covered in more than 300,000 ceramic tiles. The colours aren't random. They echo the fruit and vegetables sold below, so the building wears its contents on the outside. The roof came with the 2005 renovation of the city's oldest covered market, designed by the architects Enric Miralles and Benedetta Tagliabue. During the work, archaeologists uncovered the foundations of a medieval convent that once stood here, and inside you can still see them. This is a working neighbourhood market, so step around the shoppers as you look.","long":"Look up at that roof. It rolls like a wave above the market hall, rising and dipping as if the whole building were breathing, and it's covered in more than 300,000 ceramic tiles. Look closely and you'll see they're small hexagons, fitted together like a mosaic. The colours aren't random. They echo the fruit and vegetables sold below, so the building wears its contents on the outside. The roof came with the 2005 renovation of the city's oldest covered market, designed by the architects Enric Miralles and Benedetta Tagliabue. Miralles died in 2000, before the work was finished, and Tagliabue carried the project through to completion. Now bring your eyes down to street level. Instead of tearing down the old market, the architects kept its nineteenth-century walls and let the new roof float above them, so you're looking at two eras in a single building. And there's a third, older layer. During the work, archaeologists uncovered the foundations of a medieval convent that once stood here, a Dominican house that gave the market its name, Santa Caterina. Rather than burying those remains again, the design left them on view, and inside you can still see them. As you walk around, notice how the waves of the roof spill past the edges of the old walls, so the outline looks different from every corner. It's worth circling part of the building to watch it change. This is a working neighbourhood market, though, and people from Sant Pere are here to shop, so step around them as you look.","new_facts":["The roof tiles are hexagonal.","Enric Miralles died in 2000, before the renovation was finished.","Benedetta Tagliabue completed the project after Miralles's death.","The renovation kept the market's nineteenth-century walls beneath the new roof.","The medieval convent was Dominican and gave the market its name, Santa Caterina."]}},"tip":"A good local alternative to the crowded Boqueria — same quality, far fewer queues."},"barcelona-cathedral":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This cathedral took over 600 years to finish. Step into the cloister and listen: you'll hear geese. There are always thirteen of them, one for each year of Saint Eulàlia's life. She was a young martyr from Roman times, Barcelona's original patron saint. Count them as they waddle past.","normal":"The cathedral took over 600 years to finish — the main façade was only completed in the late 1800s. Step into the cloister and you'll hear geese. There are always thirteen of them, one for each year of Saint Eulàlia's life. She was a young martyr from Roman times, and the city's original patron saint.","long":"Stand in the square in front of the cathedral and take in the whole front at once. This cathedral took more than six hundred years to finish. Work began in 1298, on the site of earlier churches, and the main façade you're looking at was only completed in the late 1800s. So those pointed arches and lacy pinnacles are younger than they seem, built to match the medieval church behind them. Its full name is the Cathedral of the Holy Cross and Saint Eulàlia, and Eulàlia is the key to this place. She was a young girl martyred in Roman times, and she was Barcelona's original patron saint. Her remains rest in the crypt beneath the main altar, so if you go inside, look for the steps leading down in front of the altar. Then find your way to the cloister, the quiet courtyard built around a garden, and listen. You'll hear geese before you see them. There are always thirteen, one for each year of Eulàlia's life. Watch them for a moment as they patrol the garden. They seem very sure this courtyard belongs to them. Notice too how the noise of the city drops away once you're inside these walls, with palm trees overhead and water trickling from the fountain. Remember this is still a working church, where locals come to pray, so keep your voice low and your phone discreet. When you step back outside, the lanes around you are home to families, so carry that same quiet with you.","new_facts":["Construction of the cathedral began in 1298.","The cathedral was built on the site of earlier churches.","The main façade was built in Gothic style to match the older medieval church.","The cathedral's full name is the Cathedral of the Holy Cross and Saint Eulàlia.","Saint Eulàlia's remains rest in the crypt beneath the main altar.","The cloister is built around a garden with palm trees and a fountain.","The cathedral is still a working church where people come to pray."]},"history":{"short":"Picture a building site that outlasted generations: this cathedral took over six hundred years to finish. Its heart belongs to Saint Eulàlia, a young Roman-era martyr and Barcelona's original patron saint. In the cloister, look for the thirteen geese, one for each year of her short life. Please keep voices soft for residents.","normal":"Stand facing the main doors and picture a building site that outlasted many generations. This cathedral took over six hundred years to finish, so the people who laid its first stones never saw it complete. Its heart belongs to Saint Eulàlia, a young girl martyred in Roman times and Barcelona's original patron saint. Her memory lives on in the cloister, where thirteen geese are kept, one for each year of her short life. As you walk on, remember that the Gothic Quarter is home to real families, so keep your voice gentle in these narrow streets.","long":"Stand facing the main doors and picture a building site that outlasted many generations. Work on this Gothic cathedral began in 1298, and it took more than six hundred years to finish, so the people who laid its first stones never saw it complete. Their great-grandchildren didn't either. Even before them, this spot was sacred ground. Earlier churches stood here, including a Romanesque cathedral that the Gothic one gradually replaced. Its heart belongs to Saint Eulàlia, a young girl martyred in Roman times and Barcelona's original patron saint. The building's full name honours her alongside the Holy Cross, and her remains lie in the crypt beneath the main altar, where people still come to pay their respects. Barcelona later took on a second patron, Our Lady of Mercy, but Eulàlia came first. Her memory lives on in the cloister, where thirteen geese are kept, one for each year of her short life. If you go in, follow the cackling and you'll find them in the garden, beneath the palm trees. Think about that: a girl who lived only thirteen years, still remembered by an entire city all these centuries later. As you leave, glance back at the doors one more time and imagine all the generations who passed through them. Then walk on gently, because the Gothic Quarter is home to real families, and these narrow streets carry every sound straight up to their windows.","new_facts":["Construction of the cathedral began in 1298.","Earlier churches stood on the site, including a Romanesque cathedral that the Gothic building replaced.","The cathedral's full name honours the Holy Cross and Saint Eulàlia.","Saint Eulàlia's remains lie in the crypt beneath the main altar.","Barcelona later took on a second patron, Our Lady of Mercy.","The cloister garden has palm trees."]},"architecture":{"short":"Look up at the main façade, all lacy pinnacles and pointed arches. It looks medieval, but it was only completed in the late 1800s, finished in Gothic style to match the older church. Compare it with the side walls. Inside, a calm cloister is home to thirteen geese.","normal":"Look up at the main façade, with its lacy pinnacles and pointed arches. It looks medieval, but here's the twist: it was only completed in the late 1800s, centuries after work on the cathedral began. Its builders chose to finish it in the Gothic style so it would match the older church behind. As you look, compare the front with the older side walls and see whether the stone looks different to you. Inside, the cathedral wraps around a cloister, a calm courtyard where thirteen geese live. Take your time, then step aside so neighbours passing through have room.","long":"Look up at the main façade, with its lacy pinnacles and pointed arches. It looks medieval, but here's the twist: it was only completed in the late 1800s, centuries after work on the cathedral began in 1298. Its builders chose to finish it in the Gothic style so it would match the older church behind, an approach usually called neo-Gothic. Now trace the lines upward. Pointed arches let medieval builders carry weight down into slender piers and buttresses, which is why Gothic churches could rise so high and open up so much space for windows. Above the main doors, notice how the stone is carved almost like lace, with spiky pinnacles crowding the skyline. Then shift your gaze to the older side walls and see whether the stone looks different to you, perhaps darker or more weathered, with plainer surfaces. That contrast is six hundred years of building written into the walls. Keep an eye out, too, for gargoyles jutting from the roofline. They're waterspouts, designed to throw rainwater clear of the walls. Inside, the cathedral wraps around a cloister, a calm courtyard with arcaded walkways around a garden where thirteen geese live. Notice how the arches of the cloister frame the light and the palm trees, a quieter, more human-scaled kind of Gothic than the soaring interior. Moving from the busy façade to this sheltered garden takes only a few steps, yet it feels like a different world. Take your time, then step aside so neighbours passing through have room.","new_facts":["Construction of the cathedral began in 1298.","The 19th-century Gothic-style façade is usually described as neo-Gothic.","Pointed arches, piers and buttresses let Gothic builders build higher and open up space for windows.","The cathedral has gargoyles that serve as waterspouts.","The cloister has arcaded walkways around a garden with palm trees."]}},"tip":"On Sunday mornings locals dance the sardana in the square in front."},"placa-del-rei":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This was the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona. Legend says that on these wide stone steps, in 1493, Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage. Look at those steps fanning into the square, and picture the crowd. Beneath your feet lie the remains of Roman Barcino.","normal":"This was the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona. Legend says that on these steps Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage in 1493. Beneath your feet, two thousand years ago, lay the Roman town of Barcino — streets, a laundry, even a wine factory, which you can still visit underground.","long":"This was the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona, the rulers who later also became Kings of Aragon, and the buildings around you were their royal palace. Turn toward the wide stone steps that fan out into the square. Legend says that on these steps, in 1493, Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage. It's a story to enjoy as a legend rather than a certainty, but it tells you how important this courtyard was. Behind the steps stands the great hall of the palace, the Saló del Tinell, built in the fourteenth century and famous inside for its huge stone arches. To one side, look for the slim bell tower of the Chapel of Santa Àgata, the royal chapel. And above the square rises a tall tower of open galleries stacked one on top of another. It's called the Mirador del Rei Martí, the lookout of King Martin. Now look down at the ground, because the oldest part of this story is beneath your feet. Two thousand years ago this was the Roman town of Barcino. Under the square lie its streets, a laundry and even a wine factory, and you can still walk among them underground. The way in is through the Barcelona History Museum, whose entrance is right here in the square. Take a last look around before you move on. People live and work in these streets, so let the stones do the talking and keep your voice low.","new_facts":["The Counts of Barcelona later also became Kings of Aragon.","The buildings around the square were their royal palace.","The palace's great hall is the Saló del Tinell, built in the fourteenth century, with huge stone arches inside.","The Chapel of Santa Àgata, the royal chapel, is on the square and has a slim bell tower.","The Mirador del Rei Martí is a tower of stacked open galleries named after King Martin.","The underground Roman remains are visited through the Barcelona History Museum, whose entrance is on the square."]},"history":{"short":"Find the wide stone steps fanning into the square. Legend says that here, in 1493, Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage. True or not, this was the heart of power, the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona. People live all around you, so enjoy it quietly.","normal":"Stand still for a moment and find the wide stone steps that fan out into the square. Legend says that here, in 1493, Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage. Whether or not it happened on these exact stones, this was the heart of power: the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona. And the story goes deeper still. Two thousand years ago, beneath your feet, lay the Roman town of Barcino, with its streets, a laundry and even a wine factory. Today people live and work all around you, so enjoy the echoes quietly.","long":"Stand still for a moment and find the wide stone steps that fan out into the square. Legend says that here, in 1493, Ferdinand and Isabella welcomed Christopher Columbus home from his first voyage. No one can say for sure that it happened on these exact stones, but the setting fits, because this was the heart of power: the courtyard of the Counts of Barcelona, whose royal palace closes the square around you. Behind the steps stands the palace's great hall, the Saló del Tinell, built in the fourteenth century. Inside, a series of huge stone arches holds up the whole roof. Beside it is the royal chapel of Santa Àgata, with its slender bell tower. Rising over the corner is the Mirador del Rei Martí, a lookout tower of stacked open galleries named after King Martin. Let your eyes travel from the steps to the tower, and you are looking at buildings made for rulers who wanted to be seen. And the story goes deeper still. Two thousand years ago, beneath your feet, lay the Roman town of Barcino, with its streets, a laundry and even a wine factory. Those remains survive underground, and you can visit them through the Barcelona History Museum, which opens onto this very square. So the ground here holds two cities, one built on top of the other. Today people live and work all around you, so enjoy the echoes quietly.","new_facts":["The royal palace of the Counts of Barcelona surrounds the square.","The palace's great hall is the Saló del Tinell, built in the fourteenth century, with huge stone arches holding up the roof.","The royal chapel of Santa Àgata, with a slender bell tower, stands beside it.","The Mirador del Rei Martí is a lookout tower of stacked open galleries named after King Martin.","The underground Roman remains can be visited through the Barcelona History Museum, which opens onto the square."]}},"tip":"The City History Museum (MUHBA) entrance is right here if you want to see Roman Barcino."},"santa-maria-del-mar":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This is the cathedral of the people, built in just 54 years by the neighbourhood's dock workers and fishermen. Porters called bastaixos carried every stone on their backs from the quarry at Montjuïc. Look at the main doors and find their small carved figures, still at work.","normal":"This is the cathedral of the people. It was built in just 54 years by the dock workers and fishermen of the neighbourhood. The bastaixos, the porters, carried every stone on their backs from the quarry at Montjuïc. Look for small carved figures of them on the main doors. Inside, the columns are so slender the space feels weightless.","long":"You're standing in front of what people here call the cathedral of the people. It isn't actually Barcelona's cathedral, which is a few streets away in the Gothic Quarter, but this church was raised by the neighbourhood itself. Work began in 1329, and by 1383 it was finished. That's just 54 years, astonishingly fast for a Gothic church of this size. The dock workers and fishermen of La Ribera, the old waterfront district, gave their labour. And the bastaixos, the porters of the harbour, carried every stone on their backs from the quarry at Montjuïc, the hill above the port. Picture them making that walk again and again, block after block. Now look at the main doors. Find the small carved figures of those porters, still at work. Look up, too, at the great round rose window above the entrance. When you step inside, the mood changes completely. The columns are so slender, and the side aisles rise so nearly to the height of the central nave, that the space feels weightless, almost empty. Part of that bareness has a sad history. In 1936, at the start of the Spanish Civil War, a fire burned inside for days and destroyed most of the decoration. What survived is the pure stone structure, and many people think it's more beautiful for it. Before you go in, remember that this is still El Born's parish church, with weddings, masses and neighbours who come to pray. Keep your voice low and your camera discreet.","new_facts":["Construction began in 1329 and was finished in 1383","Santa Maria del Mar is not Barcelona's official cathedral, which is in the Gothic Quarter","The surrounding area is historically known as La Ribera, the old waterfront district","Montjuïc is the hill above the port","There is a large rose window above the main entrance","The side aisles rise nearly to the height of the central nave","In 1936, at the start of the Spanish Civil War, a fire burned inside for days and destroyed most of the decoration","It is still a working parish church with weddings and masses"]},"history":{"short":"Picture carrying a block of stone on your back from Montjuïc hill, again and again. The bastaixos, Barcelona's port porters, did exactly that to build this church. Look at the main doors and find their small figures, bent under their loads. Pause quietly here, because people still live and pray in El Born.","normal":"Imagine carrying a block of stone on your back, all the way from the quarry on Montjuïc hill, then doing it again, and again. That's what the bastaixos, the porters of Barcelona's port, did to build this church. Look at the main doors and you'll spot small figures of them, bent under their loads. Together with the fishermen and dock workers of this neighbourhood, they saw the whole basilica raised in just 54 years. In 1936, a fire burned inside for days, yet these walls still stand. Take a quiet moment here. People still live and pray in El Born.","long":"Imagine carrying a block of stone on your back, all the way from the quarry on Montjuïc hill, then turning round and doing it again, and again. That's what the bastaixos, the porters of Barcelona's port, did to build this church. Before you go any closer, look at the main doors. You'll spot small figures of them there, bent under their loads, a tribute to the men whose backs carried these walls into place. This was a church built by and for its neighbourhood. It rose in La Ribera, the old quarter by the sea, and it was the fishermen, dock workers and porters who lived in these streets who saw it through. The first stone was laid in 1329, and the whole basilica was raised in just 54 years, which is remarkably fast for a building of this size in the Middle Ages. Its story didn't end there. In 1936, at the start of the Spanish Civil War, a fire burned inside for days. It destroyed much of the decoration, yet these walls still stand, and the bare stone you see inside today is partly a result of that loss. If you walk round to the side of the church, you'll find the Fossar de les Moreres, a former cemetery where defenders who died in the siege of Barcelona in 1714 were buried. Today it's a memorial. Take a quiet moment before moving on. People still live and pray in El Born, and this is their parish as much as it is a landmark.","new_facts":["The first stone was laid in 1329.","The area was historically known as La Ribera, the neighbourhood by the sea.","The 1936 fire happened at the start of the Spanish Civil War.","The 1936 fire destroyed much of the church's interior decoration.","The Fossar de les Moreres beside the church is a former cemetery where defenders who died in the 1714 siege of Barcelona were buried, now a memorial."]},"architecture":{"short":"Step back and look at the façade, a tall, almost bare wall of stone with a great rose window at its heart. That restraint is classic Catalan Gothic. Notice the two octagonal bell towers with flat tops, not spires. Enjoy it calmly, because this is a working parish.","normal":"Step back and look at the façade. It's almost bare, a tall, plain wall of stone with a great rose window at its heart. That restraint is classic Catalan Gothic. Now notice the two octagonal bell towers, with flat tops rather than spires. The real surprise waits inside, where the columns are so slender and spaced so widely that the whole space feels weightless. Because the church went up in just 54 years, its style is remarkably unified from first stone to last. Enjoy it calmly. This is a working parish in a lived-in neighbourhood.","long":"Step back, as far as the little square allows, and look at the façade. It's almost bare, a tall, plain wall of stone with a great rose window at its heart. That restraint is classic Catalan Gothic. Instead of a forest of carved saints and pinnacles, you get broad, flat surfaces and strong horizontal lines. The rose window you see isn't quite the original. The first one was damaged in an earthquake in 1428 and replaced later in the fifteenth century, in the more ornate style of that time. Now let your eye rise to the two octagonal bell towers. Notice their flat tops. There are no spires here, which gives the whole front a calm, solid, almost fortress-like feel. The real surprise waits inside. The columns are slender and octagonal, and they're spaced so widely that the space seems weightless. The side aisles rise almost as high as the central nave, so the interior reads as one vast, open hall rather than a series of separate corridors. Much of the decoration was lost in a fire in 1936, and that loss lets the architecture speak for itself. Because the church went up in just 54 years, between 1329 and 1383, its style is remarkably unified from first stone to last. You're looking at one coherent idea, not centuries of additions. If you go in, move slowly and quietly. This is a working parish in a lived-in neighbourhood, and the people around you may be here to pray.","new_facts":["The original rose window was damaged in an earthquake in 1428 and replaced later in the fifteenth century in a more ornate style.","The interior columns are octagonal.","The side aisles are almost as high as the central nave, creating a hall-like interior.","Much of the interior decoration was lost in a fire in 1936.","Construction ran from 1329 to 1383."]},"culture":{"short":"If this church feels familiar, you may know it from Ildefonso Falcones's novel Cathedral of the Sea, about a young man who becomes a porter carrying stone for these walls. Look at the main doors for small figures of those porters, bent under their loads. Neighbours live here, so keep your voice low.","normal":"If this church feels familiar, you may have met it in a book. Ildefonso Falcones's best-selling novel, Cathedral of the Sea, follows a young man in medieval Barcelona who becomes one of the bastaixos, the porters who carried stone for these very walls. Look at the main doors for small figures of those porters, bent under their loads. The novel was later adapted for television, sending new readers and viewers here to see the real thing. Read a chapter nearby if you like, but remember that neighbours live on these streets, so keep your voice low.","long":"If this church feels familiar, you may have met it in a book. Ildefonso Falcones's best-selling novel, Cathedral of the Sea, first published in Spanish in 2006 as La catedral del mar, follows a young man in medieval Barcelona who becomes one of the bastaixos, the porters who carried stone for these very walls. In the story, the slow rise of the church runs alongside his own life, so the building becomes almost a character in its own right. Before you go further, look at the main doors. You'll find small figures of those porters, bent under their loads. They're easy to miss, but once you've read about the bastaixos, it's hard to look at them without thinking of the real men who hauled this stone, block by block, from the quarry on Montjuïc. The novel was later adapted for television, in a series first broadcast in 2018, and between the book and the screen it has sent new readers and viewers here to see the real thing. Many arrive expecting something like a film set and find instead a living building, still used for services and quiet prayer. Take your time looking around the outside, and if you have the book with you, read a chapter on a bench nearby. Just remember that this isn't a backdrop. Neighbours live on these streets and the church is their parish, so keep your voice low and let El Born carry on with its day.","new_facts":["The novel was first published in Spanish in 2006 under the title La catedral del mar.","The television adaptation was first broadcast in 2018.","The bastaixos carried the stone from the quarry on Montjuïc."]}},"tip":"Entry to the basilica is usually free at set times — check the door."},"museu-picasso":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Picasso arrived in Barcelona aged just 13, already painting like an old master. Look at the stone fronts along Carrer Montcada: five medieval merchants' palaces now hold the museum. Inside, you can watch that teenage prodigy learn every rule, then break them one by one.","normal":"Picasso arrived in Barcelona aged 13 and grew up as an artist on these streets. The museum fills five medieval palaces on Carrer Montcada, the street where wealthy merchants once lived. Inside, you'll see how a teenage prodigy who painted like an old master slowly broke every rule he'd learned.","long":"Picasso arrived in Barcelona in 1895, aged 13, and it was on these streets that he grew up as an artist. He'd been born in Málaga, and his father, an art teacher, took a post at the city's fine arts school, La Llotja, where the young Pablo enrolled too. Now look along Carrer Montcada. This narrow lane was where Barcelona's wealthy merchants once lived, and the heavy stone fronts and big doorways you see were built to show off that wealth. Five of these medieval palaces have been joined together to house the Museu Picasso, which opened in 1963. If you step inside, notice the courtyards behind the doors, with open stone staircases climbing to the main floor, a typical layout for the grand houses of this street. The collection is especially strong on Picasso's early years. You can follow him almost room by room, from careful academic studies to Science and Charity, a large, serious painting he made at fifteen, showing a doctor and a nun at a sick woman's bedside. It looks like the work of a seasoned professional. Then the rules start to slip. Another part of the collection holds his variations on Velázquez's Las Meninas, which Picasso himself gave to the museum. In them he takes one famous painting apart again and again. It's the clearest picture you'll get of a prodigy who learned to paint like an old master, then spent his life breaking everything he'd been taught. While you wait, keep your voice down: people live in the flats above these lanes.","new_facts":["Picasso arrived in Barcelona in 1895","Picasso was born in Málaga","His father was an art teacher who took a post at La Llotja, Barcelona's fine arts school, where Picasso also enrolled","The Museu Picasso opened in 1963","The Montcada palaces typically have courtyards with open stone staircases leading to the main floor","The museum holds Science and Charity, painted when Picasso was fifteen, showing a doctor and a nun at a sick woman's bedside","The museum holds Picasso's variations on Velázquez's Las Meninas, which Picasso donated himself"]},"art":{"short":"Imagine being thirteen and already painting like a seasoned master: that was Picasso when he reached Barcelona. Behind these stone palace fronts on Carrer Montcada, you can follow him learning every rule, then breaking them one by one. Please keep your voice low, as people live above these lanes.","normal":"Imagine being thirteen and already painting like a seasoned master. That was Picasso when he arrived in Barcelona. Look along Carrer Montcada at these stone palace fronts: behind them, five medieval mansions hold the museum's collection. Inside, you can follow his early years almost step by step, from careful academic portraits to bolder, stranger experiments. It's a rare chance to watch an artist learn every rule, then break them one by one. As you wait, keep your voice low, because people live above these narrow lanes.","long":"Imagine being thirteen and already painting like a seasoned master. That was Picasso when he arrived in Barcelona from Málaga, where he'd been born. His father was an art teacher, and the boy was soon studying at the city's fine arts school, La Llotja. Now look along Carrer Montcada at these stone palace fronts. Behind them, five medieval mansions have been joined together to hold the museum's collection, and its great strength is exactly those early years. Inside, you can follow him almost step by step. First come the careful academic drawings and portraits, the exercises of a student mastering anatomy, light and shadow. Look out for Science and Charity, a big, solemn canvas he painted at fifteen: a doctor takes a sick woman's pulse while a nun holds a child. It earned an honourable mention at a national exhibition in Madrid. Then the work gets bolder and stranger. You'll find pieces from his Blue Period, when he painted the poor and lonely in cold, sad blues. And there's a whole series of variations on Velázquez's Las Meninas, which Picasso made late in life and gave to the museum himself. Watch how he takes one famous painting apart again and again, changing colours, flattening space, playing with the figures. It's a rare chance to see an artist learn every rule, then break them one by one, on purpose. As you wait, keep your voice low, because people live above these narrow lanes.","new_facts":["Picasso was born in Málaga","His father was an art teacher, and Picasso studied at La Llotja, Barcelona's fine arts school","The museum holds Science and Charity, painted when Picasso was fifteen, showing a doctor taking a sick woman's pulse and a nun holding a child","Science and Charity received an honourable mention at a national exhibition in Madrid","The museum holds works from Picasso's Blue Period","The museum holds Picasso's late-life variations on Velázquez's Las Meninas, which he donated himself"]},"history":{"short":"Picture this narrow street centuries ago, lined with the homes of Barcelona's wealthy merchants. Look up at the heavy stone walls and grand doorways: they were status symbols. Five of these medieval palaces now form the Museu Picasso. El Born is still a living neighbourhood, so step aside for residents.","normal":"Picture this narrow street centuries ago, lined with the homes of Barcelona's wealthy merchants. Look up at the heavy stone walls and grand doorways along Carrer Montcada: these were status symbols, built close to the trade that made the city rich. Five of these medieval palaces now form the Museu Picasso, joined into one museum. Much later, a thirteen-year-old boy named Pablo arrived in Barcelona and grew up as an artist in these old streets. El Born is still a living neighbourhood, so step aside for residents as you look around.","long":"Picture this narrow street centuries ago, lined with the homes of Barcelona's wealthy merchants. In the Middle Ages the city grew rich on trade across the Mediterranean, and this neighbourhood sat close to the waterfront where goods came in. Look up at the heavy stone walls and grand doorways along Carrer Montcada. These were status symbols, built to be seen by everyone passing through. Notice how solid the walls are at street level. In palaces like these, the ground floor and courtyard handled the practical side of life, while the family lived on the main floor above, reached by an open stone staircase rising from the courtyard. If a courtyard gate is open, you may catch that layout for yourself. Five of these medieval palaces now form the Museu Picasso, joined into one museum. It opened in 1963 in the Palau Aguilar, and grew from a gift of works by Jaume Sabartés, Picasso's lifelong friend and secretary. Over the years it expanded into the neighbouring houses. Centuries later, a thirteen-year-old boy named Pablo arrived in Barcelona and grew up as an artist in these old streets, so it's fitting that his early work hangs here. If you follow Carrer Montcada to its end, you'll be right beside Santa Maria del Mar, the great Gothic church of this seafaring quarter. El Born is still a living neighbourhood, with homes and small shops tucked behind these walls, so step aside for residents as you look around.","new_facts":["Medieval Barcelona grew rich on Mediterranean trade, and El Born lay close to the waterfront","In these palaces the family lived on the main upper floor, reached by an open stone staircase from the courtyard, with the ground floor and courtyard used for practical purposes","The Museu Picasso opened in 1963 in the Palau Aguilar","The museum grew from a donation of works by Jaume Sabartés, Picasso's friend and secretary","The museum later expanded into neighbouring palaces","Carrer Montcada leads to the Gothic church of Santa Maria del Mar"]}},"tip":"Free entry on Thursday evenings and the first Sunday of the month (book ahead)."},"el-born-centre-de-cultura":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Under this old market roof lies a whole neighbourhood frozen in time. In 1714, after Barcelona fell, these streets were torn down for a fortress. In 2002, workers found the houses, wells and lanes just as they were left. Look down at those low stone walls: real homes. Every 11 September, Catalonia remembers it.","normal":"Under this old market roof lies an entire neighbourhood frozen in time. In 1714, after Barcelona fell in the War of the Spanish Succession, these streets were demolished to build a fortress. When workers dug here in 2002, they found the houses, wells and streets just as they were left. Every 11 September, Catalonia's national day remembers that defeat.","long":"Stand under this great old market roof for a moment and let your eyes drop to the ground. Beneath you lies an entire neighbourhood frozen in time. The roof belongs to the old Born Market, a cast-iron building that opened in 1876. But the story underneath is much older. In 1714, after a long siege, Barcelona fell in the War of the Spanish Succession. The city had backed the losing side, and the new king, Philip the Fifth, ordered these streets demolished to build a fortress, the Ciutadella, to keep watch over the city. Families lost their homes, and the district vanished under the new defences. The fortress itself was pulled down in the nineteenth century, and its grounds became the Parc de la Ciutadella, a short walk from here. For centuries, the ruins slept underground. Then, in 2002, workers digging here found the houses, wells and streets just as they had been left. Look for the outlines of rooms, the narrow lanes between them, and the openings of old wells. These were homes and doorways where ordinary people cooked, worked and talked with their neighbours. Barcelona fell on 11 September, and every year on that date, Catalonia's national day remembers that defeat. Standing here, you can see why the memory runs so deep. As you leave, remember that El Born is a living neighbourhood, so keep your voice low as you step back outside.","new_facts":["The roof is the old Born Market, a cast-iron building that opened in 1876","The siege of Barcelona was long","Barcelona had backed the losing side in the war","King Philip V ordered the demolition and the fortress","The fortress was called the Ciutadella","The Ciutadella was demolished in the nineteenth century and its grounds became the Parc de la Ciutadella, nearby","Barcelona fell on 11 September 1714"]},"history":{"short":"Look down at the ruins below. Those low stone walls are the floor plans of real homes, lived in until 1714, when Barcelona fell after a siege and the victors pulled this district down for a fortress. Workers uncovered it in 2002. El Born is a living neighbourhood again, so explore quietly.","normal":"Look down at the ruins below. Those low stone walls are the floor plans of real homes, the streets where Barcelona families lived until 1714. That year the city fell after a siege in the War of the Spanish Succession, and the victors ordered this district pulled down to make room for a fortress. For centuries the ruins slept beneath this market, until workers dug here in 2002 and found houses, wells and lanes just as they were left. Every 11 September, Catalonia's national day remembers that defeat. El Born is a living neighbourhood again, so explore it quietly.","long":"Look down at the ruins below you. Those low stone walls are the floor plans of real homes, laid out along the streets where Barcelona families lived until 1714. Try to pick out a single house: the line of its outer wall, the gaps where doors once opened, the narrow lane running past its front. That year, after a siege lasting more than a year, the city fell in the War of the Spanish Succession. Barcelona had supported Archduke Charles, the losing claimant to the Spanish throne, and the victors under Philip the Fifth ordered this district pulled down to make room for a fortress, the Ciutadella. For centuries the ruins slept beneath the old Born Market, the iron-roofed hall above your head. Then in 2002, workers preparing the site for a new library dug here and found houses, wells and lanes just as they were left. The discovery was so complete that the plans changed, and this cultural centre opened in 2013 so everyone could see what lay below. Every 11 September, Catalonia's national day remembers that defeat, the day the city fell in 1714. Standing here, you are looking at the reason that date still carries so much meaning. Notice how close the ruins sit to the streets outside; the old neighbourhood and the new one lie almost on top of each other. El Born is a living neighbourhood again, home to families, shops and workshops, so please explore it quietly.","new_facts":["The siege lasted more than a year","Barcelona supported Archduke Charles, the losing claimant to the Spanish throne","The victorious side was led by Philip V","The fortress was called the Ciutadella","The market above is the old Born Market, an iron-roofed hall","The 2002 excavation happened while preparing the site for a new library","After the discovery the plans changed and the cultural centre opened in 2013","The city fell on 11 September 1714"]}},"tip":"Entry to the central area overlooking the ruins is free."},"macba":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This bright white museum of contemporary art, designed by Richard Meier, sits in one of Barcelona's oldest, densest neighbourhoods. Nobody planned what happened outside: the square became one of Europe's most famous skate spots. Stand still for a moment and listen. Wheels on stone are the soundtrack here.","normal":"This bright white museum of contemporary art was designed by Richard Meier and dropped into one of the city's oldest, densest neighbourhoods. The square outside became something nobody planned: one of Europe's most famous skateboarding spots. Stand for a minute and listen — wheels on stone is the soundtrack here.","long":"You're standing in front of MACBA, Barcelona's Museum of Contemporary Art, and that bright white building is hard to miss. It was designed by the American architect Richard Meier and opened in 1995, dropped right into the Raval, one of the city's oldest and most tightly packed neighbourhoods. Look at the narrow streets around you, the balconies and the old façades, and then back at this clean white block. That contrast is a big part of the story here. Meier, who won the Pritzker Prize, architecture's best-known award, in 1984, is famous for working almost entirely in white, so the building changes with the light as the day goes on. The square you're in is the Plaça dels Àngels, and what happened here was never part of the plan. The smooth stone, the ledges and the gentle slopes turned out to be perfect for skateboarding, and over the years this became one of the most famous skate spots in Europe. Skaters from around the world know it from videos and photos, and some travel here just to ride it. So stand for a minute and simply listen. That clack, roll and scrape of wheels on stone is the soundtrack of this place. You might hear a trick land, or miss, again and again. Notice too the older building on the square. That's the former Convent dels Àngels, which gave the square its name. Remember that people live in the flats all around you, so enjoy the show quietly.","new_facts":["The museum is MACBA, Barcelona's Museum of Contemporary Art","The museum opened in 1995","Richard Meier won the Pritzker Prize in 1984","The square is called Plaça dels Àngels","The former Convent dels Àngels stands on the square and gave it its name"]},"art":{"short":"Look at that wall of white. Richard Meier, who designed this museum, builds almost entirely in white, so it shifts with Barcelona's light. Notice the long glass front: ramps climb behind it, turning visitors into part of the view. Outside, skaters made the square famous. Enjoy it quietly; people live here.","normal":"Look at that wall of white. Richard Meier, the American architect who designed this museum, is known for buildings that are almost entirely white, so they seem to shift with the Barcelona light through the day. Now notice the long glass front. Behind it, ramps climb the building, so visitors become part of the view as they move between floors. Inside is contemporary art. Outside, an unplanned creation took shape: the square became one of Europe's most famous skate spots, filmed and photographed by skaters worldwide. This is still a lived-in neighbourhood, so enjoy the show quietly.","long":"Start with that wall of white. Richard Meier, the American architect who designed this museum, is known for buildings that are almost entirely white, and he won the Pritzker Prize, architecture's best-known award, in 1984. You may know another of his famous projects, the Getty Center in Los Angeles. Here, the white isn't just a colour choice. Watch how it takes on the Barcelona light: crisp and bright at midday, warmer and softer late in the afternoon. The building opened in 1995, and it still looks startling next to the older façades around the square. Now let your eye move along the long glass front. Behind it, ramps climb the building, so instead of hiding visitors in stairwells, the architecture puts them on display. If the museum is open, you may spot small figures walking slowly upward. They're looking at art, and you're looking at them. Notice too the play between solid white panels and open glass. Inside is contemporary art. Outside, something nobody designed took shape. The smooth stone of the square, the ledges and the slopes drew skaters, and this became one of Europe's most famous skate spots, filmed and photographed by skaters worldwide. In a way, it's another kind of moving art, performed every day against Meier's white backdrop. Take a moment to watch, but remember this is still a lived-in neighbourhood, with homes all around, so enjoy the show quietly.","new_facts":["Richard Meier won the Pritzker Prize in 1984","Richard Meier designed the Getty Center in Los Angeles","The museum opened in 1995"]},"local":{"short":"Listen first: that clack and roll of wheels on stone is this square's everyday soundtrack. In the Raval, one of Barcelona's oldest, densest neighbourhoods, this space works like a shared living room. Look for skaters repeating one trick, kids on scooters, neighbours heading home with shopping. People live here, so keep your voice low.","normal":"Listen first: that clack and roll of wheels on stone is the everyday soundtrack of this square. This bright white museum sits in the Raval, one of Barcelona's oldest and densest neighbourhoods, and the open space in front has become a kind of shared living room. Look around and you'll likely see skaters practising the same trick over and over, kids weaving on scooters, and neighbours cutting across on their way home with shopping. People really live in the flats around you, so keep your voice low and let the square stay theirs.","long":"Listen first. Close your eyes for a second if you like. That clack, roll and slap of wheels on stone is the everyday soundtrack of this square. The bright white building in front of you is MACBA, Barcelona's Museum of Contemporary Art, and the square is the Plaça dels Àngels. It sits in the Raval, one of the oldest and most densely packed neighbourhoods in the city, where space to simply be outside is precious. Many streets here are narrow and close together, so this wide, open square has become a kind of shared living room. Now open your eyes and look around. You'll probably see skaters practising the same trick over and over, falling, getting up and trying again, with friends watching from the edges. Some come from across the world, because this is one of Europe's best-known skate spots. But just as often you'll see local kids weaving between them on scooters, parents chatting, and neighbours cutting across on their way home with shopping bags. The Raval is one of the city's most diverse neighbourhoods, and you might hear several languages in the same few minutes. Look up at the balconies around the square, too. Those are real homes, and the people inside hear this square from morning to night. Next door is the CCCB, another cultural centre, so the area mixes museum-goers, students and residents. Enjoy it the way locals do: find a spot at the side, watch for a while, keep your voice low and let the square stay theirs.","new_facts":["The museum is MACBA, Barcelona's Museum of Contemporary Art","The square is called Plaça dels Àngels","The Raval is one of Barcelona's most diverse neighbourhoods","The CCCB, another cultural centre, is next door"]}},"tip":"Respect the skaters' space, and wander the Raval's side streets for small independent galleries."},"mercat-de-sant-antoni":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This huge cross-shaped market reopened in 2018 after nine years of restoration, and the works uncovered part of the 17th-century city wall. Go down to the basement and you can see those old stones right beneath the shoppers' feet. On Sunday mornings, collectors still swap books, comics and stamps around the outside.","normal":"This huge cross-shaped market reopened in 2018 after nine years of restoration. During the works they uncovered part of the 17th-century city wall, now visible in the basement. On Sunday mornings the outer ring turns into a second-hand book, comic and stamp market, where collectors have been swapping since the 1930s.","long":"Stand back for a moment and take in the size of this place. The Mercat de Sant Antoni fills a whole block of the Eixample, the grid of streets laid out when Barcelona finally spread beyond its old walls. It first opened in 1882, designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, and its iron and glass frame is a fine example of the great covered markets of that time. Seen from above, the building forms a huge cross. After nine years of restoration, it reopened in 2018, and those works brought a surprise. As they dug, builders uncovered part of the 17th-century city wall, the very kind of barrier the Eixample was built to escape. Rather than bury it again, they left it on view in the basement, so you can walk down and see the old stonework lying beneath a working market. Before you go in, look up at the ironwork and the long sweep of the roofs. If you're here on a Sunday morning, the outer ring changes character completely. Stalls of second-hand books, comics and stamps line the outside, and collectors have been meeting here to swap and bargain since the 1930s. Watch the regulars flicking through boxes with practised hands, or leaning over albums of stamps, deep in conversation. Whatever day it is, remember this is still the neighbourhood's market. Keep to the side when it's busy, and let people get on with their shopping.","new_facts":["The market first opened in 1882.","It was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","It occupies a whole block of the Eixample.","The Eixample was laid out when Barcelona expanded beyond its old city walls."]},"food":{"short":"Look up at this enormous iron and glass market, shaped like a giant cross and reopened in 2018. Inside, see what locals are buying: fish laid out on ice, glossy fruit, cured meats and cheeses. That's tonight's dinner. Fancy a taste? Buy a small piece and enjoy it outside, so the aisles stay clear.","normal":"Look up at this enormous iron and glass market. Seen from above, it forms a giant cross. Inside is a working food market, reopened in 2018 after nine years of restoration, and it still feeds the neighbourhood every day. Wander the aisles and notice what people are actually buying: glossy fruit and vegetables, fish laid out on ice, cured meats and cheeses. This is tonight's dinner for the people who live around here. If something tempts you, buy a small piece, pay, and enjoy it outside, so the aisles stay clear for shoppers.","long":"Look up at this enormous iron and glass market. Seen from above, it forms a giant cross, and it fills an entire block of the Eixample grid. It first opened in 1882, designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, and after nine years of restoration it reopened in 2018. Through all of that, its main job hasn't changed. This is a working food market, and it still feeds the neighbourhood every day. Step inside and let your eyes adjust to the colour. Wander the aisles slowly and notice what people are actually buying. There's glossy fruit and vegetables stacked with real care, often following the seasons, so what you see depends on when you visit. At the fish stalls, the catch is laid out on ice, and you may see stallholders cleaning and filleting to order. Further along, look for legs of cured ham, sausages, and wheels and wedges of cheese. Listen too, for regulars chatting with stallholders as they wait. This is tonight's dinner for the people who live around here, not a show put on for visitors. If something tempts you, it's fine to join in. Buy a small piece, a few slices of ham or a wedge of cheese, pay, and take it outside to enjoy, so the aisles stay clear for shoppers. Keep your bag close in the narrow spaces, step aside when someone needs to reach a counter, and ask before photographing anyone at work.","new_facts":["The market first opened in 1882.","It was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","It occupies an entire block of the Eixample grid."]},"local":{"short":"Notice the shopping trolleys: neighbours still wheel them in daily and stop to chat with stallholders. At a busy stall, ask 'Qui és l'últim?', meaning 'Who's last?', and you'll know your turn. Downstairs, part of the 17th-century city wall is on view. Keep your voice low; people are shopping.","normal":"Notice the shopping trolleys. Neighbours still wheel them in for their daily shop and stop to chat with stallholders. This cross-shaped market reopened in 2018 after nine years of restoration, and for this neighbourhood it brought back an everyday meeting place. Here's a local ritual worth knowing. When you reach a busy stall, ask 'Qui és l'últim?', meaning 'Who's last?', and you'll know when it's your turn. Downstairs, part of the 17th-century city wall, uncovered during the works, is visible in the basement. Please keep your voice low. People here are doing their shopping.","long":"Notice the shopping trolleys. Neighbours still wheel them in for their daily shop, and many stop to chat with stallholders they know. That's the real life of this place. The market first opened in 1882, designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, and it fills a whole block of the Eixample. Seen from above, it forms a great cross. Its restoration took nine years, and when it reopened in 2018 this neighbourhood got back an everyday meeting place. Watch how people use it. Someone picks up vegetables, stops for a word at the cheese counter, compares fish at the next stall, and heads home. It's shopping, but it's also how neighbours keep in touch. Here's a local ritual worth knowing. Queues at busy stalls aren't always lines you can see. When you arrive, ask 'Qui és l'últim?', meaning 'Who's last?'. Someone will answer, and once that person has been served, it's your turn. It's Catalan, and people will appreciate the effort. Downstairs, there's something older. During the restoration, part of the 17th-century city wall was uncovered, and it's now visible in the basement. It's a reminder that this whole district grew up outside the walls once the city finally expanded. Take your time down there, but when you come back up, remember where you are. Please keep your voice low, keep to the edges of the aisles, and ask before taking photos of stallholders or shoppers. People here are doing their shopping, and you're a guest in their daily routine.","new_facts":["The market first opened in 1882.","It was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","It occupies a whole block of the Eixample.","The Eixample district grew up outside the old city walls when Barcelona expanded.","'Qui és l'últim?' is Catalan."]}},"tip":"Come on a Sunday morning for the book market, then join the locals for vermut nearby."},"carrer-de-blai":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This pedestrian street is lined with pintxo bars: small bites on bread, each held together with a toothpick. Take what you like from the bar, and at the end they count your toothpicks to make the bill. Look along the counters as you walk, and keep every stick.","normal":"This pedestrian street is lined with pintxo bars: small bites on bread, held together with a toothpick. Grab what you like from the bar, and at the end they count your toothpicks to make the bill. Poble-sec was a working-class neighbourhood built for the people of the port and the theatres of the nearby Paral·lel.","long":"You're standing on Carrer de Blai, a pedestrian street in Poble-sec, and it's lined from end to end with pintxo bars. A pintxo is a small bite on a slice of bread, held together with a toothpick. The word comes from the Spanish for 'to pierce', and that little stick is the whole idea. The tradition comes from the Basque Country, in the north of Spain, and here it suits an evening stroll perfectly. Look through the open doors at the counters, with rows of bites waiting, all within reach. Grab what you like straight from the bar, and when you've finished, the staff count your toothpicks to make the bill. So hold on to every one. Many people eat a bite or two in one bar, then move on to the next. Now take a moment to look past the food. Poble-sec was a working-class neighbourhood, built for the people who worked at the port and in the theatres of the nearby Paral·lel, a broad avenue once famous for its music halls and cabarets. The neighbourhood sits at the foot of Montjuïc, the hill overlooking the port. Look up above the bar signs at the balconies. Those are homes, and this is still a street where people live, so keep your voice gentle as you go. Even the neighbourhood's name is local. Poble-sec means 'dry village' in Catalan. Enjoy the bites, and enjoy the street.","new_facts":["The word pintxo comes from the Spanish for 'to pierce'.","The pintxo tradition comes from the Basque Country, in the north of Spain.","The Paral·lel was once famous for its music halls and cabarets.","Poble-sec sits at the foot of Montjuïc, the hill overlooking the port.","Poble-sec means 'dry village' in Catalan."]},"food":{"short":"Notice the toothpicks. Every little bite on these counters is pierced by one, and that stick is your receipt. Take what tempts you from the bar, and when you're done, the staff count the toothpicks on your plate to make the bill. Hold on to every stick.","normal":"Notice the toothpicks. Every little bite on the counters along this pedestrian street is pierced by one, and that tiny stick is your receipt. These are pintxos: small bites on bread, held together with a toothpick. Step inside, look along the bar, and simply take what tempts you. When you're done, the staff count the toothpicks on your plate to make the bill. It's a tradition from the Basque Country, and here it suits grazing perfectly: one bite, then another bar, then another. Hold on to every stick, and enjoy.","long":"Notice the toothpicks. Every little bite on the counters along this pedestrian street is pierced by one, and that tiny stick is your receipt. These are pintxos: small bites on bread, held together with a toothpick. The name comes from the Spanish word for 'to pierce', so the stick is right there in the word. Step inside any bar and look along the counter. You'll see the bites lined up in rows, ready to take, with no menu to puzzle over for what's in front of you. Simply take what tempts you, put it on your plate, and keep going. When you're done, the staff count the toothpicks on your plate to make the bill. It's a tradition from the Basque Country, in the north of Spain, where going from bar to bar for a bite and a drink is part of everyday life. Here it suits grazing perfectly: one bite, then another bar, then another. A small drink goes well alongside, perhaps a caña, a small glass of beer. Take a moment to look at how varied the toppings are, piled on little slices of bread, and choose with your eyes first. Remember, the bars along here are also the neighbourhood's local spots, and the balconies above are people's homes, so a friendly 'gràcies', the Catalan for thank you, as you leave goes a long way. Hold on to every stick, and enjoy.","new_facts":["The word pintxo comes from the Spanish for 'to pierce'.","The Basque Country is in the north of Spain, where going from bar to bar for a bite and a drink is part of everyday life.","A caña is a small glass of beer.","'Gràcies' is Catalan for thank you."]},"local":{"short":"Look up, above the bar signs, at the balconies. Behind them are homes, and for the people inside, this is simply their street, the route home. Do as they do: a 'bon dia' or 'bona tarda' when you walk into a bar, and a 'gràcies' as you leave.","normal":"Look up, above the bar signs, at the balconies. Behind them are homes, and the people inside are as much a part of this street as the food. Poble-sec grew as a working-class neighbourhood, built for those who worked at the port and in the theatres of the nearby Paral·lel. For residents, Carrer de Blai is simply their street, the route home after work or school. Do as they do: a simple 'bon dia' or 'bona tarda' when you walk into a bar, a 'gràcies' as you leave, and stroll like a guest in someone's neighbourhood.","long":"Look up, above the bar signs, at the balconies. Perhaps you'll spot plants, laundry, or shutters half open. Behind them are homes, and the people inside are as much a part of this street as the food. Poble-sec grew as a working-class neighbourhood, built for those who worked at the port and in the theatres of the nearby Paral·lel, the avenue once famous for its music halls and cabarets. The neighbourhood sits at the foot of Montjuïc, the hill overlooking the port, and its name, Poble-sec, means 'dry village' in Catalan. For residents, Carrer de Blai is simply their street, the route home after work or school. Many of the bars you're browsing are their local spots as well. So do as they do. Catalan is the local language here, and a few words go a long way. Say 'bon dia', good morning, or 'bona tarda', good afternoon, when you walk into a bar, and 'gràcies', thank you, as you leave. Keep your voice low, especially late in the evening, since homes sit just above the bars and doorways. Leave doorways clear, because those doors lead to people's stairwells. If you're with a group, give way on the street to people carrying shopping or walking children home. Stroll like a guest in someone's neighbourhood, and you'll be welcomed like one.","new_facts":["The Paral·lel was once famous for its music halls and cabarets.","Poble-sec sits at the foot of Montjuïc, the hill overlooking the port.","Poble-sec means 'dry village' in Catalan.","Catalan is the local language.","'Bon dia' means good morning, 'bona tarda' good afternoon, and 'gràcies' thank you, in Catalan."]}},"tip":"Weekday early evenings are more relaxed — and keep it quiet, people live upstairs."},"parc-de-la-ciutadella":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Picture a fortress here, built after 1714 and standing for 150 years as a symbol of occupation. When it came down, the people made it the city's first public park. Find the monumental fountain. A young student called Antoni Gaudí worked on it, long before he was famous.","normal":"Remember that fortress built after 1714? This is where it stood — for 150 years, a symbol of occupation. When it was finally torn down, the people turned it into the city's first public park. The monumental fountain was worked on by a young student called Antoni Gaudí. Today it's where Barcelona plays: jugglers, drummers and picnics.","long":"Remember that fortress built after 1714? You're standing where it stood. After the siege ended, King Philip the Fifth ordered a huge star-shaped citadel raised right here, and for 150 years it loomed over the city as a symbol of occupation. Picture soldiers on the ramparts where people now stroll beneath the trees. When it was finally torn down, the people turned this ground into something the city had never had: its first public park. It was designed by the architect Josep Fontserè, and in 1888 it hosted Barcelona's Universal Exhibition. That's why the red-brick Arc de Triomf stands nearby: it was built as the exhibition's entrance. Now head toward the monumental fountain, with its tumbling water and the golden chariot shining on top. Fontserè designed it, and one of the people who worked on it was a young student called Antoni Gaudí, years before the buildings that made him famous. Look closely at the rockwork and sculptures and picture him there, learning his trade. Some of the old fortress survives, too. The Parliament of Catalonia meets in its former arsenal, inside the park. So the place built to control the city now holds its parliament and its picnics. Today this is where Barcelona plays. Listen for drummers, watch the jugglers, and notice families spreading out on the grass. If you have time, the park's lake is a calm spot to rest your feet. Many of the people around you live just around the corner, so enjoy it the way they do, relaxed and unhurried.","new_facts":["The citadel was ordered by King Philip V","The citadel was star-shaped","The park was designed by architect Josep Fontserè","The park hosted Barcelona's Universal Exhibition in 1888","The Arc de Triomf was built as the entrance to the 1888 exhibition","Fontserè designed the monumental fountain","The fountain is topped by a golden chariot","The Parliament of Catalonia meets in the fortress's former arsenal inside the park","The park has a lake"]},"outdoors":{"short":"This green, open space was once a fortress, a symbol of occupation. Now it's the city's first public park, and it belongs to everyone. Wander to the monumental fountain and listen to the water. A young student called Antoni Gaudí worked on it. Locals unwind here too, so share it gently.","normal":"Here's something lovely to sit with: this green, open space was once a fortress, a symbol of occupation. Now it's the city's first public park, and it belongs to everyone. Look around at the lawns and the light falling through the trees. Then wander toward the monumental fountain and listen to the water; a young student called Antoni Gaudí worked on it. Find a quiet patch of grass and watch Barcelona at play: jugglers, drummers, picnics. Locals come here to unwind too, so share the space gently and leave it as green as you found it.","long":"Here's something lovely to sit with: this green, open space was once a fortress, a symbol of occupation. Today it's the city's first public park, and it belongs to everyone. Take a slow breath and look around. Notice the wide lawns, the shade of the big trees, and the way the light falls through the leaves and dapples the paths. Then wander toward the monumental fountain. Before you see it, you'll probably hear it: water tumbling over rocks into a broad basin, with a golden chariot gleaming on top. The park and the fountain were designed by the architect Josep Fontserè, and one of those who worked on the fountain was a young student called Antoni Gaudí. Stand at the edge for a minute and just listen. It's one of the most soothing sounds in the city. Nearby there's a small lake, where people rent rowing boats and drift slowly under the trees. If you like plants, look out for the park's two old botanical buildings, the glass Hivernacle and the slatted Umbracle, made to shelter plants from cold and from sun. When you're ready, find a quiet patch of grass and watch Barcelona at play. You might see jugglers practising, hear a circle of drummers, or catch the smell of someone's picnic. Locals come here to unwind after work and on weekends, many from the surrounding neighbourhoods. So share the space gently, keep the music low, and leave it as green as you found it.","new_facts":["The park and the fountain were designed by architect Josep Fontserè","The fountain is topped by a golden chariot","The park has a lake with rowing boats for hire","The park contains two historic botanical buildings, the glass Hivernacle and the slatted Umbracle"]},"history":{"short":"Imagine walls instead of trees. After 1714 a fortress rose here, and for about 150 years it loomed over Barcelona as a symbol of occupation. Once it was torn down, the people made it the city's first public park. Look for the monumental fountain. A young student named Antoni Gaudí worked on it.","normal":"Stand still for a moment and imagine walls instead of trees. After 1714, a fortress was built right here, and for about 150 years it loomed over Barcelona as a symbol of occupation. When it was finally torn down, the people did something remarkable: they turned the ground into the city's first public park. Look for the grand monumental fountain. Among those who worked on it was a young student named Antoni Gaudí, long before his famous buildings. So the place built to control the city became the place where it relaxes, in a neighbourhood people still call home.","long":"Stand still for a moment and imagine walls instead of trees. In 1714, Barcelona fell after a long siege at the end of the War of the Spanish Succession. Soon after, King Philip the Fifth had a fortress built right here. To make room, a large part of the neighbouring Ribera district was demolished and its residents forced out. For about 150 years the citadel loomed over Barcelona as a symbol of occupation. Picture soldiers on the ramparts where people now stroll beneath the trees. When it was finally torn down, in the second half of the nineteenth century, the people did something remarkable: they turned the ground into the city's first public park. The architect Josep Fontserè designed it, and in 1888 the park hosted Barcelona's Universal Exhibition. The red-brick Arc de Triomf nearby was built as its grand entrance. Now look for the monumental fountain. Fontserè designed it too, and among those who worked on it was a young student named Antoni Gaudí, long before his famous buildings. Not everything of the fortress vanished. Its former arsenal still stands inside the park, and today the Parliament of Catalonia meets there. So the place built to control the city became the place where it relaxes, and where its elected representatives gather, in a neighbourhood people still call home. As you walk on, remember that the families around you are part of that long story too.","new_facts":["Barcelona fell in 1714 after a long siege at the end of the War of the Spanish Succession","King Philip V had the fortress built","Part of the Ribera district was demolished to build the fortress and its residents were displaced","The fortress was torn down in the second half of the nineteenth century","The park was designed by architect Josep Fontserè","The park hosted Barcelona's Universal Exhibition in 1888","The Arc de Triomf was built as the entrance to the 1888 exhibition","Fontserè designed the monumental fountain","The fortress's former arsenal stands in the park and houses the Parliament of Catalonia"]}},"tip":"Rent a rowing boat on the small lake, or just find shade under the palms."},"barceloneta-beach":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Barceloneta was built in the 1750s for fishermen and for families who lost their homes when the Ribera was demolished. Look at its narrow streets, tiny flats and laundry on the balconies. The beach in front of you was largely created for the 1992 Olympics, when the city finally turned to face the sea.","normal":"Barceloneta was built in the 1750s for fishermen and families who lost their homes when the Ribera was demolished. Its narrow streets are lined with tiny flats, laundry on the balconies. The beach as you see it was largely created for the 1992 Olympics, when the city finally turned to face the sea.","long":"You're standing at the edge of one of Barcelona's most distinctive neighbourhoods, and its story starts with a loss. In the 1750s, Barceloneta was built for fishermen and for families who had lost their homes when the Ribera district was demolished. That demolition came after Barcelona fell in 1714, when the new king ordered a huge fortress, the Ciutadella, built on the site of their old streets. The people pushed out needed somewhere to live, and this triangle of land by the port became the answer. Turn around and look at the streets behind the beach. Notice how straight and narrow they are, laid out in long, regular rows. That orderly grid was planned by military engineers, which is very different from the tangled medieval lanes of the old city. The houses were first built low, and more floors were added later, which is why many buildings now stand taller over such slim streets. Inside are tiny flats, and you can see the life in them in the laundry drying on the balconies. These are homes, so enjoy them from a respectful distance. Now face the water. The beach as you see it was largely created for the 1992 Olympics. Before that, the waterfront was cut off by industry and railway lines, and the city mostly lived with its back to the sea. The Olympic project opened up this shoreline, and Barcelona finally turned to face the Mediterranean. Take a moment to look along the sand and imagine how recent all of it really is.","new_facts":["The Ribera was demolished after Barcelona fell in 1714, to build the Ciutadella fortress ordered by the new king.","Barceloneta sits on a roughly triangular piece of land by the port.","Its straight grid of long, narrow blocks was planned by military engineers.","The houses were originally built low and extra floors were added later.","Before the Olympic redevelopment, the waterfront was cut off by industry and railway lines."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Here's a surprise: the beach under your feet was largely created for the 1992 Olympics. Before then, the city mostly turned its back on the sea. Look out to the horizon and watch the light shift on the water, from silver to deep blue. Walking back, keep your voice low, because people live here.","normal":"Here's something surprising: the beach under your feet was largely created for the 1992 Olympics. Before then, the city mostly turned its back on the sea. Now look out at the horizon and notice how the light shifts on the water, from silver to deep blue. Find a spot on the sand, slip off your shoes, and just listen to the waves. Early morning and evening tend to be the quietest times to linger. When you head back, remember that the streets behind you are a real neighbourhood where people live, so keep your voice low as you walk through.","long":"Here's something surprising: the beach under your feet was largely created for the 1992 Olympics. Before then, the city mostly turned its back on the sea. The shore was cut off by industry and railway lines, and much of the sand you're standing on was brought in and shaped as part of that Olympic transformation. So this wide, open beach, which feels like it has always been here, is younger than many of the people lying on it. Now look out at the horizon and notice how the light shifts on the water, from silver to deep blue. Watch for a while and you'll see the colours move as clouds pass and the sun changes angle. Along the shore you may spot the low stone breakwaters reaching into the water, built to hold the sand in place so the sea doesn't carry it away. Find a spot on the sand, slip off your shoes, and just listen to the waves. Feel how the sand changes from dry and loose to cool and firm as you get closer to the water's edge. Early morning and evening tend to be the quietest times to linger, when the light is softer and the beach belongs mostly to swimmers, walkers and joggers from the neighbourhood. When you're ready to head back, remember that the streets behind you are a real neighbourhood where people live, in small flats close together. Sound carries easily in those narrow lanes, so keep your voice low as you walk through.","new_facts":["Before the Olympic redevelopment, the shore was cut off by industry and railway lines.","Much of the beach sand was brought in as part of the Olympic transformation.","Stone breakwaters along the shore help hold the sand in place."]},"local":{"short":"Look past the sand to the narrow streets behind you, where laundry hangs from tiny balconies. This neighbourhood was built in the 1750s for fishermen and families displaced from the Ribera, and those small flats are still homes. If you wander in, walk quietly and keep your camera out of windows and doorways.","normal":"Look past the sand to the narrow streets behind you, where laundry hangs from tiny balconies. This neighbourhood was built in the 1750s for fishermen and for families who had lost their homes when the Ribera district was demolished. Those small flats are still homes today, and for the people who live here the beach is part of everyday life, not just a holiday spot. If you wander in, walk quietly, step aside in the narrow lanes, and avoid pointing your camera into windows or doorways. You're a guest on someone's home street.","long":"Look past the sand to the narrow streets behind you, where laundry hangs from tiny balconies. This neighbourhood was built in the 1750s for fishermen and for families who had lost their homes when the Ribera district was demolished. Their old streets were cleared after Barcelona fell in 1714, to make room for the Ciutadella fortress, and this piece of land by the port became their new home. Notice how straight the streets are, running in long, even rows. Military engineers planned that grid, a sharp contrast to the winding lanes of the old town. The houses started out low, and extra floors were added over time, so families have been fitting their lives into small spaces here for generations. Those small flats are still homes today. Look up and you may notice the signs of it: plants on the railings, shirts and sheets drying in the sea air, shutters half closed against the sun. For the people who live here, the beach is part of everyday life, not just a holiday spot. It's where neighbours swim, walk the dog, and meet friends after work. If you wander in, walk quietly, and step aside in the narrow lanes so people carrying shopping or pushing prams can pass. Avoid pointing your camera into windows or doorways, and keep conversations soft, because sound bounces easily between these close walls. You're a guest on someone's home street, and a little care goes a long way here.","new_facts":["The Ribera was demolished after Barcelona fell in 1714, to make room for the Ciutadella fortress.","Barceloneta's straight grid of streets was planned by military engineers.","The houses were originally built low and extra floors were added over time."]}},"tip":"Wear a top when walking through the neighbourhood streets — it's a local request, and the law."},"park-guell":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This hillside was meant to be a luxury estate for sixty families, but only two houses ever sold, one to Gaudí himself. The project failed, and the city gained a park. Look at the serpentine bench: legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster so its curve would fit your back.","normal":"This was meant to be a luxury housing estate for sixty families. Only two houses were ever sold — one to Gaudí himself. The project failed, but the city gained a park. The famous serpentine bench was designed to fit the curve of a seated body; legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster to get the shape right.","long":"Welcome to Park Güell. Before it was a park, this hillside was meant to be something quite different: a luxury housing estate for sixty wealthy families, commissioned by the industrialist Eusebi Güell, Gaudí's great patron. Notice that the name uses the English spelling of park. Güell admired the garden cities of Britain, and he hoped to build one here, with elegant homes looking out over the city to the sea. It never took off. Only two houses were ever sold, and one of them went to Gaudí himself, who lived here for many years. His pink house with its slender spire, now the Gaudí House Museum, stands along the paths nearby. The project failed, but the city gained something better. Barcelona later bought the land and opened it as a public park, and today it's part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Now find the famous serpentine bench winding around the main terrace. It was designed to fit the curve of a seated body, so sit back and test it. Legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster so the builders could copy the shape just right. True or not, you can feel how it holds you. Run your hand over the colourful tiles, then look out past the rooftops toward the sea. And remember that the streets below are home to the people of Gràcia, so keep your voice gentle and enjoy the view.","new_facts":["The estate was commissioned by the industrialist Eusebi Güell, Gaudí's patron.","The name uses the English spelling 'park' because Güell admired British garden cities.","Gaudí lived in his house in the park for many years.","Gaudí's former home, a pink house with a spire, is now the Gaudí House Museum.","The city later bought the land and opened it as a public park.","Park Güell is part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]},"outdoors":{"short":"On a clear day, that silver line beyond the rooftops is the Mediterranean. This hillside was meant for sixty wealthy families, but only two houses sold, one to Gaudí. Sit on the curving bench, feel the sun-warmed tiles, and keep your voice soft for the Gràcia neighbours below.","normal":"Look out past the rooftops. On a clear day, that silver line on the horizon is the Mediterranean. This hillside was meant to be a private estate for sixty wealthy families, but only two houses were ever sold, one to Gaudí himself. The project failed, and Barcelona gained a park instead. Find a spot along the long, curving bench, feel the sun-warmed tiles, and let your eyes drift from the city to the sea. The streets below are home to the people of Gràcia, so keep your voice soft and enjoy the calm.","long":"Look out past the rooftops. On a clear day, that silver line on the horizon is the Mediterranean. Between you and the water lies most of Barcelona, and if you scan the skyline you may pick out the spires of the Sagrada Família, Gaudí's great unfinished basilica, rising above the grid of the Eixample. This hillside was meant to be a private estate for sixty wealthy families, a quiet garden suburb high above the noise of the city. Only two houses were ever sold, one to Gaudí himself. The project failed, and Barcelona gained a park instead, a place where anyone can come and sit with this view. Find a spot along the long, curving bench. Feel the sun-warmed tiles beneath your hands. Gaudí wanted the landscape to feel like part of the architecture, so notice how the paths, walls and viaducts of the park are built from rough stone dug from this very hillside, which helps them blend into the slope as if they had grown there. As you wander later, look for the leaning columns along the walkways, shaped a little like tree trunks, and listen for the birds in the trees. Then let your eyes drift slowly, from the green of the park, across the rooftops, all the way out to the sea. Take a few deep breaths. Up here the city feels far away. The streets below are home to the people of Gràcia, a neighbourhood with its own village spirit, so keep your voice soft and enjoy the calm.","new_facts":["The Sagrada Família, Gaudí's unfinished basilica, can be seen on the skyline from the park.","The Eixample district's street grid lies between the park and the sea.","The park's paths, walls and viaducts are built from stone taken from the hillside itself.","The walkways have leaning columns that resemble tree trunks."]},"architecture":{"short":"Notice how the bench snakes around the terrace. Legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster so its curve would fit a seated body. Beneath your feet, a forest of columns holds this terrace up, and the whole surface was designed to gather rainwater rather than let it run away.","normal":"Notice how the bench snakes around the edge of the terrace. Gaudí shaped it to fit the curve of a seated body, and legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster to get the shape right. Now think about what lies beneath your feet. This open terrace rests on a forest of columns below, and it was designed to gather rainwater instead of letting it run off. All of this was built as the groundwork for a luxury estate. Only two houses sold, but the engineering survived and became a park for everyone.","long":"Notice how the bench snakes around the edge of the terrace, bending in and out like a wave. Gaudí shaped it to fit the curve of a seated body, and legend says a worker sat naked in wet plaster to get the shape right. Those curves do more than look playful. The little bays let people sit facing each other in small groups, like a row of open-air sitting rooms. Now think about what lies beneath your feet. This open terrace rests on a forest of columns below, in a hall that was planned as the market for the estate's residents. When you head downstairs, look at the columns around the edge of that hall and you'll see they lean slightly, their style borrowed from ancient Greek temples. Up here, the ground was designed to gather rainwater instead of letting it run off. The water soaks through the surface, travels down channels hidden inside the columns, and collects in a large cistern underneath, where it could be stored to water the gardens. On a dry, rocky hillside, Gaudí turned a practical problem into something beautiful, so the very ground you're standing on is part of a water system. All of this was built as the groundwork for a luxury estate. Only two houses sold, but the engineering survived and became a park for everyone. Take a moment to enjoy it calmly, out of respect for the residents of Gràcia who live just below.","new_facts":["The bench's curving bays allow people to sit facing each other in small groups.","The columned hall beneath the terrace was planned as a market for the estate's residents.","The outer columns of the hall lean slightly and are inspired by ancient Greek (Doric) temples.","Rainwater filters through the terrace, runs down channels inside the columns and collects in a cistern below.","The stored water could be used to water the gardens."]},"art":{"short":"Lean in close to the bench. Its skin is a patchwork of broken tiles and crockery, a mosaic technique called trencadís, much of it credited to Gaudí's collaborator Josep Maria Jujol. Let your eye wander over the shards of colour and see what shapes you find. Nothing here was meant to be tidy.","normal":"Lean in close to the bench and study its skin. It's a patchwork of broken tiles and crockery, a mosaic technique known as trencadís. Gaudí worked here with his collaborator Josep Maria Jujol, who is credited with much of the bench's playful decoration. Let your eye wander over the shards of colour and fragments of old patterns, and see what shapes you find. Nothing here was meant to be tidy. Born from a failed housing scheme, the park became a canvas everyone can share. Take your time, and leave room for neighbours passing through.","long":"Lean in close to the bench and study its skin. It's a patchwork of broken tiles and crockery, a mosaic technique known as trencadís, from the Catalan word for broken. Much of the ceramic came as offcuts and rejects from tile factories, and broken plates and cups were mixed in too, so look carefully and you may spot the rim of a dish or a fragment of an old printed pattern. Gaudí worked here with his collaborator Josep Maria Jujol, who is credited with much of the bench's playful decoration. Jujol had a painter's eye and loved to improvise, and you can feel that freedom in the way colours clash and then settle into harmony as you move along. Look too for words and small symbols scratched into the tiles; Jujol added religious inscriptions here and there, almost like secret notes. If you go down to the hall of columns below, glance up at the ceiling, where large round mosaic medallions, also attributed to Jujol, glow like suns. And by the main entrance stairs you'll find the colourful mosaic dragon, which many people call the salamander. Let your eye wander over the shards of colour and fragments of old patterns, and see what shapes you find. Nothing here was meant to be tidy. Born from a failed housing scheme, the park became a canvas everyone can share. Take your time, and leave room for neighbours passing through.","new_facts":["The name trencadís comes from the Catalan word for broken.","Much of the ceramic was offcuts and rejects from tile factories, mixed with broken plates and cups.","Jujol had a painter's eye and liked to improvise.","Jujol added religious inscriptions and symbols scratched into the bench's tiles.","The ceiling of the hall of columns has large round mosaic medallions attributed to Jujol.","A colourful mosaic dragon, often called the salamander, sits on the main entrance stairs."]}},"tip":"Book a timed ticket for the monumental zone — the forest paths above are free."},"arc-de-triomf":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This red-brick arch never celebrated a battle. It was the grand entrance to Barcelona's first world fair, the 1888 Universal Exhibition, designed by Josep Vilaseca. Look closely at the brickwork and you'll spot Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage. The promenade beyond leads to Parc de la Ciutadella.","normal":"This red-brick arch was not built to celebrate a battle. It was the grand entrance to the 1888 Universal Exhibition, Barcelona's first world fair, designed by the architect Josep Vilaseca. Look closely at the brickwork and you'll spot Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage, while the carved friezes welcome visitors to the city. From here, the wide promenade leads straight to Parc de la Ciutadella, where the exhibition itself was held.","long":"This red-brick arch was not built to celebrate a battle. It was the grand entrance to the 1888 Universal Exhibition, Barcelona's first world fair, and its architect was Josep Vilaseca. The city wanted to show the world it was modern, confident and open to visitors, and this arch was the first thing many of them saw. Start with the material. Rather than marble or stone, Vilaseca used red brick, and he laid it in Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage. This style is often called Neo-Mudéjar, and it was fashionable in Spain at the time. Now look at the carved frieze on the side facing Passeig de Sant Joan. It was made by the sculptor Josep Reynés, and it shows Barcelona welcoming the nations who came to the fair. Walk through and turn around, and on the park side you'll find a second frieze by Josep Llimona, on the theme of the awards given out at the exhibition. Near the top, look for the shields of Spain's provinces, crowned by Barcelona's own coat of arms. Then follow the wide promenade, Passeig de Lluís Companys, lined with palm trees and tall, ornate street lamps. It leads straight to Parc de la Ciutadella, where the exhibition itself was held. People live in the buildings all around you, so enjoy the view quietly and keep the pavement clear for neighbours on their way home.","new_facts":["The decorative style is often called Neo-Mudéjar and was fashionable in Spain at the time","The frieze facing Passeig de Sant Joan is by sculptor Josep Reynés and shows Barcelona welcoming the nations","The frieze on the park side is by Josep Llimona and deals with the awards given at the exhibition","The upper part bears shields of Spain's provinces, crowned by Barcelona's coat of arms","The promenade is called Passeig de Lluís Companys","The promenade is lined with palm trees and ornate street lamps"]},"architecture":{"short":"Here's a triumphal arch with no triumph behind it. Look up at the warm red brick. Josep Vilaseca skipped grand marble and turned humble brick into decoration, laying it in geometric, Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage. It was the entrance to the 1888 Universal Exhibition.","normal":"Here's a triumphal arch with no triumph behind it. Look up at the warm red brick. Instead of grand marble, the architect Josep Vilaseca chose humble brick and turned it into decoration. Notice how the bricks are laid in geometric, Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage. Now let your eyes travel to the carved friezes, which greet arrivals rather than glorify a war. The arch was built as the entrance to the 1888 Universal Exhibition, so it was designed to impress newcomers heading toward Parc de la Ciutadella.","long":"Here's a triumphal arch with no triumph behind it. Look up at the warm red brick. Instead of grand marble, the architect Josep Vilaseca chose humble brick and turned it into decoration. That choice belongs to a style often called Neo-Mudéjar, which revived the brick craft of Spain's medieval Mudéjar builders and was popular across the country in the late nineteenth century. Notice how the bricks step in and out, laid in geometric, Moorish-style patterns, a nod to Spain's Islamic heritage. Let the shadows show you the depth. Now let your eyes travel to the carved friezes, which greet arrivals rather than glorify a war. On the side facing Passeig de Sant Joan, the sculptor Josep Reynés showed Barcelona welcoming the nations to the fair. On the side facing the park, Josep Llimona's frieze is about the awards handed out at the exhibition. So one face says hello, and the other says well done. Higher up, look for the row of shields representing Spain's provinces, with Barcelona's own coat of arms crowning the top. The arch was built as the entrance to the 1888 Universal Exhibition, so it was designed to impress newcomers heading toward Parc de la Ciutadella. Glance down the promenade, Passeig de Lluís Companys, and you'll see ornate street lamps that carry the same celebratory mood all the way toward the park. If you stop to sketch or photograph details, step to the edge of the pavement so residents can pass easily.","new_facts":["The style is often called Neo-Mudéjar, reviving the brick craft of Spain's medieval Mudéjar builders, and was popular in late nineteenth-century Spain","The frieze facing Passeig de Sant Joan is by Josep Reynés and shows Barcelona welcoming the nations","The frieze facing the park is by Josep Llimona and concerns the awards given at the exhibition","The upper part bears shields of Spain's provinces, crowned by Barcelona's coat of arms","The promenade is called Passeig de Lluís Companys and has ornate street lamps"]},"history":{"short":"Imagine Barcelona in 1888, eager to impress the world. This arch was the front door to its first world fair, the Universal Exhibition. No general ever marched beneath it in victory; crowds of visitors passed through toward Parc de la Ciutadella. Look at the carved friezes. Their message is simply welcome.","normal":"Imagine Barcelona in 1888, eager to show the world what it could do. This arch was the front door to the city's first world fair, the Universal Exhibition. No general ever marched beneath it in victory. Instead, crowds of visitors passed through on their way to Parc de la Ciutadella, where the exhibition was held. The park's name recalls the military citadel that once stood on that ground. Look at the carved friezes. Their message is simply welcome. People live all around here, so take in the story quietly, as a guest of the neighbourhood.","long":"Imagine Barcelona in 1888, eager to show the world what it could do. This arch was the front door to the city's first world fair, the Universal Exhibition. No general ever marched beneath it in victory. Instead, crowds of visitors passed through on their way to Parc de la Ciutadella, where the exhibition was held. The park's name recalls the military citadel that once stood on that ground, and that story gives this arch its meaning. After Barcelona fell in 1714, at the end of the War of the Spanish Succession, King Philip V built the citadel to keep the city under control, and many homes were cleared to make room for it. For generations, people here saw it as a symbol of oppression. In the nineteenth century it was finally pulled down, and the land was turned into a public park. So when Barcelona invited the world to a fair on that very ground, it was making a statement about who it had become. Today it is the people of Barcelona, not soldiers, who stroll toward that ground. Look at the carved friezes. On the side facing Passeig de Sant Joan, Josep Reynés showed the city welcoming the nations. On the park side, Josep Llimona's frieze honours the awards given at the fair. Their message is simply welcome, and well done. The architect, Josep Vilaseca, chose red brick laid in Moorish-style patterns. People live all around here, so take in the story quietly, as a guest of the neighbourhood.","new_facts":["Barcelona fell in 1714 at the end of the War of the Spanish Succession","King Philip V built the citadel afterwards to control the city, clearing many homes","The citadel was widely seen locally as a symbol of oppression","The citadel was demolished in the nineteenth century and the land became a public park","The frieze facing Passeig de Sant Joan is by Josep Reynés and shows the city welcoming the nations","The frieze on the park side is by Josep Llimona and concerns the awards given at the fair"]},"outdoors":{"short":"Stand back and let the sky do the work. When sunlight hits this arch, its red brick glows, changing as the light shifts through the day. A wide promenade stretches away toward Parc de la Ciutadella, home of the 1888 Universal Exhibition. Pause, enjoy the light, and keep voices low.","normal":"Stand back for a moment and let the sky do the work. When sunlight hits this arch, its red brick glows, and it looks different as the light shifts through the day. Notice how open everything feels here. A wide promenade stretches away from the arch, giving you room to breathe in the middle of the city. At its far end lies Parc de la Ciutadella, the green space that hosted the 1888 Universal Exhibition. Find a spot to pause and enjoy the light, and keep voices low. This is a real neighbourhood, with homes close by.","long":"Stand back for a moment and let the sky do the work. When sunlight hits this arch, its red brick glows, and it looks different as the light shifts through the day. Early and late, when the sun is low, the brick warms toward orange, and the Moorish-style patterns in the brickwork throw small shadows that make the surface look almost carved. Around midday the colour flattens and the details soften. If you have time, come back at another hour and compare. Notice how open everything feels here. A wide promenade, Passeig de Lluís Companys, stretches away from the arch, giving you room to breathe in the middle of the city. Look along it and you'll see tall palm trees and ornate street lamps lining the way, with benches where you can sit for a while. Listen, too: footsteps, bicycles, conversations drifting past, the everyday sound of a city taking a breather. At its far end lies Parc de la Ciutadella, the green space that hosted the 1888 Universal Exhibition, and it's a pleasant, easy walk if you want to continue. Before you go, look back at the arch from a distance. From further down the promenade you can take in the whole shape at once, framed against the sky. Find a spot to pause and enjoy the light, and keep voices low. This is a real neighbourhood, with homes close by, and the people who live here use this promenade every day.","new_facts":["The promenade is called Passeig de Lluís Companys","The promenade is lined with palm trees, ornate street lamps and benches"]}},"tip":"Walk down the promenade towards Ciutadella: it's one of the most pleasant short walks in the city."},"placa-reial":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This arcaded square was built in the 19th century where a convent once stood. Now look at the tall iron lampposts, with their helmets and curling arms. They were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, long before the Sagrada Família. Around you, cafés turn it into an outdoor living room.","normal":"This elegant arcaded square was built in the 19th century on land where a convent once stood. Look at the tall iron lampposts with their helmets and curling arms: they were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, long before the Sagrada Família. Palm trees, a central fountain and the cafés under the arches make it feel like an outdoor living room, busy from morning coffee to late at night.","long":"Welcome to Plaça Reial, one of the most elegant squares in Barcelona, tucked just off La Rambla. Before you take in the details, picture what stood here before: a convent belonging to the Capuchin friars. In the 19th century that land was cleared, and this arcaded square was built in its place, designed by the architect Francesc Daniel Molina as one harmonious whole. Notice how the same arches repeat all the way around, and how the buildings above them match, floor by floor, so the square feels closed off and calm, almost like a courtyard. Now look at the tall iron lampposts. Each one spreads six curling arms, and on top sits a helmet with wings. That is the winged helmet of Mercury, the Roman god of commerce, a nod to a city that lived by trade. These lampposts were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, commissioned by the city long before anyone had heard of the Sagrada Família. If you look closely, you can already sense his love of curves and ornament. In the middle, the fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces. The palm trees around it soften all those straight lines and give the square its relaxed, almost southern feel. From morning coffee to late at night, the cafés under the arches turn this place into an outdoor living room. People live in the flats above, so enjoy it the way they do: unhurried, and kindly.","new_facts":["The square is just off La Rambla","The convent belonged to the Capuchin friars","The square was designed by the architect Francesc Daniel Molina as a unified whole","The Gaudí lampposts each have six arms","The lampposts are topped by the winged helmet of Mercury, Roman god of commerce","The lampposts were commissioned by the city","The central fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces","People live in the flats above the arcades"]},"architecture":{"short":"Look up at the tall iron lampposts beside the fountain. Notice their helmet-like crowns and curling arms. These were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, long before the Sagrada Família. Around them, this 19th-century square was designed as one harmonious whole, arch after arch.","normal":"Look up at the tall iron lampposts beside the fountain. Notice their helmet-like crowns and curling arms. These were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, long before the Sagrada Família. Now turn slowly and take in the square itself. It was laid out in the 19th century on land where a convent once stood, and designed as one harmonious whole. See how the arcades repeat all the way around, arch after arch, giving the space its calm, orderly rhythm, while the palm trees soften all those straight lines.","long":"Start by looking up at the tall iron lampposts beside the fountain. Notice their helmet-like crowns and the curling arms that branch out from the central shaft, six of them on each lamppost. The helmet has little wings: it is the winged helmet of Mercury, the Roman god of commerce. These were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí, commissioned by the city long before the Sagrada Família. Even here you can see his taste for flowing curves, although the design is still quite traditional compared with what came later. Now turn slowly and take in the square itself. It was laid out in the 19th century on land where a convent once stood, and the architect Francesc Daniel Molina designed it as one harmonious whole. That is why every facade matches its neighbour. See how the arcades repeat all the way around, arch after arch, and how the windows and balconies above line up in steady rows. The square is almost completely enclosed, with only a few narrow openings to the surrounding streets, so it feels like a large courtyard set apart from the bustle outside. The open paved space lets the architecture speak, and the palm trees soften all those straight lines. Finally, back in the centre, the fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces, its figures standing together above the water. The arches above you frame homes, so as you look, remember that people live behind those balconies.","new_facts":["The Gaudí lampposts each have six arms","The lampposts are topped by the winged helmet of Mercury, Roman god of commerce","The lampposts were commissioned by the city","The square was designed by the architect Francesc Daniel Molina","The square is almost fully enclosed, reached through a few narrow openings","The central fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces","People live in the homes above the arcades"]},"local":{"short":"Picture someone lingering over a coffee under these arches before the day gets busy. From morning until late, the cafés turn Plaça Reial into an outdoor living room. Find a shady spot by the palm trees, watch neighbours stop to chat, and share the space the way locals do: unhurried and kind.","normal":"Picture this square at breakfast time, with someone lingering over a coffee under the arches before the day gets busy. From morning until late, the cafés beneath these arcades turn Plaça Reial into a kind of outdoor living room for the Gothic Quarter. Find a shady spot near the palm trees or the central fountain and simply watch: people cutting across on their way somewhere, friends stopping to chat. This is a real neighbourhood, not a stage set. So slow down, take a seat, and share the space the way locals do, unhurried and kind.","long":"Picture this square at breakfast time, with someone lingering over a coffee under the arches before the day gets busy. Maybe a waiter is still setting out chairs, and the light is just reaching the top floors. From morning until late, the cafés beneath these arcades turn Plaça Reial into a kind of outdoor living room for the Gothic Quarter. Find a shady spot near the palm trees or the central fountain, known as the Fountain of the Three Graces, and simply watch. You will see people cutting across on their way somewhere, using the square as a shortcut between La Rambla, just a few steps away, and the narrow streets of the old town. You will see friends stopping to chat, maybe someone carrying shopping home. Look up at the balconies above the arches. Those are homes. People live here, sleep here and wake up here, which is why this is a real neighbourhood, not a stage set. Notice the tall iron lampposts too, among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí. Many locals walk past them every day without a second glance, and that is part of the charm: history here is simply part of daily life. As the evening goes on, the square fills up and gets livelier, and it stays busy late into the night. If you come back then, remember that the windows above you belong to people who need their sleep, so keep your voice down as you leave. So slow down, take a seat, and share the space the way locals do, unhurried and kind.","new_facts":["The central fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces","The square is just a few steps from La Rambla","People live in the homes above the arcades","The tall iron lampposts were among the first public works of a young Antoni Gaudí"]},"history":{"short":"Stand by the fountain and imagine quiet cloisters: a convent once stood right here. In the 19th century this arcaded square rose in its place. Then look at the tall iron lampposts, designed by a young, little-known Antoni Gaudí, long before anyone imagined his Sagrada Família.","normal":"Stand by the fountain and imagine walls and quiet cloisters, because before this square existed, a convent stood right here. In the 19th century that land was given a new life, and this grand arcaded square rose in its place, designed by the architect Francesc Daniel Molina. Later, a young and little-known Antoni Gaudí designed something for it: look at those tall iron lampposts with their helmets and curling arms. They were among his first public works. Nobody then could have guessed he would one day become the architect of the Sagrada Família.","long":"Stand by the fountain and imagine walls and quiet cloisters, because before this square existed, a convent stood right here. It belonged to the Capuchin friars, a place of prayer and quiet in the middle of the old city. In the 19th century, after the convent was gone, that land was given a new life. This grand arcaded square rose in its place, designed by the architect Francesc Daniel Molina. Look around and you can see his plan: one harmonious whole, with matching facades and arches repeating all the way around, a single elegant composition rather than a jumble of separate buildings. Its name, Plaça Reial, means Royal Square. Later, a young and little-known Antoni Gaudí designed something for it. Look at those tall iron lampposts with their helmets and curling arms. The city commissioned them, and they were among his first public works. On top, the winged helmet of Mercury, the Roman god of commerce, stands as a symbol of trade. Nobody then could have guessed that this young man would one day become the architect of the Sagrada Família. The fountain beside you is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces. And all around, the arcades built where a convent once stood now shelter cafés, while people live in the homes above. Layer upon layer, the square has kept changing, yet it remains part of everyday life in the Gothic Quarter.","new_facts":["The convent belonged to the Capuchin friars","Plaça Reial means Royal Square","The square was designed as a unified whole with matching facades","The lampposts were commissioned by the city","The lampposts are topped by the winged helmet of Mercury, Roman god of commerce","The central fountain is known as the Fountain of the Three Graces","Cafés occupy the arcades and people live in the homes above"]}},"tip":"People live in the flats above the arcades: at night, enjoy the square quietly."},"placa-sant-felip-neri":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This is one of the quietest corners of the old town, and one of the saddest. Look at the church façade, pitted with holes. They are scars from a bombing raid in January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, which killed dozens of people, many of them children. The marks were left on purpose.","normal":"This is one of the quietest corners of the old town, and one of the saddest. Look at the church façade: it is pitted with holes. They are scars from a bombing raid in January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, when bombs fell on the square and killed dozens of people, many of them children. The marks were left on purpose, so that no one forgets.","long":"This is one of the quietest corners of the old town, and one of the saddest. Take a moment before you look up. Notice how the noise of the busy streets drops away here, how the small fountain in the middle softens everything. Now look at the church façade. It is pitted with holes, some shallow, some deep enough to put your fingers in. They are scars from a bombing raid on the thirtieth of January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War. The planes were Italian, flying in support of Franco's forces, and Barcelona was being bombed from the air again and again that year. Bombs fell on this square and killed dozens of people, many of them children who had come here seeking shelter. A second bomb fell while people were trying to help the wounded. The church itself dates from the eighteenth century, but it is these marks from one winter morning that people remember. They were left on purpose, so that no one forgets. Look for the plaque nearby that remembers the victims. Then look around you. There is still a school on this square, and on a weekday you may hear children's voices echoing off the same walls. People live in the flats above you, too. So if you stay a while, keep your voice low and give the square its quiet. It is a beautiful place, and it is also a place of mourning. Both things are true here at once.","new_facts":["The raid took place on 30 January 1938, in the morning","The bombers were Italian aircraft supporting Franco's forces","Barcelona was bombed from the air repeatedly in 1938","Many of the children killed had been sheltering at the church","A second bomb fell while people were helping the wounded","The church dates from the eighteenth century","There is a fountain in the middle of the square","A plaque in the square commemorates the victims","A school still operates on the square"]},"history":{"short":"Look at the church wall in front of you. Those pits and gouges aren't weathering. In January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, bombs fell on this little square and killed dozens of people, many of them children. The scars were left on purpose, as a memorial. Pause quietly; people live here.","normal":"Look at the church wall in front of you. Those pits and gouges in the stone are not weathering. In January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, bombs fell on this small square, and dozens of people were killed, many of them children. The damage could have been patched over, but it was deliberately left as it is, so that no one forgets. Those scars are a memorial written by war itself. Pause here for a quiet moment; this is still a real neighbourhood where people live and work.","long":"Look at the church wall in front of you, the façade of the church of Sant Felip Neri. Those pits and gouges in the stone are not weathering. On the thirtieth of January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, bombs dropped by Italian aircraft fighting on Franco's side fell on this small square. Dozens of people were killed, and many of them were children who had taken shelter in the basement of the church. Barcelona was bombed many times during that war, but few places carry the memory as plainly as this one. The damage could easily have been patched over. Instead it was deliberately left as it is, so that no one forgets. Step a little closer, without touching, and you'll see the marks scattered across the stone, some shallow, some deep enough to hold a shadow. Look for the plaque nearby that remembers the people who died here. Those scars are a memorial written by war itself, and once you know their story they need no further explanation. Now turn slowly and take in the rest of the square: the fountain in the middle, the old buildings closing you in on every side. It's hard to imagine violence in a place this hushed. There's a school on this square today, and children's voices still carry across these stones, which makes that wall feel even more poignant. Pause here for a quiet moment. Keep your voice low, and if you take a photo, be mindful of the people around you. This is still a real neighbourhood where people live and work.","new_facts":["The church is the church of Sant Felip Neri","The bombing took place on 30 January 1938","The bombs were dropped by Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side","Many of the children killed had taken shelter in the church's basement","Barcelona was bombed many times during the Spanish Civil War","A plaque in the square commemorates the victims","There is a fountain in the middle of the square","There is a school on the square today"]},"culture":{"short":"This enclosed, hushed square feels like a stage set, and scenes of the film Perfume are often said to have been shot here. But the marks on that church wall are real: a bombing in January 1938, during the Civil War, killed dozens, many of them children. Take it in quietly; people live here.","normal":"Notice how this square feels almost like a stage set: enclosed, hushed, with the scarred church façade as its backdrop. Filmmakers have noticed too. Scenes of the film Perfume: The Story of a Murderer were shot in Barcelona's old town, and this square is often named among its locations. But the marks on that wall are real, not props. They come from a bombing in January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, that killed dozens, many of them children. Kept on purpose, they turn this quiet corner into a memorial. Remember it's a living neighbourhood, so take it in quietly.","long":"Notice how this square feels almost like a stage set. It's enclosed on every side, hushed, with a fountain at its centre and the scarred church façade as its backdrop. Filmmakers have noticed too. Scenes of the film Perfume: The Story of a Murderer, directed by Tom Tykwer and released in 2006, were shot in Barcelona's old town, standing in for eighteenth-century France, and this square is often named among its locations. It's easy to see why. Look at the old stone buildings around you. Two of them didn't start life here: they were guild houses, for the shoemakers and the coppersmiths, moved to this square from elsewhere in the city in the twentieth century. So even the set has been rearranged. But the marks on that wall are not props. They come from a bombing on the thirtieth of January 1938, during the Spanish Civil War, carried out by Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side. Dozens of people were killed, many of them children who had sheltered in the church's basement. The damage was kept on purpose, and that choice turns this quiet corner into a memorial. Once you know, the beauty of the place and its grief are hard to separate. Spend a moment looking at the pitted stone, and at the plaque that remembers the victims. There's a school on this square today, and daily life carries on all around you. Remember it's a living neighbourhood, so take it in quietly and keep your voice low.","new_facts":["Perfume: The Story of a Murderer was directed by Tom Tykwer","The film was released in 2006","Barcelona's old town stood in for eighteenth-century France in the film","Two buildings on the square, the shoemakers' and coppersmiths' guild houses, were moved here from elsewhere in the city in the twentieth century","The bombing took place on 30 January 1938","The bombs were dropped by Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side","Many of the children killed had sheltered in the church's basement","There is a fountain at the centre of the square","A plaque in the square commemorates the victims","There is a school on the square today"]}},"tip":"There is a school on the square: please keep your voice low."},"temple-august":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Hidden in a small courtyard on Carrer del Paradís stand four Roman columns, nearly two thousand years old. They belonged to a temple to the emperor Augustus, built on Mont Tàber, the highest point of the Roman town of Barcino. Step inside, look up, and picture the temple crowning this modest hill.","normal":"Hidden inside a small courtyard on Carrer del Paradís stand four Roman columns, nearly two thousand years old. They belonged to the temple dedicated to the emperor Augustus, built on the highest point of the Roman town of Barcino, a small hill called Mont Tàber. Outside the door, look for a round millstone set into the pavement: it marks the top of that hill.","long":"Hidden inside a small courtyard on Carrer del Paradís stand four Roman columns, nearly two thousand years old. Before you go in, look down outside the door. Set into the pavement is a round millstone. It marks the top of Mont Tàber, a small hill that was the highest point of the Roman town of Barcino. You might not notice much of a climb, but this is where the Romans built their temple to the emperor Augustus. Now step inside, and keep your voice low. This courtyard belongs to the Centre Excursionista de Catalunya, a hiking and mountaineering club that still uses the building. Look up. The columns rise about nine metres, and their tops are carved in the Corinthian style, with curling acanthus leaves. Let your eyes run down the shafts and notice the long vertical grooves, called fluting, cut along their length. At the bottom, the columns rest on a raised stone base, part of the platform the temple stood on. Picture the whole building around you, with more columns like these holding up a roof and the little town spreading down the slopes on every side. Barcino's main square, its forum, lay just nearby, around today's Plaça Sant Jaume. For centuries, later houses grew up around these columns and hid them from view. Today they stand in a quiet corner of a living neighbourhood. Enjoy them, and leave the courtyard as calm as you found it.","new_facts":["The courtyard belongs to the Centre Excursionista de Catalunya, a hiking and mountaineering club","The columns are about nine metres tall","The columns have Corinthian capitals with acanthus leaves","The column shafts are fluted","The columns stand on part of the temple's raised podium","Barcino's forum lay near today's Plaça Sant Jaume","For centuries the columns were hidden inside later buildings"]},"history":{"short":"You are standing on the summit of a Roman town: Mont Tàber, the highest point of little Barcino. Here the Romans raised a temple to Augustus, the man who turned Rome from a republic into an empire. Look down outside the door: a round millstone in the pavement marks the top of that hill.","normal":"You are standing on the summit of a Roman town. Barcino was small, and its highest point was a modest hill called Mont Tàber. Right here, the Romans raised a temple to the emperor Augustus, the man who turned Rome from a republic into an empire. Before you go in, look down outside the door: a round millstone set into the pavement marks the top of that hill. Then step into the courtyard and look up at four Roman columns, nearly two thousand years old, still standing among the homes and workplaces of a living neighbourhood.","long":"You are standing on the summit of a Roman town. Barcino was small, and its highest point was a modest hill called Mont Tàber. Before you go in, look down outside the door. A round millstone set into the pavement marks the top of that hill. It is easy to walk past, so take a moment to find it. Right here, the Romans raised a temple to the emperor Augustus, the man who turned Rome from a republic into an empire. He was Rome's first emperor, and Barcino itself was founded in his time as a Roman colony. Its official name even carried his title, Augusta. Placing his temple on the highest ground gave the emperor pride of place above the whole town. Now step into the courtyard, quietly, because this is a working building, home to the Centre Excursionista de Catalunya, a hiking and mountaineering club. Look up at four Roman columns, nearly two thousand years old. They rise about nine metres, topped with Corinthian capitals carved with acanthus leaves, and their shafts are cut with long vertical grooves. Look at their feet too. They stand on part of the temple's raised stone platform. For centuries these columns were hidden, wrapped inside later houses, as the city simply built around them. Today they still stand among the homes and workplaces of a living neighbourhood. Take your time, then let the residents get on with their day as you head back out into the street.","new_facts":["Augustus was Rome's first emperor","Barcino was founded in the time of Augustus as a Roman colony","Barcino's official name included the title Augusta","The courtyard belongs to the Centre Excursionista de Catalunya, a hiking and mountaineering club","The columns are about nine metres tall","The columns have Corinthian capitals with acanthus leaves","The column shafts are fluted","The columns stand on part of the temple's raised podium","For centuries the columns were hidden inside later buildings"]}},"tip":"Entry to the courtyard is free; step in quietly, it's a working building."},"placa-sant-jaume":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Stand in the middle and turn around. On one side, the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government. Facing it, Barcelona's City Hall. Two governments looking at each other across one square, where the Roman forum once stood. Look from one façade to the other: this is where the city still gathers.","normal":"Stand in the middle and turn around: on one side is the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government, and facing it is Barcelona's City Hall. Two governments looking at each other across one square. This was already the heart of the city in Roman times, when the forum stood here. It's still where people gather to celebrate, protest and watch human towers on festival days.","long":"Stand in the middle of the square and turn slowly all the way around. On one side is the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government. Directly facing it is Barcelona's City Hall. Two governments, looking at each other across one open space. Few places put power so plainly face to face. Take a closer look at the Generalitat first. Its front on this square is in a sober Renaissance style. Above the entrance, in a niche, stands Saint George, patron saint of Catalonia, the dragon-slayer you'll meet all over this city. Now turn to City Hall. The front you see here is neoclassical, added much later, but walk round the corner onto Carrer de la Ciutat and you'll find its older Gothic façade tucked into the side street. None of this is new ground. This was already the heart of the city in Roman times, when the forum of Barcino stood here, close to where the town's two main streets crossed. Just a few steps away, on Carrer del Paradís, four tall columns survive from the Roman temple of Augustus, standing inside a building's courtyard. The square itself takes its name from a church of Saint James that once stood here. Today it's still where people gather to celebrate and to protest, and on festival days to watch teams of castellers build human towers between the two façades. People live in the lanes all around, so as you head off exploring, keep your voice low.","new_facts":["The Roman town here was called Barcino","The Generalitat's façade on the square is in Renaissance style","A statue of Saint George, patron saint of Catalonia, stands in a niche above the Generalitat's entrance","City Hall's façade on the square is neoclassical and was built later","City Hall has an older Gothic façade on Carrer de la Ciutat","The forum stood close to where the Roman town's two main streets crossed","Four columns of the Roman temple of Augustus survive inside a building's courtyard on Carrer del Paradís","The square is named after a church of Saint James that once stood here"]},"history":{"short":"Stand in the middle and picture stone columns around you: two thousand years ago, this was Barcelona's Roman forum. Now look at the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government, then turn to City Hall directly opposite. Two governments, eye to eye. People work inside, so keep your voice gentle.","normal":"Stand in the middle and picture stone columns around you. This spot was already the heart of the city in Roman times, when the forum stood here. Two thousand years later, power still lives here. Look to one side at the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government, then turn to face Barcelona's City Hall directly opposite. Two governments, eye to eye across one square. Many of the city's turning points have played out between these façades, with crowds gathering to celebrate or protest. People still work inside these buildings, so keep your voice gentle.","long":"Stand in the middle of the square and picture stone columns rising around you. This spot was already the heart of the city in Roman times, when the town was called Barcino and its forum stood right here, close to the highest point of the little hill the Romans built on. If you have time later, walk a few steps to Carrer del Paradís, where four tall columns from the Roman temple still stand inside a courtyard. They're a reminder of how close that ancient city is beneath your feet. Two thousand years later, power still lives here. Look to one side at the Palau de la Generalitat, home of the Catalan government. Above its main door, find the carving of Saint George, Sant Jordi, the patron saint of Catalonia, on horseback. Then turn to face Barcelona's City Hall directly opposite, with its more formal, classical front. Two governments, eye to eye across one square, each with its own balcony looking at the other. The square itself is younger than you might think. For centuries this was a tight knot of streets, with a church dedicated to Saint James, Sant Jaume, which gave the square its name. The church was taken down and the space opened up in the nineteenth century. Many of the city's turning points have played out between these façades, with crowds gathering to celebrate or protest. Take a slow look around and imagine those crowds. People still work inside these buildings, so keep your voice gentle.","new_facts":["The Roman town on this site was called Barcino.","The forum stood near the highest point of the hill on which the Roman town was built.","Four columns of the Roman temple survive nearby on Carrer del Paradís, inside a courtyard.","The Palau de la Generalitat's façade on the square has a sculpture of Sant Jordi (Saint George) on horseback above the main door.","Sant Jordi is the patron saint of Catalonia.","City Hall's façade on the square is in a classical style.","The square is named after a church of Sant Jaume (Saint James) that stood here.","The church was demolished and the square opened up in the nineteenth century."]},"local":{"short":"Notice who's crossing the square right now: office workers on a break, people heading into City Hall, neighbours cutting through on their way home. For Barcelonans, this is the city's living room. If you land in a gathering, enjoy it from the edges and leave room for the people who live here.","normal":"Notice who's crossing the square right now. Some are visitors, but many are locals: people heading into City Hall, office workers on a break, neighbours cutting through the Gothic Quarter on their way home. For Barcelonans, this is something like the city's living room. When there's something to celebrate or something to protest, this is where people come together. On festival days, human towers rise right here. If you find yourself in the middle of a gathering, enjoy it from the edges and leave room for the people who live here. This is a real neighbourhood, after all.","long":"Notice who's crossing the square right now. Some are visitors, but many are locals: people heading into City Hall, office workers on a coffee break, neighbours cutting through the Gothic Quarter on their way home. For Barcelonans, this is something like the city's living room. When there's something to celebrate or something to protest, this is where people come together, often looking up at the balconies of the two government buildings facing each other across the stones. Look at the flags on those balconies, too. You'll usually see the Catalan flag, with its red and yellow stripes, flying alongside others. On certain days banners appear that tell you what the city is talking about. On festival days, human towers rise right here. Groups of castellers, each in their own coloured shirt, build a broad base of arms and shoulders. Then they climb higher and higher, until a small child scrambles to the very top and raises one hand. The whole square holds its breath and then cheers. These towers are one of Catalonia's most loved traditions, recognised by UNESCO as part of the world's cultural heritage. One big occasion is La Mercè, the city's main festival in September, when castellers gather in this very square. If you find yourself in the middle of a gathering, enjoy it from the edges. Keep your bag close and your voice friendly, and leave room for the people who live here. This is a real neighbourhood, after all, not a stage set.","new_facts":["The Catalan flag has red and yellow stripes and is usually flown on the buildings' balconies.","Each casteller group wears its own coloured shirt.","A child climbs to the top of a human tower and raises one hand.","Human towers (castells) are recognised by UNESCO as part of the world's intangible cultural heritage.","La Mercè is Barcelona's main festival, held in September, and castellers perform in Plaça de Sant Jaume during it."]},"sports":{"short":"Look up at the balconies. When FC Barcelona wins a major title, the players have traditionally come here to show off their trophies, and the square fills with fans in blue and red. The rest of the time it's a calm, working square in a lived-in neighbourhood, so save the chanting for game day.","normal":"Look up at the balconies around you. When FC Barcelona wins a major title, the players have traditionally come here to show off their trophies, and the square fills with fans in blue and red. Glance from the Palau de la Generalitat to City Hall opposite, because the team is usually welcomed at both. On festival days you'll see another kind of teamwork: castellers building human towers on each other's shoulders while the crowd holds its breath. The rest of the time, this is a calm, working square in a lived-in neighbourhood, so save the chanting for game day.","long":"Look up at the balconies around you. When FC Barcelona wins a major title, the players have traditionally come here to show off their trophies. The square below fills with fans in blue and red, the club's famous blaugrana colours. Picture it packed shoulder to shoulder, scarves in the air and Catalan flags waving among the club shirts. Glance from the Palau de la Generalitat to City Hall opposite, because the team is usually welcomed at both. That's a sign of how closely the club is tied to the city and to Catalonia. Its famous slogan, Més que un club, means more than a club. Moments like these, when footballers are received by the government like visiting dignitaries, help explain why. On festival days you'll see another kind of teamwork. Castellers build human towers on each other's shoulders. A wide base of people pushes in tight at the bottom, steadier layers stand above, and finally a small child climbs to the very top to raise one hand. Every person has a job, and if one slips, the whole tower can wobble, so the crowd holds its breath until the climbers come safely down. If you're here when they perform, listen for the traditional pipes and drums that play as the tower rises. The rest of the time, this is a calm, working square in a lived-in neighbourhood. People are working inside these buildings and living in the streets around them, so enjoy the view quietly and save the chanting for game day.","new_facts":["FC Barcelona's blue and red colours are known as blaugrana.","FC Barcelona's well-known slogan is 'Més que un club' (more than a club).","A human tower has a wide, tightly packed base at the bottom, and a child climbs to the top and raises one hand.","Traditional pipes and drums play while a human tower is being built."]}},"tip":"Walk round the corner into Carrer del Bisbe to see Sant Jordi carved on the Generalitat's Gothic façade."},"fossar-moreres":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"You're standing on a former cemetery. This sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar holds the defenders who died in the siege of Barcelona, which ended on 11 September 1714. Find the inscription and read it: it says that no traitor is buried here. Every 11 September, people gather here to remember.","normal":"This small, sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar was a cemetery for the defenders who died during the siege of Barcelona, which ended on 11 September 1714. Read the inscription: it says that no traitor is buried here. Today the red arch and the eternal flame make it a place of memory, and every 11 September, Catalonia's national day, people gather here to remember.","long":"You're standing in the Fossar de les Moreres, and the name tells you what this place was. Fossar is Catalan for graveyard, and moreres are mulberry trees. This small, sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar was the church's burial ground, and it became the resting place of defenders who died during the siege of Barcelona, a siege that ended on 11 September 1714. Turn toward the church and take in its side wall: this Gothic basilica was raised in the fourteenth century, and it has watched over this ground ever since. Now find the inscription. It says that no traitor is buried here. Those words come from a poem by the nineteenth-century Catalan writer Frederic Soler, better known as Pitarra, and they turned a simple cemetery into a statement of loyalty. Next, look at the red arch and the eternal flame that burns there. Together they make this a place of memory rather than just a square. Every 11 September, Catalonia's national day, people gather here to remember. If you want to see more of what that year changed, walk to the end of Passeig del Born, where the Born cultural centre shows the remains of streets demolished after the siege to make room for a fortress. Before you go, notice the doors and balconies around you. People live here, so speak softly and leave the space as calm as you found it.","new_facts":["In Catalan, 'fossar' means graveyard and 'moreres' means mulberry trees.","The square was the burial ground of Santa Maria del Mar.","Santa Maria del Mar is a Gothic basilica built in the fourteenth century.","The inscription comes from a poem by the nineteenth-century Catalan writer Frederic Soler, known as Pitarra.","The Born cultural centre, at the end of Passeig del Born, displays remains of streets demolished after the siege to make room for a fortress."]},"history":{"short":"Look at the ground beneath you: this sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar was once a cemetery for defenders who died in the siege of Barcelona, which ended on the eleventh of September, 1714. Find the inscription and read it slowly. It declares that no traitor is buried here.","normal":"Look at the ground beneath you: this gently sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar was once a cemetery. Here lie the defenders who died during the siege of Barcelona, a siege that ended on the eleventh of September, 1714. Find the inscription and read it slowly. It declares that no traitor is buried here. Now notice the red arch and the eternal flame, which turn a burial ground into a place of memory. Every eleventh of September, Catalonia's national day, people gather here to remember. The rest of the year, it's a quiet corner of a real, lived-in neighbourhood.","long":"Look at the ground beneath you. This gently sloping square beside Santa Maria del Mar was once a cemetery, and its name still says so. In Catalan, fossar means graveyard, and moreres are mulberry trees. It was the burial ground of the church beside you, and here lie the defenders who died during the siege of Barcelona, a siege that ended on the eleventh of September, 1714. Lift your eyes to the great stone wall of Santa Maria del Mar. This Gothic church was built in the fourteenth century, so it had already stood for centuries when the siege came. Now find the inscription and read it slowly. It declares that no traitor is buried here. The line comes from a poem by Frederic Soler, a nineteenth-century Catalan playwright better known as Pitarra. Let the words settle before you move on. Now notice the red arch and the eternal flame, which turn a burial ground into a place of memory. Every eleventh of September, Catalonia's national day, people gather here to remember. If you'd like to see more of that story, walk to the far end of Passeig del Born. In the Born cultural centre there, you can see the excavated remains of streets that were demolished after the siege to make way for a fortress. The rest of the year, this is a quiet corner of a real, lived-in neighbourhood. People sleep, work and raise families behind these walls, so keep your voice low while you're here.","new_facts":["In Catalan, 'fossar' means graveyard and 'moreres' means mulberry trees.","The square was the burial ground of Santa Maria del Mar.","Santa Maria del Mar is a Gothic church built in the fourteenth century.","The inscription comes from a poem by Frederic Soler, a nineteenth-century Catalan playwright known as Pitarra.","The Born cultural centre, at the far end of Passeig del Born, displays excavated remains of streets demolished after the siege to make way for a fortress."]}},"tip":"It's a memorial, not a meeting point: keep it calm and respectful."},"antic-hospital":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This Gothic building, founded in 1401, was Barcelona's main hospital for over five hundred years. In 1926 an old man hit by a tram was brought here, so shabbily dressed nobody knew him. It was Antoni Gaudí, and he died here days later. Step into the courtyard and look for the orange trees.","normal":"For more than five hundred years this Gothic building was the city's main hospital, founded in 1401. In 1926 an old man hit by a tram was brought here, so poorly dressed that nobody recognised him at first. It was Antoni Gaudí, and he died here a few days later. Today the building holds the National Library of Catalonia, and its courtyard of orange trees is a quiet green refuge.","long":"You're standing at one of Barcelona's great survivors. This Gothic building was founded in 1401, when the city brought several smaller medieval hospitals together under one roof, and for more than five hundred years it was Barcelona's main hospital. Look at the walls around you: thick, plain stone, built for work rather than show. Inside, the old wards are long halls spanned by tall pointed arches, and for centuries the city's sick lay beneath them. Now picture June 1926. An old man, crossing the city on foot, is struck by a tram. His clothes are so worn and plain that people take him for a poor stranger, and nobody recognises him at first. He is brought here, to the hospital that cared for the city's poor. Only later does it become clear who he is: Antoni Gaudí, the architect of the Sagrada Família. He dies here a few days later, and today he lies buried in the crypt of the church he gave his final years to. By then the hospital's time here was ending. Its work moved to a new site, the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau, and these old halls found a quieter purpose. Today they hold the National Library of Catalonia, so where patients once rested, readers now sit with books. Before you go, wander into the courtyard. Orange trees grow among the old arcades, and the noise of El Raval fades. Neighbours cut through here every day and readers come to study, so keep your voice low and enjoy one of the calmest corners of the city.","new_facts":["Gaudí was struck by the tram in June 1926","The hospital was created by bringing several smaller medieval hospitals together","The old wards are long halls spanned by pointed stone arches","The hospital cared for the city's poor","Gaudí is buried in the crypt of the Sagrada Família","The hospital's work moved to the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau","The courtyard is lined with arcades"]},"history":{"short":"Picture an old man, struck by a tram in 1926, carried through these doors in clothes so shabby nobody recognises him. He is Antoni Gaudí, architect of the Sagrada Família, and he dies here days later. Look at these old stone walls: they were Barcelona's main hospital for over five centuries.","normal":"Picture an elderly man, struck by a tram in 1926, carried through these doors. His clothes are so shabby that at first nobody recognises him. He is Antoni Gaudí, the architect of the Sagrada Família, and a few days later he dies here. Look around at these old stone walls. This Gothic building, founded in 1401, was Barcelona's main hospital for more than five hundred years, and generations of the city's sick were cared for inside. Today it has a quieter life as home to the National Library of Catalonia.","long":"Picture a day in June 1926. An elderly man in a worn suit is walking across the city, as he did almost every day, on his way to the church of Sant Felip Neri. A tram strikes him and he falls. His clothes are so shabby that people take him for a poor old stranger. So he is brought here, to the hospital that cared for the city's poor, and at first nobody recognises him. He is Antoni Gaudí, the architect of the Sagrada Família. A few days later he dies here, inside these walls. He will be buried in the crypt of the Sagrada Família, the church he gave his final years to. Now look around at the old stone. This Gothic building was founded in 1401, when Barcelona gathered several smaller medieval hospitals into one, and for more than five hundred years it was the city's main hospital. Generations of the city's sick were carried through these same doors and nursed in long wards under tall pointed arches. Eventually the hospital moved to a new home, the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau, and the old building began a quieter life. Today it houses the National Library of Catalonia. As you stand here, imagine those two lives layered together: the sickrooms and the reading rooms, and the old man in shabby clothes who changed this city's skyline.","new_facts":["Gaudí was struck by the tram in June 1926","Gaudí walked almost daily to the church of Sant Felip Neri and was heading there when he was hit","The hospital cared for the city's poor","Gaudí is buried in the crypt of the Sagrada Família","The hospital was created by gathering several smaller medieval hospitals into one","The wards were long halls under pointed stone arches","The hospital moved to the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau"]},"architecture":{"short":"Look up: this is Gothic Barcelona at work, not at prayer. Founded in 1401, this served as the city's main hospital for over five hundred years. Notice the pointed arches and heavy, plain stone. Today it holds the National Library of Catalonia, and Gaudí, Barcelona's most daring architect, died inside these walls.","normal":"Look up at these stone walls and you're seeing Gothic Barcelona at work, not at prayer. Founded in 1401, this was built as a hospital, and it served as the city's main one for more than five hundred years. Notice the pointed arches and the heavy, plain stone. This was a building made to last, and it did. Spaces that once held patients now hold books, as home to the National Library of Catalonia. And here's a twist: Gaudí, Barcelona's most daring architect, died inside these walls in 1926.","long":"Look up at these stone walls and you're seeing Gothic Barcelona at work, not at prayer. We often think of Gothic as cathedrals and soaring towers, but this building, founded in 1401, was made for something more practical: caring for the sick. It served as the city's main hospital for more than five hundred years. Notice the pointed arches and the heavy, plain stone. There's very little decoration here, and that's typical of Catalan Gothic, which favoured broad, simple spaces and solid walls over lacy ornament. Inside, the old wards are long halls spanned by great pointed stone arches that carry the roof, leaving wide open floors where rows of beds once stood. Today those same halls are reading rooms of the National Library of Catalonia, and the arches now rise over shelves and desks instead of patients. It's a fine example of an old building finding a new life without losing its character. Step into the courtyard and you'll see arcades around the edges and orange trees in the middle, a calm open space at the heart of the complex. And here's the twist. Antoni Gaudí, Barcelona's most daring architect, died inside these walls in 1926, after being hit by a tram. Gaudí was deeply influenced by Gothic architecture and spent his career trying to push its ideas further, with curves and slanting columns in the Sagrada Família. So the man who reimagined Gothic ended his life in one of the city's great Gothic buildings. Look again at those plain arches and imagine what he saw in them.","new_facts":["Catalan Gothic favoured broad, simple spaces and solid walls with little ornament","The old wards are long halls spanned by pointed stone arches that carry the roof","The former wards are now reading rooms of the National Library of Catalonia","The courtyard has arcades around its edges","Gaudí was brought here after being hit by a tram","Gaudí was deeply influenced by Gothic architecture and developed its ideas with curves and slanting columns in the Sagrada Família"]},"outdoors":{"short":"Step in from El Raval's busy streets and listen as the noise drops away. Notice the orange trees, their dark glossy leaves and, in season, bright fruit. This courtyard belongs to a Gothic hospital founded in 1401. Locals pass through here, so speak softly and keep it peaceful.","normal":"Step in from the busy streets of El Raval and listen. The noise drops away. Notice the orange trees around you, their dark glossy leaves and, depending on the season, bright fruit. This courtyard belongs to a Gothic hospital founded in 1401, which cared for the city's sick for more than five hundred years. Today scholars work inside at the National Library of Catalonia, and locals pass through on their way across the neighbourhood. Look up and watch the light shift over the old stone. Speak softly, and help this green pocket of a real neighbourhood stay peaceful.","long":"Step in from the busy streets of El Raval and listen. The traffic, the voices and the rattle of shop shutters fall away, and what's left is footsteps on stone and birds in the branches. Take a slow breath. Notice the orange trees around you, their dark glossy leaves and, depending on the season, bright fruit or white blossom. In spring, orange blossom has a sweet scent you may catch on the air. See how the trees stand out against the old stone arcades around the edges, green against grey. This courtyard belongs to a Gothic hospital founded in 1401, which cared for the city's sick for more than five hundred years. Antoni Gaudí died here in 1926, brought in after a tram accident. Later the hospital moved to a new site, the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau, and these buildings found a calmer role. Today scholars work inside at the National Library of Catalonia, in long Gothic halls that were once hospital wards. Locals pass through on their way across the neighbourhood, and you might see someone carrying shopping home or reading quietly in the shade. Look up and watch the light shift over the old stone and under the arches. Remember that for the people who live here, this is an everyday shortcut and a breathing space, not just a sight. Speak softly, keep the paths clear, and help this green pocket of a real neighbourhood stay peaceful.","new_facts":["Orange trees produce white, sweet-scented blossom in spring","The courtyard is edged by old stone arcades","Antoni Gaudí died here in 1926 after a tram accident","The hospital moved to the Hospital de la Santa Creu i Sant Pau","The library occupies long Gothic halls that were once hospital wards"]}},"tip":"The courtyard is free and shady: a perfect place to rest your feet."},"refugi-307":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Behind this door, the neighbours of Poble-sec dug their own tunnels by hand during the Spanish Civil War, sheltering from the bombs falling on Barcelona. Families, children and older people did the digging. Look at how modest this doorway is: it hides one of the city's best-preserved shelters, out of more than a thousand.","normal":"Behind this door is a network of tunnels dug by the neighbours of Poble-sec themselves during the Spanish Civil War, to shelter from the bombing raids on Barcelona. Families, children and older people dug by hand, and built benches, a fountain and even a small infirmary inside. It's one of more than a thousand shelters dug across the city, and one of the best preserved.","long":"Behind this door is something you'd never guess from the street: a network of tunnels dug by the neighbours of Poble-sec themselves during the Spanish Civil War. Barcelona was bombed from the air, mostly by Italian aircraft flying in support of Franco, and this neighbourhood sits between the port and the hill of Montjuïc, close to the kind of targets the bombers were after. So the people who lived here didn't wait for someone else to protect them. Families, children and older people dug into the side of Montjuïc by hand, and the tunnels grew to roughly four hundred metres of narrow, vaulted passages. Then they made it a place you could actually wait out a raid: benches along the walls, a fountain for drinking water, and even a small infirmary. Imagine sitting on one of those benches as the sirens sounded overhead, shoulder to shoulder with the same people you passed every day on these streets. Now look at the entrance itself. Notice how ordinary it is, tucked into the slope like any other doorway. That was part of the point: a shelter had to be close by, so people could reach it quickly once the alarm went off. More than a thousand shelters were dug across the city, and this is one of the best preserved. Today it's cared for by the Barcelona History Museum. As you stand here, remember that the buildings around you are people's homes, so keep your voice low and leave the pavement clear.","new_facts":["The air raids on Barcelona were carried out mostly by Italian aircraft supporting Franco's side.","Poble-sec lies between the port and the hill of Montjuïc, and the port area was a target of the bombing.","The shelter is dug into the side of Montjuïc.","The tunnels are roughly four hundred metres long.","The tunnels are narrow, vaulted passages.","Air-raid sirens warned people to go to the shelters.","Refugi 307 is managed by the Barcelona History Museum (MUHBA)."]},"history":{"short":"Look at this modest doorway. Behind it, during the Spanish Civil War, the neighbours of Poble-sec dug a shelter by hand to protect each other from the bombing. Inside they even built a small infirmary. Please pause quietly here: the homes around you still belong to a living neighbourhood.","normal":"Look at this modest doorway. Behind it is a shelter that the neighbours of Poble-sec dug for themselves, by hand. During the Spanish Civil War, Barcelona was bombed from the air, and the people of these streets chose to protect each other. Families, children and older people dug the tunnels together, then built benches, a fountain and even a small infirmary inside. More than a thousand shelters were dug across the city, and this is one of the best preserved. Please pause quietly: the homes around you still belong to a living neighbourhood.","long":"Look at this modest doorway, set into the slope of Montjuïc. Behind it is a shelter that the neighbours of Poble-sec dug for themselves, by hand, during the Spanish Civil War. From 1937 Barcelona was bombed from the air, largely by Italian planes supporting Franco's side, and in March 1938 the city lived through days of especially heavy raids that killed hundreds of people. Poble-sec, close to the port, was exposed. The people of these streets could have waited for help that might never come. Instead, they chose to protect each other. Families, children and older people dug the tunnels together, and over time they stretched to around four hundred metres, with room for roughly two thousand people. They didn't stop at bare rock. They built benches, a fountain and even a small infirmary inside, turning a hole in the hill into somewhere a frightened family could wait and be cared for. More than a thousand shelters were dug across the city, and this is one of the best preserved, now looked after by the Barcelona History Museum. Take a moment to picture these same streets with the sirens sounding, and neighbours hurrying toward this door, carrying children and helping older people along. The shelter is a reminder that, when the bombs fell, ordinary people organised themselves to survive. Please pause quietly here: the homes around you still belong to a living neighbourhood, and the people inside them are not part of the exhibit.","new_facts":["The shelter is set into the slope of Montjuïc.","Barcelona was bombed from the air from 1937.","The air raids were carried out largely by Italian planes supporting Franco's side.","In March 1938 Barcelona suffered days of especially heavy raids that killed hundreds of people.","Poble-sec is close to the port.","The tunnels are around four hundred metres long.","The shelter had room for roughly two thousand people.","Refugi 307 is looked after by the Barcelona History Museum (MUHBA).","Air-raid sirens warned people to go to the shelters."]}},"tip":"Visits are guided and need booking in advance through the City History Museum (MUHBA)."},"drassanes":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"These huge stone halls were Barcelona's royal shipyards, where medieval galleys were built for the Crown of Aragon's fleet. Step back and look at the long rows of Gothic arches: they were made wide and high so whole ships could be built under cover. Today they house the Maritime Museum.","normal":"These huge stone halls were the royal shipyards of Barcelona, where galleys were built for the Crown of Aragon's fleet in the Middle Ages. Step back and look at the long rows of Gothic arches: they were made wide and high so whole ships could be built under cover. Today the shipyards house the Maritime Museum, one of the finest medieval industrial buildings in the world.","long":"These huge stone halls were the royal shipyards of Barcelona, the Drassanes Reials. In the Middle Ages, galleys were built here for the fleet of the Crown of Aragon, and work on the yards began in the late thirteenth century, with major expansions in the century that followed. Step back now and take in the long rows of Gothic arches. They were made wide and high for a very practical reason: so that whole ships could be built under cover, out of the sun and rain. Let your eye travel along them and notice how they repeat, bay after bay, giving the building its calm, steady rhythm. Then look at the sheer weight of the stone. This was a workplace, not a palace or a church, yet it was built to last for centuries, and it has. It's considered one of the finest medieval industrial buildings in the world. It also sat right at the edge of the medieval city, close to the sea, and a stretch of the old city wall still survives alongside the complex. Today the shipyards house the Maritime Museum. Inside, under those same arches, you can see a full-size replica of the Real, the flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571. Standing beside it gives you a real sense of how much space a galley needed, and why these halls are so vast. Before you move on, take one last look along the arches and picture the ships slowly taking shape beneath them.","new_facts":["Work on the shipyards began in the late thirteenth century.","The shipyards were greatly expanded in the fourteenth century.","The shipyards stood at the edge of the medieval city, close to the sea.","A stretch of the old city wall survives alongside the complex.","The Maritime Museum displays a full-size replica of the Real, flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571."]},"history":{"short":"Imagine hammers and saws echoing under these stone roofs. This was Barcelona's medieval royal shipyard, where workers turned timber into galleys for the Crown of Aragon's Mediterranean fleet. Look along the long stone walls in front of you. Today they hold the Maritime Museum. Keep your voice gentle for El Raval's residents.","normal":"Imagine the sound of hammers and saws echoing under these stone roofs. In the Middle Ages, this was the royal shipyard of Barcelona, where galleys were built for the fleet of the Crown of Aragon, a power whose ships sailed across the Mediterranean. Look along the long stone walls in front of you. Behind them, generations of workers turned timber into warships. Today the old yards hold the Maritime Museum. As you stand here, remember that El Raval around you is a living neighbourhood, so keep your voice gentle for the people who call it home.","long":"Imagine the sound of hammers and saws echoing under these stone roofs. In the Middle Ages, this was the royal shipyard of Barcelona, where galleys were built for the fleet of the Crown of Aragon, a power whose ships sailed across the Mediterranean. Work on the yards began in the late thirteenth century, and they were greatly enlarged in the century that followed. Look along the long stone walls in front of you. Behind them, generations of workers turned timber into warships, by hand, one plank at a time. Picture the smell of fresh-cut wood, the shouted instructions, and the slow work of shaping a hull until it was ready for the sea. The shipyard stood at the edge of the medieval city, close to the shore, and a stretch of the old city wall still survives beside the complex. Long after the age of galleys, the old yards found new uses, and today they hold the Maritime Museum. Inside, you can see a full-size replica of the Real, the flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, which helps you picture the kind of ship once born in halls like these. As you stand here, remember that El Raval around you is a living neighbourhood. People live, work and raise families on these streets, so keep your voice gentle and leave room on the pavement for the people who call it home. Then take one last listen, and imagine those hammers again.","new_facts":["Work on the shipyards began in the late thirteenth century.","The shipyards were greatly enlarged in the fourteenth century.","The shipyard stood at the edge of the medieval city, close to the shore.","A stretch of the old city wall survives beside the complex.","The yards had other uses before becoming the Maritime Museum.","The Maritime Museum displays a full-size replica of the Real, flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571."]},"architecture":{"short":"Here's a building designed around ships. Follow the long rows of pointed Gothic arches marching into the distance. They were made wide and high so whole galleys could be built under cover, sheltered from the weather. It's considered one of the finest medieval industrial buildings in the world, now home to the Maritime Museum.","normal":"Here's a building designed around ships. Step back and follow the long rows of pointed Gothic arches as they march into the distance. They were made wide and high on purpose, so that whole galleys could be built under cover, sheltered from the weather. Notice how the arches repeat, bay after bay, dividing the space into long, open halls of stone. It's practical medieval engineering on a grand scale. Today it's considered one of the finest medieval industrial buildings in the world, and it's now home to the Maritime Museum.","long":"Here's a building designed around ships. Step back and follow the long rows of pointed Gothic arches as they march into the distance. They were made wide and high on purpose, so that whole galleys could be built under cover, sheltered from the weather. A galley was a long, low ship, and the halls are shaped to match: long, open and generous overhead. Notice how the arches repeat, bay after bay, dividing the space into long, open halls of stone. That repetition is the secret of the design. Each bay is like a building block, and a design like this lends itself to growth. The complex did grow over time: work began in the late thirteenth century, and the yards were greatly enlarged in the century that followed. Look, too, at how plain it all is. There's little decoration here; the beauty comes from proportion, from stone and from the steady rhythm of the arches. It's practical medieval engineering on a grand scale, and it's considered one of the finest medieval industrial buildings in the world. Today it's home to the Maritime Museum. If you go inside, stand beneath the arches beside the full-size replica of the Real, the flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, and you'll see how comfortably a great galley fits within these walls. Out here, one last thing: let your eyes rest on the long rooflines and imagine them as a kind of stone hangar, the medieval answer to a modern factory.","new_facts":["A galley was a long, low ship.","Work on the shipyards began in the late thirteenth century.","The shipyards were greatly enlarged in the fourteenth century.","The Maritime Museum displays a full-size replica of the Real, flagship of the Holy League fleet at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571."]}},"tip":"Look at the arches from the entrance hall even if you don't visit the museum."},"palau-guell":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Gaudí built this mansion in the late 1880s for his patron, the industrialist Eusebi Güell. Look at the two parabolic entrance arches, wide enough for horse-drawn carriages, with the iron coat of arms of Catalonia between them. Hidden up on the roof, a forest of chimneys wears broken tiles.","normal":"Gaudí built this mansion in the late 1880s for his patron, the industrialist Eusebi Güell, on a narrow street just off the Rambla. Look at the two parabolic arches of the entrance, wide enough for horse-drawn carriages, and the iron coat of arms of Catalonia between them. Up on the roof, out of sight from here, a forest of chimneys covered in broken tiles hints at the playful Gaudí to come.","long":"You're standing on Carrer Nou de la Rambla, a narrow street just off the Rambla, facing one of Antoni Gaudí's early masterpieces. He built this mansion between 1886 and 1888 for his patron, the industrialist Eusebi Güell. Start with the two entrance arches. They're parabolic, a curve that carries weight smoothly down to the ground, and they're wide enough for horse-drawn carriages. Once inside, the carriages went down a ramp to stables in the basement. Now look closely at the iron gates in those arches. Can you spot the letters E and G worked into the metal? Those are Eusebi Güell's initials. Between the arches is the iron coat of arms of Catalonia, a proud sign of identity. Let your eyes travel up the facade. It's calm, almost severe, with straight lines and smooth stone, nothing like the swirling buildings Gaudí would design later. The drama is kept inside and above. The main hall rises to a dome pierced with small holes that let in light like stars. And up on the roof, out of sight from here, a forest of chimneys is covered in broken tiles, a first hint of the playful Gaudí to come. Today the building is part of a UNESCO World Heritage listing for Gaudí's work. People live on this street, so as you take it all in, please keep your voice low and leave room on the pavement.","new_facts":["The street is Carrer Nou de la Rambla","Built between 1886 and 1888","Carriages went down a ramp to stables in the basement","The letters E and G (Eusebi Güell's initials) are worked into the iron entrance gates","The main hall rises to a dome pierced with small holes that let in light like stars","The building is part of the UNESCO World Heritage listing for the works of Antoni Gaudí"]},"architecture":{"short":"Picture a horse-drawn carriage rolling through one of those arches. Gaudí made both entrance arches parabolic, a curve that carries weight down to the ground, and wide enough for carriages. Notice how calm the facade looks; the playful tiled chimneys hide on the roof. Please keep your voice low: people live here.","normal":"Imagine a horse-drawn carriage rolling straight through one of those arches in front of you. Gaudí made both entrance arches parabolic, a curve that carries weight down to the ground, and wide enough for carriages to pass. He designed this mansion in the late 1880s for the industrialist Eusebi Güell, on a narrow street just off the Rambla. Notice how calm and severe the facade looks. The playfulness is hidden: up on the roof, out of sight from here, sits a forest of tiled chimneys. Please keep your voice low, because people live on this street.","long":"Imagine a horse-drawn carriage rolling straight through one of those arches in front of you. Gaudí made both entrance arches parabolic, a curve that carries weight smoothly down to the ground, and wide enough for carriages to pass. Once inside, the carriages went down a ramp to stables in the basement. Down there, sturdy brick columns spread out at the top like mushrooms to hold up the floors above. He designed this mansion between 1886 and 1888 for the industrialist Eusebi Güell, on a narrow street just off the Rambla. Step back as far as the pavement allows and notice how calm and severe the facade looks, with flat stone and strong straight lines. On a street this tight there was no space for a grand approach, so the drama goes inward and upward instead. Inside, the main hall rises high to a dome pierced with small holes, so daylight falls through like stars. And up on the roof, out of sight from here, sits a forest of tiled chimneys, where Gaudí finally let himself play. Look again at the arches and you'll see the same idea that runs through his later work: let the shape of the structure do the work, and make it beautiful at the same time. You're seeing an early Gaudí, still mostly within straight walls, testing ideas he would push much further across the city. Please keep your voice low and leave the doorways clear, because people live on this street.","new_facts":["Built between 1886 and 1888","Carriages went down a ramp to stables in the basement","The basement stables have brick columns that spread out at the top like mushrooms","The main hall rises to a dome pierced with small holes that let in light like stars"]},"art":{"short":"Every great artist needs someone who believes in them, and for Gaudí that was Eusebi Güell, who gave him this mansion to design in the late 1880s. Between the entrance arches, find the coat of arms of Catalonia. Enjoy it quietly: this is someone's neighbourhood.","normal":"Every great artist needs someone who believes in them, and for Antoni Gaudí that person was Eusebi Güell. In the late 1880s the industrialist gave him this mansion to design, just off the Rambla. Between the two entrance arches, find the coat of arms of Catalonia, a proud statement of identity. Out of sight on the roof, chimneys are dressed in broken tiles, a mosaic style Gaudí would later make famous across the city. You're looking at one chapter of a partnership that later gave Barcelona Park Güell. Enjoy it quietly: this is someone's neighbourhood.","long":"Every great artist needs someone who believes in them, and for Antoni Gaudí that person was Eusebi Güell. In the late 1880s, between 1886 and 1888, the industrialist gave the young architect this mansion to design, on a narrow street just off the Rambla. It was a big commission early in his career, and Güell gave him room to experiment. Look at the iron gates in the two entrance arches. Worked into the metal are the letters E and G, Eusebi Güell's initials, so the patron's name greets you at the door. Between the arches, find the coat of arms of Catalonia, a proud statement of identity in iron. Notice how much craft there is in that metalwork, twisting and curling against the plain stone around it. Out of sight on the roof, the chimneys are dressed in broken tiles. This mosaic style, called trencadís in Catalan, is one Gaudí would later make famous across the city, from benches to rooftops. You're looking at one chapter of a partnership that lasted for decades. Güell went on to commission Park Güell, up on the hill above the city, and a church crypt for the workers' village he built outside Barcelona, the Colònia Güell. Today this mansion is part of a UNESCO World Heritage listing for Gaudí's work. Think of it as Güell's bet on a young talent. And enjoy it quietly, because this is someone's neighbourhood.","new_facts":["Built between 1886 and 1888","The letters E and G (Eusebi Güell's initials) are worked into the iron entrance gates","The broken-tile mosaic style is called trencadís in Catalan","The partnership between Gaudí and Güell lasted for decades","Park Güell is on a hill above the city","Güell commissioned Gaudí to design a church crypt for Colònia Güell, Güell's workers' village outside Barcelona","The building is part of the UNESCO World Heritage listing for the works of Antoni Gaudí"]}},"tip":"Look up at the wrought ironwork over the doors: it's one of Gaudí's earliest masterpieces in iron."},"casa-vicens":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This colourful house was Antoni Gaudí's first important building, a summer home from the 1880s when Gràcia was a separate town among fields. Look at the green and white tiles and find the little yellow flowers: they're marigolds, inspired by the ones that grew right here on the plot.","normal":"This colourful house was Antoni Gaudí's first important building, designed in the 1880s as a summer home when Gràcia was still a separate town surrounded by fields. Notice the green and white tiles decorated with small yellow flowers: Gaudí was inspired by the marigolds growing on the plot. The mix of brick, tiles and Moorish details shows a young architect already breaking the rules.","long":"Stand back for a moment and take in the whole house. This was Antoni Gaudí's first important building, designed in the 1880s as a summer home for Manuel Vicens, a stockbroker, and finished in 1885. Back then Gràcia was still a separate town, surrounded by fields, and this plot was a country retreat away from the crowded city. Now let your eyes settle on the walls. You'll see bands of green and white tiles, and if you look closely, small yellow flowers painted on them. They're marigolds. Gaudí found them growing on the plot and turned them into decoration, so the flowers that once grew here still bloom on the house. Next, notice how the tiles sit alongside plain brick. Instead of hiding everything behind smooth stone, he let the materials show and stacked them in stripes, which gives the façade its lively rhythm. Look up toward the top of the house and you'll spot Moorish details, small turrets and decorative shapes that borrow from the Islamic architecture of Spain's past. Then glance at the iron railings: the fence is decorated with fan-shaped palm leaves, another piece of nature brought into the design. All of this shows a young architect already breaking the rules, years before his more famous works in the city. Today the house is a museum, and since 2005 it has been part of the UNESCO World Heritage listing of Gaudí's works. The fields are long gone, and this is now a busy residential neighbourhood, so please keep the pavement clear for the people who live here.","new_facts":["The house was built for Manuel Vicens, a stockbroker","The house was finished in 1885","There are small turrets along the top of the house","The iron fence is decorated with fan-shaped palm leaves","The house is now a museum","Since 2005 it has been part of the UNESCO World Heritage listing of Gaudí's works"]},"architecture":{"short":"Picture this street as open fields. In the 1880s, when Gràcia was a separate town, Gaudí designed this summer home, his first important building. Notice how brick and bands of green and white tiles are stacked together, then look up at the Moorish-inspired details along the top.","normal":"Picture this street as open fields. When Antoni Gaudí designed this house in the 1880s, Gràcia was still a separate town, and this was a summer home in the countryside. It was his first important building. Notice how he refused to hide behind plain stone: brick and bands of green and white tiles are stacked together, giving the walls rhythm. Now look up and spot the Moorish-inspired details along the top. Mixing materials and styles like this was daring for a young architect. Today the countryside is gone, replaced by a lived-in neighbourhood.","long":"Picture this street as open fields. When Antoni Gaudí designed this house in the 1880s, Gràcia was still a separate town, and this was a summer home in the countryside, built for the stockbroker Manuel Vicens and completed in 1885. It was Gaudí's first important building, and he was only in his early thirties. Start low and work your way up. Notice how he refused to hide behind plain stone. Brick and bands of green and white tiles are stacked together in horizontal stripes, and that repetition gives the walls a steady rhythm, almost like a pattern on fabric. Now look higher. Along the top you'll see Moorish-inspired details, including small turrets and decorative shapes that echo the Islamic buildings of southern Spain. Gaudí was drawing on a style many architects of his day found exotic, and blending it with local brick and colourful ceramic tiles. Look at the railings too: the iron fence is shaped like fan palm leaves, so even a practical part becomes decoration. Mixing materials and styles like this was daring for a young architect, and you can see early hints of the freedom he'd later bring to buildings like the Sagrada Família. The house was extended in the twentieth century, and today it's a museum. The countryside is gone, replaced by a lived-in neighbourhood, so enjoy the view quietly and leave room on the pavement for residents.","new_facts":["The house was built for the stockbroker Manuel Vicens","The house was completed in 1885","Gaudí was in his early thirties when he designed it","There are small turrets along the top of the house","The iron fence is shaped like fan palm leaves","Gaudí later designed the Sagrada Família","The house was extended in the twentieth century","The house is now a museum"]},"art":{"short":"Look closely at the green and white tiles and you'll find tiny yellow flowers. They're marigolds, which were growing on this plot when Gaudí designed the summer home in the 1880s. He turned them into decoration, so the plants that once grew here live on in the walls.","normal":"Look closely at the tiles and you'll find tiny yellow flowers. They're marigolds. When Antoni Gaudí came to design this summer home in the 1880s, marigolds were growing on the plot, and he turned them into decoration, repeating them across the green and white tiles. It's a lovely idea: the plants that once grew here live on in the walls. Notice too the Moorish touches, a sign of how freely he borrowed from other traditions. This was Gaudí's first important building, and already his love of nature shines through.","long":"Look closely at the tiles and you'll find tiny yellow flowers. They're marigolds. When Antoni Gaudí came to design this summer home in the 1880s, marigolds were growing on the plot, and he turned them into decoration, repeating them across the green and white tiles. Take a moment to follow the pattern with your eyes. The same small flower appears again and again, so a single plant from the garden becomes a rhythm running across the walls. It's a lovely idea: the plants that once grew here live on in the walls. Now look at the iron fence. Its railings are shaped like fan palm leaves, another plant turned into ornament. Gaudí seems to have looked at what surrounded him and asked how it could become art. Notice too the Moorish touches higher up, a sign of how freely he borrowed from other traditions. Patterns like these recall the tilework of Spain's Islamic past, but here they sit beside everyday brick, and the result feels playful rather than grand. This was Gaudí's first important building, finished in 1885 for a stockbroker named Manuel Vicens, and already his love of nature shines through. That idea stayed with him for the rest of his career, in the curving forms and natural shapes of his later work around the city. If you visit the museum inside, you'll find much more nature-inspired decoration. From out here, though, the tiles say plenty. And as you admire them, remember that people live on this street, so keep your voice low and the pavement clear.","new_facts":["The iron fence railings are shaped like fan palm leaves","The house was finished in 1885","It was built for a stockbroker named Manuel Vicens","Natural forms remained central to Gaudí's later work in Barcelona","The house is now a museum with nature-inspired decoration inside"]}},"tip":"It's on a narrow residential street: admire it from the pavement without blocking neighbours' doors."},"casa-punxes":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Pointed towers, red brick, a fairy-tale castle on Avinguda Diagonal: locals call it the House of Spikes. Josep Puig i Cadafalch designed it in the early 1900s as flats for three sisters. Look up at the façade for the ceramic panel of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint.","normal":"With its pointed towers, this building looks like a castle from a northern European fairy tale dropped onto Avinguda Diagonal. Locals call it the House of Spikes. It was designed in the early 1900s by Josep Puig i Cadafalch, one of the great architects of Catalan Modernisme, as flats for three sisters. Look for the ceramic panels on the façade, including one of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint.","long":"Take a moment to look up at those pointed towers. With their sharp spires, this building looks like a castle from a northern European fairy tale that somehow landed on Avinguda Diagonal, one of Barcelona's busiest avenues. Locals call it the Casa de les Punxes, the House of Spikes, though its official name is Casa Terrades, after the family who commissioned it. Count the towers as you run your eyes along the roofline. There are six of them, each topped with a cone-shaped spike. It was designed in the early 1900s by Josep Puig i Cadafalch, one of the great architects of Catalan Modernisme, the same man behind Casa Amatller on Passeig de Gràcia. The clients were three sisters, and the building was planned as flats for them. Yet what you see reads as a single castle, wrapped in warm red brick, with carved stone around the windows and balconies. Now let your eyes drift across the façade and look for the ceramic panels set into the walls. The most famous shows Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, the knight who slays the dragon. Its inscription asks the saint to give Catalonia back its freedom. It's a reminder that this building was also a statement of Catalan identity at a time when that mattered deeply here. Notice how the Gothic-style towers sit beside these colourful tiles: old forms, playfully reinvented. People still live and work around here, so enjoy the view quietly before you move on.","new_facts":["The building's official name is Casa Terrades, after the family who commissioned it","The local name is Casa de les Punxes","Avinguda Diagonal is one of Barcelona's busiest avenues","The building has six towers, each topped with a conical spike","The façade is red brick with carved stone around the windows and balconies","Puig i Cadafalch also designed Casa Amatller on Passeig de Gràcia","Sant Jordi is depicted as the knight who slays the dragon","The Sant Jordi panel's inscription asks the saint to give Catalonia back its freedom"]},"architecture":{"short":"This building refuses to sit square. Avinguda Diagonal slices across the Eixample grid, leaving a wedge-shaped plot, and Josep Puig i Cadafalch turned it into a red-brick castle with pointed towers. Look for the ceramic panel of Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint. It's a real neighbourhood, so please keep voices low.","normal":"Notice how this building refuses to sit square. The Diagonal slices across the Eixample's neat grid, leaving an awkward wedge-shaped plot, and Josep Puig i Cadafalch turned that problem into a castle. Designed in the early 1900s, it held flats for three sisters, yet he wrapped them in one continuous façade of red brick, crowned by pointed towers that borrow from northern European Gothic. Look closely at the ceramic panels set into the walls, among them Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint. This is Catalan Modernisme: old forms, reinvented. This is a real neighbourhood, so please keep voices low.","long":"Stand back for a moment and notice how this building refuses to sit square. Most of the Eixample follows a neat grid of blocks with cut-off corners. But Avinguda Diagonal slices straight across that grid, and it leaves awkward wedge-shaped plots like this one, bounded here by three streets: the Diagonal, Carrer del Rosselló and Carrer del Bruc. Josep Puig i Cadafalch, designing in the early 1900s, turned that problem into a castle. The building held flats for three sisters, three separate homes. Yet he wrapped them in one continuous façade of red brick, so your eye reads a single fortress rather than three houses. Follow the brick up to the roofline. The pointed towers borrow from northern European Gothic, and their spikes give the building its nickname, the House of Spikes. Notice how the corners are rounded into towers, softening the sharp angles of the plot. Then bring your eyes down to the details: carved stone around the windows, wrought-iron balconies, and the ceramic panels set into the walls. Among them is Sant Jordi, Catalonia's patron saint, shown as the dragon-slaying knight. This mix is pure Catalan Modernisme. The movement looked back to medieval forms and craft, then reinvented them with colour and fantasy. Puig i Cadafalch was also a historian of medieval architecture, which helps explain his love of towers and Gothic detail. This is a real neighbourhood, so please keep voices low as you look.","new_facts":["Eixample blocks have cut-off (chamfered) corners","The plot is bounded by Avinguda Diagonal, Carrer del Rosselló and Carrer del Bruc","The sisters' flats were three separate homes unified behind one façade","The building's nickname is the House of Spikes","The corners of the building are rounded into towers","The façade has carved stone around the windows and wrought-iron balconies","Sant Jordi is depicted as the dragon-slaying knight","Catalan Modernisme drew on medieval forms and craft","Puig i Cadafalch was also a historian of medieval architecture"]}},"tip":"Cross to the other side of the Diagonal for the best view of all six towers."},"fundacio-tapies":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Look up at the roof. That tangle of metal wire is Cloud and Chair, a sculpture by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Beneath it is a red-brick building by the Modernista architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner, once a publishing house. Old and new art, stacked right before you.","normal":"Look up at the roof: that tangle of metal wire is a sculpture called Cloud and Chair, by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important 20th-century artists. Underneath it stands a red-brick building by the Modernista architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner, originally a publishing house. Old and new art meet here: a 19th-century building crowned by a modern artwork.","long":"Look up at the roof. That tangle of metal wire floating against the sky is a sculpture called Cloud and Chair, by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Tàpies was born here in Barcelona in 1923, and he set up this foundation to share his work with the public. The sculpture went up on the roof in 1990, when the foundation opened its doors. Now bring your eyes down to the building it sits on. This red-brick façade was designed by the Modernista architect Lluís Domènech i Montaner, the same architect who later created the Palau de la Música Catalana. It was built in the 1880s as the home of the Montaner i Simon publishing house, a firm with ties to the architect's family. Notice how the brick is left bare rather than hidden under stone or paint, and how it is combined with iron, a bold choice at the time. Historians see it as one of the early buildings of the Modernista movement in Barcelona. So in one glance you are taking in two very different moments: a nineteenth-century workplace made for printing books, and a late twentieth-century artwork that turns the roofline into a kind of drawing in the air. Old and new art meet here, one on top of the other. Take your time looking, but keep to the edge of the pavement. This is an ordinary Eixample street, and the people walking past you live and work right here.","new_facts":["Antoni Tàpies was born in Barcelona in 1923.","Cloud and Chair was installed on the roof in 1990, when the foundation opened.","Domènech i Montaner later designed the Palau de la Música Catalana.","The building was built in the 1880s.","It housed the Montaner i Simon publishing house, a firm with ties to the architect's family.","The façade combines exposed brick with iron.","It is considered one of the early buildings of Barcelona's Modernista movement."]},"art":{"short":"Look up above the rooftop at that tangle of metal wire: Cloud and Chair, by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Can you find the chair hiding inside the cloud? Tàpies loved turning humble, everyday things into art. Enjoy it quietly, because neighbours live all around you.","normal":"Look up above the rooftop. That tangle of metal wire floating there is a sculpture called Cloud and Chair, by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Can you find the chair hiding inside the cloud? Tàpies loved turning humble, everyday things into art, and here a simple chair drifts in a scribble of sky. He set up this foundation to share his work, and his sculpture now crowns a nineteenth-century building. Take a quiet moment on the pavement. This is a lived-in Eixample street, where neighbours are heading home all around you.","long":"Look up above the rooftop. That tangle of metal wire floating there is a sculpture called Cloud and Chair, by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Can you find the chair hiding inside the cloud? Take your time. Once you spot its legs and its seat, the whole scribble starts to make sense: a simple chair drifting through a knot of sky. Tàpies loved turning humble, everyday things into art. In his paintings he worked with materials like sand, varnish and marble dust, and he often brought in ordinary objects, scratches and marks, so that his canvases feel almost like old walls. He was born in Barcelona in 1923 and became internationally known, yet he kept returning to the plain things of daily life. Here he took that idea outdoors and gave it a giant scale. He set up this foundation to share his work, and it opened to the public in 1990, the same year the sculpture was placed on the roof. Notice how the wire changes as the light moves across it, sometimes sharp against the sky, sometimes almost disappearing. It crowns a nineteenth-century building, so a modern drawing in metal now sits on top of an old brick façade. Take a quiet moment on the pavement and let your eyes wander along the lines. This is a lived-in Eixample street, where neighbours are heading home all around you, so stay out of their way while you look.","new_facts":["Tàpies used materials such as sand, varnish and marble dust, along with ordinary objects, scratches and marks, in his paintings.","Antoni Tàpies was born in Barcelona in 1923.","Tàpies became internationally known.","The foundation opened to the public in 1990.","Cloud and Chair was placed on the roof in 1990.","The building has a brick façade."]},"architecture":{"short":"Notice the red brick in front of you. Many Eixample buildings wear stone or render, but this façade shows its bare brick. Lluís Domènech i Montaner, later famous for the Palau de la Música Catalana, designed it as a publishing house. Now look up: modern metal wire crowns the roofline.","normal":"Notice the red brick in front of you. Many Eixample buildings wear stone or painted render, but this façade proudly shows off its bare brick. It was designed by Lluís Domènech i Montaner, the Modernista architect later famous for the Palau de la Música Catalana. It began life as a publishing house, a working building made for producing books. Now let your eyes climb to the top, where a cloud of modern metal wire crowns the old roofline. Pause here while leaving space for passers-by, because people live and work all along this street.","long":"Notice the red brick in front of you. Many Eixample buildings wear stone or painted render, but this façade proudly shows off its bare brick. It was designed by Lluís Domènech i Montaner, the Modernista architect later famous for the Palau de la Música Catalana and the Hospital de Sant Pau. It went up in the 1880s, which makes it one of the earliest Modernista buildings in the city, from a time when the movement was only just finding its voice. It began life as a publishing house, a working building made for producing books, for the firm Montaner i Simon, which had ties to the architect's own family. Look at how the brick is laid and patterned, and how it works together with iron, a material Domènech i Montaner used openly here instead of hiding it. That honesty about materials was a bold idea in its day. Notice how a practical workplace for books was still given real care and craftsmanship. Today it houses the foundation set up by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists. Now let your eyes climb to the top, where a cloud of modern metal wire, his sculpture Cloud and Chair, crowns the old roofline. A nineteenth-century workplace and a twentieth-century artwork share one silhouette. Pause here while leaving space for passers-by, because people live and work all along this street.","new_facts":["Domènech i Montaner also designed the Hospital de Sant Pau.","The building went up in the 1880s.","It is one of the earliest Modernista buildings in Barcelona.","It housed the Montaner i Simon publishing house, a firm with ties to the architect's family.","The façade uses iron openly alongside the brick.","The building now houses the foundation set up by Antoni Tàpies, one of Catalonia's most important twentieth-century artists.","The rooftop sculpture is Cloud and Chair by Tàpies."]}},"tip":"Even without entering, the façade and the rooftop sculpture are worth a pause."},"cccb":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This cultural centre lives inside the old Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house. Stand in the courtyard and look up at the glass wall leaning forward. It was added when architects cut away one side of the courtyard, and now it reflects the Raval's rooftops and the hills behind the city.","normal":"This cultural centre lives inside the old Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house. When it was renovated, the architects cut away one side of the courtyard and added a tall glass wall that leans forward. Stand in the courtyard and look up: the glass reflects the rooftops of the Raval and the hills behind the city. It hosts exhibitions, talks and festivals all year round.","long":"You're standing in the courtyard of the CCCB, Barcelona's Centre for Contemporary Culture. These walls once belonged to the Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house that sheltered the city's poor. Look around at the stone, the long rows of windows, the sober lines. Then find the one side that breaks the pattern. When the building was renovated, the architects, Albert Viaplana and Helio Piñón, cut that side of the courtyard away and put up a tall glass wall instead. The cultural centre opened here in 1994. Notice that the wall doesn't stand straight. Near the top it leans forward, out over the courtyard, as if it's bending down to look at you. Tilt your head back and follow it up. Because of that lean, the glass works like a giant mirror, showing you the rooftops of the Raval and the hills behind the city, views you can't see directly from down here. Stay with it for a moment. A passing cloud or a shift in the sun, and the whole picture changes. On a grey day it turns soft and silvery; in bright light the reflection sharpens. That's the quiet trick of this place: old charity house and new glass side by side, each making the other easier to see. The centre hosts exhibitions, talks and festivals all year round, so it's worth checking what's on before you move on. And just around the corner is MACBA, the Museum of Contemporary Art, with its bright white building and a square that skateboarders love.","new_facts":["CCCB stands for Centre de Cultura Contemporània de Barcelona (Centre for Contemporary Culture)","The Casa de la Caritat sheltered the city's poor","The renovation architects were Albert Viaplana and Helio Piñón","The cultural centre opened in 1994","MACBA, the Museum of Contemporary Art, is just around the corner, in a white building","The square in front of MACBA is popular with skateboarders"]},"art":{"short":"Look up at that glass wall leaning out over the courtyard. It's an artwork in itself. Architects cut away one side of the old Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house, to set it here. Watch it catch the Raval's rooftops and the hills, like a canvas changing with the light.","normal":"Look up at that glass wall leaning out over the courtyard, and you are looking at a work of art in its own right. When the old Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house, was turned into this cultural centre, the architects cut away one side of the courtyard and set this glass in its place. Watch how it catches the rooftops of the Raval and the hills beyond, like a canvas that changes with the light. Inside, exhibitions, talks and festivals fill the year, so the old stone and new glass around you are always framing something new.","long":"Look up at that glass wall leaning out over the courtyard. You're looking at a work of art in its own right, and you're standing inside the frame. When the old Casa de la Caritat, a former charity house, was turned into this cultural centre, the architects Albert Viaplana and Helio Piñón cut away one side of the courtyard and set this glass in its place. Start at the bottom, where the wall rises straight and clear. Now let your eyes climb. Near the top, the glass tips forward, out over your head, and that tilt is the whole idea. It turns the wall into a mirror angled toward the city, catching the rooftops of the Raval and the hills beyond. Think of it as a canvas that never holds still. Each passing cloud repaints it. Morning light, evening light and a grey afternoon all give you a different picture, and nobody has to touch a brush. Now look at the old stone sides around you, with their steady rows of windows. They're the frame, solid and calm, and the glass is the picture: weightless, modern, always moving. Notice how each makes the other look sharper. The building reopened as a cultural centre in 1994, and since then exhibitions, talks and festivals have filled the year, so the stone and glass are always framing something new. When you leave, glance toward MACBA, the Museum of Contemporary Art just around the corner. Its bright white walls offer a very different answer to the same question: how do you build for art in an old neighbourhood?","new_facts":["The renovation architects were Albert Viaplana and Helio Piñón","The cultural centre opened in 1994","MACBA, the Museum of Contemporary Art, is just around the corner, in a white building"]},"local":{"short":"Before you look at anything, listen. This courtyard sits in the middle of the Raval, one of the city's most lived-in neighbourhoods. Look up at the leaning glass wall. It reflects the rooftops of homes where people are getting on with their day. Enjoy it the local way: unhurried, with your voice low.","normal":"Before you look at anything, listen. Behind these walls is the Raval, one of the city's most mixed and lived-in neighbourhoods, and this courtyard sits right in the middle of it. Now look up at the tall glass wall leaning over you: it reflects the rooftops of homes where people are simply getting on with their day. This building was once the Casa de la Caritat, a charity house, and today it's a cultural centre that neighbours drop into for talks and festivals through the year. Enjoy it the local way: unhurried, and with your voice low.","long":"Before you look at anything, stop and listen for a moment. You might hear footsteps, a bike bell, voices in more than one language. Behind these walls is the Raval, one of the city's most mixed and lived-in neighbourhoods, and this courtyard sits right in the middle of it. This building was once the Casa de la Caritat, a charity house that sheltered the city's poor. Today it's the CCCB, a cultural centre that opened in 1994, and the courtyard you're standing in is called the Pati de les Dones. Now look up at the tall glass wall leaning over you. When the building was renovated, the architects cut away one side of the old courtyard and put the glass there instead. Near the top it tips forward like a mirror, and what it reflects isn't a postcard view. It's the rooftops and terraces of homes where people are simply getting on with their day. Hold on to that thought. This is their neighbourhood, and the centre is part of it too. Neighbours drop in here for talks and festivals through the year, and on a warm evening the courtyard can feel more like a shared living room than a museum. So enjoy it the local way: unhurried, and with your voice low. If you take photos, point your camera at the glass rather than into people's windows. And as you head off through the narrow streets, keep the pace you'd want visitors to keep outside your own front door.","new_facts":["The Casa de la Caritat sheltered the city's poor","The cultural centre opened in 1994","The courtyard is called the Pati de les Dones"]}},"tip":"The courtyard is free to enter and often has outdoor events in summer."},"rambla-raval":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This wide avenue is younger than it looks. Around the year 2000, several crowded blocks of the Raval were knocked down to let in light and air. Now look toward one end and find the huge bronze cat by the Colombian artist Fernando Botero. It's a firm favourite with local children.","normal":"This wide avenue is much younger than it looks: it was opened around the year 2000 by knocking down several crowded blocks of the Raval to bring in light and air. At one end sits a huge bronze cat by the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, a favourite with local children. Around it, shops and cafés from dozens of countries show how international this neighbourhood has become.","long":"Stand for a moment and take in the width of this avenue, the open sky, the rows of trees. It feels settled, as if it has always been here, but it's much younger than it looks. Around the year 2000, the city knocked down several crowded blocks of the Raval to open up this space and bring in light and air. Picture the narrow streets that still surround you, packed tight with tall buildings, and you'll understand how big a change this was for the people who live here. Now let your eyes travel to one end of the avenue, where a huge bronze cat stands. It's the work of the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, who was born in Medellín and became famous for figures that swell with round, generous volume. Look at the cat's plump body and its long tail. The cat didn't start out here; it moved around the city several times before finding its home on this avenue. Today it's a favourite with local children, who love to play around it, so give them plenty of room. As you walk back along the avenue, look at the shops and cafés lining both sides. You'll find signs and smells from dozens of countries, in small grocery stores and restaurants. Together they show how international this neighbourhood has become. And remember that people live in the flats above, so keep your voice down and enjoy the street as a guest.","new_facts":["Fernando Botero was born in Medellín.","The Botero cat was moved around the city several times before settling on the Rambla del Raval."]},"local":{"short":"Here's a surprise: this broad avenue is younger than many of the people strolling along it. Around the year 2000, several crowded blocks were knocked down to let in light and air. Look at the shopfronts from dozens of countries. And keep your voice low; people live in the flats above you.","normal":"Here's a surprise: this broad avenue is younger than many of the people strolling along it. Around the year 2000, several crowded blocks of the Raval were knocked down to let in light and air, and the neighbours made the new space their own. Look along the benches and café tables, and notice the shopfronts from dozens of countries, a sign of how international this neighbourhood has become. Down at one end, the big bronze cat is a favourite with local children. Remember that people live in the flats above you, so keep your voice low as you wander.","long":"Here's a surprise: this broad avenue is younger than many of the people strolling along it. Around the year 2000, several crowded blocks of the Raval were knocked down to let in light and air. Look at the narrow streets running off to either side and imagine the whole area packed that tightly. Clearing these blocks was a big change for the people who lived here, and over the years the neighbours have made the new space their own. Take a moment to watch how it's used. Look along the benches, where people sit and talk, and at the café tables spilling out onto the pavement. This is a living room for the neighbourhood as much as a street. Then turn to the shopfronts. You'll see signs and goods from dozens of countries, a clear mark of how international the Raval has become. Down at one end you'll find the big bronze cat. It's by the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, and its plump, rounded shape makes it easy to love. It's a favourite with local children, so if they're playing there, let them have it first and wait for your photo. Before you move on, look up at the balconies and windows above the shops. Those are people's homes. Keep your voice low as you wander, and treat the avenue the way you'd want visitors to treat your own street.","new_facts":[]},"art":{"short":"Find the enormous bronze cat at one end of this avenue. It's by the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, famous for swelling, rounded figures. Notice how its plump body turns an everyday animal into something playful and monumental. If neighbourhood children are playing around it, step back and give them room.","normal":"Find the enormous bronze cat at one end of this avenue. It's the work of the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, famous for figures that swell with generous, rounded volume. Notice how the cat's plump body turns an everyday animal into something playful and monumental. Its setting is young too: this avenue was opened around the year 2000, when several crowded blocks were knocked down to bring in light and air. Children from the neighbourhood love this cat, so if they're playing around it, step back and give them room.","long":"Find the enormous bronze cat at one end of this avenue and walk slowly all the way around it before you take a photo. It's the work of the Colombian artist Fernando Botero, who was born in Medellín and worked as both a painter and a sculptor. He's famous for figures that swell with generous, rounded volume, a style so recognisable that people often call it Boterismo. His people, animals and even fruit all seem gently inflated, and you'll find his large sculptures in public spaces in many cities around the world. Look first at the cat's plump body. Botero isn't making fun of the animal; he's using that swollen volume to give it weight and presence. Notice how the smooth curves catch the light, and how the face seems small against so much mass. That contrast is what turns an everyday animal into something playful and monumental at the same time. The cat didn't begin its life here. It moved around the city several times before settling on this avenue, and the setting suits it, because the avenue is young too. It was opened around the year 2000, when several crowded blocks were knocked down to bring in light and air. Stand back and see how the cat holds its own in all that open space. Children from the neighbourhood love this cat, and that may be the best way to see it: as something to play beside, not just to admire. So if they're playing around it, step back and give them room.","new_facts":["Fernando Botero was born in Medellín.","Botero worked as both a painter and a sculptor.","His style is often called Boterismo.","His large sculptures stand in public spaces in many cities around the world.","The Botero cat was moved around the city several times before settling on the Rambla del Raval."]}},"tip":"This is a real neighbourhood high street: support the small local shops."},"cap-barcelona":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This giant face by American pop artist Roy Lichtenstein was made for the 1992 Olympic Games. Look closely: it's covered in broken ceramic, the trencadís technique Gaudí made famous, but the pieces are arranged like the dots of a comic strip. Pop art and Catalan Modernisme, meeting by the old port.","normal":"This giant face by the American pop artist Roy Lichtenstein was made for the 1992 Olympic Games. Look closely: it's covered in pieces of broken ceramic, the trencadís technique Gaudí made famous, but arranged like the dots of a comic strip. It's a playful meeting between American pop art and Catalan Modernisme, standing by the old port.","long":"Stop for a moment and take in this giant face. It's the work of Roy Lichtenstein, the American pop artist, and it was made for the 1992 Olympic Games. Lichtenstein became famous for paintings that look like enlarged frames from comic books, with thick black outlines, flat bright colours and rows of little printed dots. Those dots are called Ben-Day dots, a cheap printing trick from old newspapers and comics, and he turned them into his signature. Now walk a little closer. Instead of paint, the face is covered in pieces of broken ceramic. This is trencadís, the technique Gaudí made famous, the same glittering patchwork you may have seen on the benches of Park Güell. Lichtenstein worked on the piece with the ceramicist Diego Delas, and together they set the shards out so they read like those comic-strip dots. From a distance it looks printed; up close it's handmade and slightly uneven, every fragment catching the light in its own way. Follow the bold outlines around the face and notice how simple, flat shapes are enough to suggest a whole personality. So what you're looking at is a meeting of two worlds: American pop art, born from advertising and comics, and Catalan Modernisme, born from craft and nature. It stands right by the old port, at the bottom of Via Laietana, greeting everyone who walks between the city and the water.","new_facts":["Lichtenstein's dots are Ben-Day dots, a technique from commercial printing of newspapers and comics","Trencadís covers the benches of Park Güell","Lichtenstein made the sculpture with the ceramicist Diego Delas","The sculpture stands at the bottom of Via Laietana"]},"art":{"short":"Doesn't this huge face look like it leapt out of a comic book? Roy Lichtenstein, the American pop artist, made it for the 1992 Olympic Games, swapping paint for broken ceramic, the trencadís Gaudí made famous. Look closely and you'll see the shards laid out like comic-strip dots.","normal":"Notice how this huge face looks like it leapt straight out of a comic book. It's the work of Roy Lichtenstein, the American pop artist famous for painting cartoon-style images with bold outlines and printed dots. Here he swapped paint for something very Catalan: broken pieces of ceramic, the trencadís technique Gaudí made famous. Look closely and you'll see the shards set out like the dots of a comic strip. Made for the 1992 Olympic Games, it's a playful conversation between American pop art and Catalan Modernisme, right beside the old port.","long":"Notice how this huge face looks like it leapt straight out of a comic book. It's the work of Roy Lichtenstein, the American pop artist famous for painting cartoon-style images with bold outlines and printed dots. In his best-known paintings from the 1960s, he copied single frames from romance and war comics and blew them up to the size of a canvas, so that something cheap and throwaway suddenly hung in a gallery. The dots were Ben-Day dots, the tiny coloured circles that commercial printers used to make shading on newsprint, and he recreated them in paint. Here he swapped paint for something very Catalan: broken pieces of ceramic, the trencadís technique Gaudí made famous. It's the same broken-tile mosaic that wraps the curving bench at Park Güell. He made the piece with the ceramicist Diego Delas. Now look closely and you'll see the shards set out like the dots of a comic strip. Step back and the eye fuses them into shading, just as it does when you read a printed page. Watch how the outlines do most of the work: a few confident lines are enough to give the face a mood. Made for the 1992 Olympic Games, it's a playful conversation between American pop art and Catalan Modernisme, between mass printing and old craft, right beside the old port. Before you move on, pick one single tile and follow it outward, and see how quickly it disappears into the larger picture.","new_facts":["Lichtenstein's best-known comic-based paintings date from the 1960s","He copied frames from romance and war comics","Ben-Day dots were used by commercial printers to create shading","Trencadís covers the curving bench at Park Güell","Lichtenstein made the sculpture with the ceramicist Diego Delas"]},"outdoors":{"short":"Feel the sea breeze? You're at the edge of the old port, beside a giant ceramic face by Roy Lichtenstein, made for the 1992 Olympic Games. Watch the sunlight on its broken tiles: on a bright day they almost glitter. Pause gently; people live all around here.","normal":"Can you feel the sea breeze? You're standing at the edge of the old port, and this giant ceramic face shares the waterside with you. Look at how the sunlight plays across its broken tiles: on a bright day the colours almost glitter, and as the light softens towards evening the whole face seems to change mood. Made by Roy Lichtenstein for the 1992 Olympic Games, it suits this open waterfront perfectly. Find a quiet spot nearby, watch the boats and the gulls, and remember that all around you are neighbourhoods where people live, so keep your pause a gentle one.","long":"Can you feel the sea breeze? You're standing at the edge of the old port, Port Vell, and this giant ceramic face shares the waterside with you. It was made by Roy Lichtenstein, the American pop artist, for the 1992 Olympic Games, and it suits this open waterfront perfectly. Take a moment to look at how the sunlight plays across its broken tiles. This is trencadís, the technique Gaudí made famous, and because every shard sits at a slightly different angle, each one catches the light in its own way. On a bright day the colours almost glitter, and as the light softens towards evening the whole face seems to change mood. If clouds roll in off the sea, watch how the tiles go quiet and the bold outlines take over. Now turn your body slowly. Behind the face, the city climbs away from the water; in front of you, the old port opens out, with boats tied up along the quays and gulls riding the wind above them. Further along the waterfront rises the Columbus monument, at the bottom of La Rambla. Breathe in the salt air and listen: rigging tapping against masts, the cry of the gulls, traffic softened by the open space. Look back at the face and notice how its bright, comic-book colours answer the blues and greys of the sea and sky. Find a quiet spot nearby, watch the boats and the gulls, and remember that all around you are neighbourhoods where people live, so keep your pause a gentle one.","new_facts":["The Columbus monument stands at the bottom of La Rambla, along the waterfront"]}},"tip":"Walk a few steps back towards the sea to see the whole face against the sky."},"placa-vila-gracia":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This square was the heart of Gràcia when it was still its own town, before joining Barcelona in 1897. Look up at the slender clock tower, built in the 1860s and now the neighbourhood's symbol. In August, during the Festa Major, giant figures dance right here.","normal":"This square was the heart of Gràcia when it was still an independent town, before it joined Barcelona in 1897. Look up at the slender clock tower: it was built in the 1860s and has become the symbol of the neighbourhood. Opposite stands Gràcia's district hall. During the Festa Major in August, giant figures dance here and the whole square fills with music.","long":"You are standing in what was once the heart of a whole town. Before 1897, Gràcia was not part of Barcelona at all. It was an independent town with its own council, and this square was where its public life came together. Look around and you can still feel that it is the centre of something. Now look up at the slender clock tower rising from the square. It was built in the 1860s, while Gràcia still governed itself, and it was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, the same man whose plan for Barcelona's grand expansion won a city competition, only to be set aside for Ildefons Cerdà's. Notice how tall and narrow the tower is, so you can spot it from almost every corner. Over time it has become the symbol of the neighbourhood. Opposite stands Gràcia's district hall. The building was designed by Francesc Berenguer, a close collaborator of Antoni Gaudí, and it is still where the district is run, so the square keeps the civic role it had when Gràcia was its own town. In August, during the Festa Major, giant figures dance here and the whole square fills with music. That same week, residents decorate their own streets, and neighbours compete to create the most imaginative ones. Whatever the season, remember that people live in the flats all around you. Find a spot, keep your voice low, and enjoy the square the way they do.","new_facts":["The clock tower was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","Rovira i Trias's plan for Barcelona's expansion won a city competition but was set aside in favour of Ildefons Cerdà's plan.","The district hall building was designed by Francesc Berenguer, a close collaborator of Antoni Gaudí.","The district hall is still where the district is administered.","During the Festa Major, residents decorate their own streets and compete for the most imaginative decorations."]},"local":{"short":"Look around: many people around you aren't visitors, they live right here. For the neighbours in the flats above, this square is a shared living room, where they stop to chat and meet beneath the tall clock tower. Enjoy it the local way: find a quiet spot, slow down and watch daily life.","normal":"Notice the people around you: many of them are not visitors, they live right here. This square sits in the middle of a real neighbourhood, and for the people in the flats above you it works like a shared living room. They cross it on the way home, stop to chat, and meet friends beneath the tall clock tower that has become Gràcia's symbol. Opposite stands the district hall. In August, during the Festa Major, giant figures dance here. Today, enjoy it the local way: find a quiet spot, slow down and watch daily life go by.","long":"Take a moment and notice the people around you. Many of them are not visitors at all; they live right here. This square sits in the middle of a real neighbourhood, and for the people in the flats above you it works like a shared living room. Watch for a while and you will see it. You might spot someone crossing on the way home with their shopping, children playing on the open paving, or two neighbours stopping mid-step for a chat. Friends arrange to meet beneath the tall clock tower, because it is the one landmark everybody here knows. It was built in the 1860s, when Gràcia was still a town of its own, and it has become the symbol of the neighbourhood. Opposite stands the district hall, so this is also where local life and local government sit face to face. Gràcia only joined Barcelona in 1897, and many residents still speak of their neighbourhood with a quiet pride, almost as if it were a village inside the city. In August, during the Festa Major, giant figures dance here, and neighbours spend months preparing decorations for their own streets, often made by hand from recycled materials. The rest of the year, the square belongs to ordinary days. Enjoy it the local way. Find a quiet spot at the edge of the square and slow down. Keep your voice low, especially in the evening when people above are resting, and simply watch daily life go by.","new_facts":["The clock tower was built in the 1860s, when Gràcia was still an independent town.","Gràcia joined Barcelona in 1897.","Residents spend months preparing handmade decorations for their streets for the Festa Major, often using recycled materials."]},"history":{"short":"Imagine standing here when this wasn't Barcelona at all. Gràcia was an independent town, and this square was its beating heart. Look up at the slender clock tower, raised in the 1860s while Gràcia still governed itself. In 1897, the town joined Barcelona, but its pride never quite left.","normal":"Imagine standing here when this was not Barcelona at all. Gràcia was once an independent town, and this square was its beating heart. Look up at the slender clock tower. It went up in the 1860s, while Gràcia still governed itself, and it has become the symbol of the neighbourhood. Across the square stands Gràcia's district hall. In 1897 Gràcia joined Barcelona, yet the old town's pride never quite left. You can still feel it in the streets around you, where real neighbours live their everyday lives.","long":"Imagine standing here when this was not Barcelona at all. Gràcia was once an independent town, with its own council and its own strong identity, and this square was its beating heart. During the nineteenth century the town grew quickly, as workshops and workers' homes spread across the land between old Barcelona and the hills. Look up at the slender clock tower. It went up in the 1860s, while Gràcia still governed itself, and it was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias. A public clock like this said something: here was a town confident enough to give its people a shared time and a landmark of their own. It has become the symbol of the neighbourhood. In 1870, during a revolt against military conscription, the tower's bell rang out over the rebellion, and the town was shelled by government troops. Across the square stands Gràcia's district hall, designed by Francesc Berenguer, who worked closely with Antoni Gaudí. In 1897 Gràcia joined Barcelona, together with several neighbouring towns. Yet the old town's pride never quite left. You can still feel it in the narrow streets around you and in the small squares that open up between them, where real neighbours live their everyday lives. As you walk on, picture the town that once stood here on its own, and keep your voice down as you pass, because those streets are still people's homes.","new_facts":["Gràcia grew quickly during the nineteenth century, with workshops and workers' housing spreading between old Barcelona and the hills.","The clock tower was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","In 1870, during a revolt against military conscription, the tower's bell was rung and Gràcia was shelled by government troops.","The district hall was designed by Francesc Berenguer, a close collaborator of Antoni Gaudí.","Several other neighbouring towns were joined to Barcelona together with Gràcia in 1897."]},"architecture":{"short":"Tilt your head back and follow the clock tower to the top. See how slender it is, a tall narrow shaft you can spot from every corner. Built in the 1860s, it gave the whole square one shared time. Opposite, the district hall faces it across the open space.","normal":"Tilt your head back and follow the clock tower all the way to the top. Notice how slender it is: a tall, narrow shaft rising from the open square, easy to spot from every corner. It was built in the 1860s, when Gràcia was still its own town, and a public clock like this let the whole square keep the same time. Now turn and look at Gràcia's district hall facing it. Together they frame this open space, made for gathering and still shared by the people who live all around it.","long":"Tilt your head back and follow the clock tower all the way to the top. Notice how slender it is: a tall, narrow shaft rising straight out of the open square, easy to spot from every corner. It was built in the 1860s, when Gràcia was still its own town, and it was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, whose plan for Barcelona's expansion had won a city competition before Ildefons Cerdà's plan was imposed instead. Let your eyes travel slowly upward, from the base, along the shaft, to the clock near the top. A public clock like this let the whole square keep the same time, and a tower this tall carried the sound of its bell far beyond the square. Now turn and look at Gràcia's district hall facing it. The building was designed by Francesc Berenguer, a close collaborator of Antoni Gaudí. Take a moment with the details of its facade, and compare them with the clean vertical line of the tower. Together, the tower and the hall frame this open space. Apart from the tower, nothing crowds the open paving, so the square works like an outdoor room, made for gathering, for conversations and for festival crowds. Then look at the surrounding buildings, with their rows of balconies enclosing the square on every side. Those balconies belong to people's homes, so as you study the architecture, remember that this space is still shared by the people who live all around it.","new_facts":["The clock tower was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias.","Rovira i Trias's plan for Barcelona's expansion won a city competition, but Ildefons Cerdà's plan was imposed instead.","The clock tower has a bell.","The district hall was designed by Francesc Berenguer, a close collaborator of Antoni Gaudí."]}},"tip":"Neighbours live all around the square: in the evening, keep the noise down."},"placa-virreina":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Small, quiet and shaded by trees, this is one of Gràcia's favourite squares for a coffee. At one end stands the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War. Watch the benches and terraces fill with neighbours: Gràcia's squares are its living rooms.","normal":"Small, quiet and lined with trees, this square is one of Gràcia's favourite places for a coffee in the shade. At one end stands the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War. Watch how the square works: parents with prams, older neighbours on the benches, friends on the terraces. Gràcia's squares are its living rooms.","long":"You're standing in Plaça de la Virreina, a small, quiet square lined with trees, and one of Gràcia's favourite places for a coffee in the shade. Its name, Virreina, means 'vicereine'. Take a moment to get your bearings. At one end stands the church of Sant Joan, which is Catalan for Saint John. Its front closes off the square and gives your eye somewhere to rest. The church was rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War, so the building in front of you is a survivor of one of the hardest chapters in the city's history. It also helps to know that Gràcia wasn't always part of Barcelona. It was an independent town until the city annexed it in 1897, and you can still feel that small-town character in its narrow streets and its many little squares. Now turn your back to the church and watch how this square works. Parents push prams across the paving. Older neighbours settle onto the benches. Friends linger on the café terraces, in no particular hurry. That's why people say Gràcia's squares are its living rooms: this is where the neighbourhood meets, chats and passes the time. Every August, during Gràcia's Festa Major, residents decorate many of the neighbourhood's streets themselves, and the whole district turns into a party. Today you're seeing the everyday version, which is just as telling. Keep your voice low, remember that people really live in the buildings all around you, and if you have a few minutes, take a seat and join in.","new_facts":["The name 'Virreina' means 'vicereine'.","'Sant Joan' is Catalan for Saint John.","Gràcia was an independent town until Barcelona annexed it in 1897.","Gràcia's Festa Major takes place every August, when residents decorate many of the neighbourhood's streets."]},"local":{"short":"Here's the secret of Gràcia: its squares are its living rooms. Look around at the prams, the neighbours on the benches, the friends lingering over slow coffees. At one end, notice the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War. People live here, so take a seat and watch.","normal":"Here's the secret of Gràcia: its squares are its living rooms. Look around you. Parents push prams across the paving, older neighbours settle onto the benches, and friends linger on the café terraces over a slow coffee. Nobody is in a hurry, and that's the point. At one end, notice the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War. People really live in the buildings around you, so do as they do: take a seat, order a coffee, and simply watch the neighbourhood go about its day.","long":"Here's the secret of Gràcia: its squares are its living rooms. Part of the reason is history. Gràcia was an independent town until Barcelona annexed it in 1897, and it has kept a strong sense of itself ever since. Squares like this one are where that village spirit lives on. So look around you. Parents push prams across the paving, older neighbours settle onto the benches, and friends linger on the café terraces over a slow coffee. Nobody is in a hurry, and that's the point. Listen, too. You'll often hear Catalan, the local language, in the conversations around you. At one end, notice the church of Sant Joan. The name is Catalan for Saint John. It was rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War, and today it quietly anchors the square, part of the everyday backdrop for the people who live here. If you're lucky enough to visit in August, you may catch Gràcia's Festa Major, when residents decorate many of the streets themselves. But honestly, an ordinary afternoon tells you just as much. Notice how the terraces, the benches and the open paving give everyone a place, whether they're two or ninety. People really live in the buildings around you, so do as they do: keep your voice down, take a seat, order a coffee, and simply watch the neighbourhood go about its day. That's the best way to see Gràcia.","new_facts":["Gràcia was an independent town until Barcelona annexed it in 1897.","Catalan is the local language spoken in Gràcia.","'Sant Joan' is Catalan for Saint John.","Gràcia's Festa Major takes place in August, when residents decorate many of the neighbourhood's streets."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Step under these trees and feel how the shade changes everything. Look up at the canopy and watch the light fall through the leaves in patches on the ground. At one end, the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned in the Civil War, closes the view. Pause quietly: people live here.","normal":"Step under these trees and notice how the shade changes the whole feel of the street. This small, leafy square is one of Gràcia's favourite spots to sit out of the sun, and on a bright day you'll see why. Look up at the canopy and watch the light fall through the leaves in patches onto the ground below. At one end, the church of Sant Joan, rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War, closes the view. Find a free bench or a terrace and enjoy a quiet pause. Remember, this is home for the people around you.","long":"Step under these trees and notice how the shade changes the whole feel of the street. Out in the open the sun can be fierce, but here the air feels softer. This small, leafy square is one of Gràcia's favourite spots to sit out of the sun, and on a bright day you'll understand why. Look up at the canopy. Watch the light fall through the leaves and land in shifting patches on the paving below. If there's a breeze, those patches move, and the whole ground seems to flicker. Now let your eyes travel to one end of the square. The church of Sant Joan, Catalan for Saint John, closes the view. It was rebuilt after it was burned during the Civil War, so the building in front of you belongs to the story of a city that has had to repair itself. Notice how the trees frame it, so that the church appears in pieces between the trunks and branches as you move. This is a good moment to remember that Gràcia was once a town of its own, until Barcelona annexed it in 1897. Its many small squares, like this one, still give it the feel of a place built around open-air meeting points. Take your time. Find a free bench or a seat on a terrace, rest your feet, and enjoy a quiet pause in the shade. Listen to the conversations, the footsteps, the clink of cups. And remember, this is home for the people around you, so keep your voice gentle and leave the benches as you found them.","new_facts":["'Sant Joan' is Catalan for Saint John.","Gràcia was an independent town until Barcelona annexed it in 1897."]}},"tip":"It's a calm residential square: sit, enjoy, and keep voices low."},"mercat-barceloneta":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Look up at that bold roof. It seems to float above the old market, and it's fitted with solar panels. Underneath sits the iron structure of a market that has served this fishermen's neighbourhood since the 19th century. Step inside for fresh fish, and watch locals lunching at the counter bars.","normal":"The fishermen's neighbourhood has had a market here since the 19th century. The old iron structure was renovated in 2007, and the architects added a bold new roof with solar panels that seems to float above it. Inside, look for fresh fish and seafood, of course, and small bars where locals have lunch at the counter.","long":"You're standing in front of the market of La Barceloneta, the old fishermen's neighbourhood by the sea. There has been a market on this spot since the 19th century, and it's still where people here do their everyday shopping. Start by looking up. That bold roof seems to float above the building, almost as if it were hovering rather than resting. It was added in 2007, when the market was renovated by the architect Josep Miàs, and it carries solar panels, so the roof that shelters the market also gathers energy from the sun. Now lower your eyes to the older layer beneath it: the iron structure of the original market. The architects kept that iron frame rather than replacing it, so you're looking at two eras at once, the old market below and the new roof above. As you walk around, notice the streets leading away from the square. They're narrow and straight, laid out in a tight grid. That's because Barceloneta was planned in the 18th century to rehouse families who lost their homes when a military fortress was built nearby. Over time it became the home of fishermen and port workers. Inside the market, the sea still sets the tone. Look for the fish and seafood counters, then for the small bars tucked among the stalls, where locals sit at the counter for lunch. If you go in, remember this is a working neighbourhood market, not a stage. Wait your turn, ask before taking photos of stallholders, and keep your voice down around the homes that line the square.","new_facts":["The 2007 renovation was designed by the architect Josep Miàs.","Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century as a grid of narrow, straight streets.","The neighbourhood was built to rehouse families displaced when a military fortress (the Ciutadella) was built nearby.","Barceloneta became home to fishermen and port workers."]},"food":{"short":"Breathe in: this is what a fishermen's neighbourhood smells like. Barceloneta has had a market here since the 19th century, and the sea still sets the menu. Head for the fish counters, then find a stool at one of the little bars among the stalls and eat at the counter, like the locals.","normal":"Breathe in. This is what a fishermen's neighbourhood smells like. Barceloneta has had a market on this spot since the 19th century, and the sea still sets the menu. Step inside and head for the fish counters, where fresh fish and seafood are laid out for the neighbourhood's shoppers. Then look for the small bars tucked in among the stalls. Locals perch at the counter there for lunch. If you join them, keep it simple: take a stool, point at what looks good, and eat the way the neighbours do, unhurried and at the bar.","long":"Breathe in. This is what a fishermen's neighbourhood smells like. Barceloneta has had a market on this spot since the 19th century, and even now the sea sets the menu. The neighbourhood itself is older than the market. It was laid out in the 18th century beside the port, and for generations many of the people living in these narrow streets made their living from the water. Step inside and let your nose guide you to the fish counters. Take your time looking. Notice how carefully the fish and seafood are laid out, and watch how the neighbourhood's shoppers choose: a question to the stallholder, a careful look, a decision. This is daily shopping for the people who live here, so give them room and wait your turn. Then look for the small bars tucked in among the stalls. You'll spot them by the stools and the people perched at the counter, eating lunch without any hurry. If you'd like to join them, keep it simple. Take a free stool, point at what looks good, and let the people behind the counter do the rest. Eat the way the neighbours do, unhurried and at the bar, and listen to the conversations around you. When you leave, step back into the square and look at the building itself. That bold roof, fitted with solar panels, seems to float above the old iron market, a modern lid added in 2007 over the stalls you've just tasted from.","new_facts":["Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century beside the port."]},"local":{"short":"This market wasn't built for visitors. It's where La Barceloneta has done its everyday shopping since the 19th century. Watch people chatting with stallholders as their bags slowly fill. Wait your turn, ask before taking photos, and keep your voice low: the flats around this square are real homes.","normal":"This market wasn't built as a show for visitors. It's where La Barceloneta does its everyday shopping, and it has been since the 19th century, back when this was a neighbourhood of fishermen. Watch the rhythm around you: people chatting with stallholders, shopping bags slowly filling up, someone grabbing a quick lunch at one of the counter bars. Join in gently. Wait your turn, ask before photographing stalls or people, and buy something if you can. The flats around this square are real homes, so keep your voice low as you wander.","long":"This market wasn't built as a show for visitors. It's where La Barceloneta does its everyday shopping, and it has been since the 19th century, back when this was a neighbourhood of fishermen. The neighbourhood is older still. It was laid out in the 18th century as a grid of narrow streets beside the port, and you can see that pattern from here. Look down any of the lanes leading off the square and notice how straight and tight they are. People have lived side by side in these streets for generations, and the market has long been one of the places where they meet. So before you go in, just watch the rhythm for a moment. People chat with stallholders, shopping bags fill up slowly, and someone ducks into one of the counter bars for a quick lunch. Nobody's in a rush, and nobody's performing. When you're ready, join in gently. Wait your turn at the stalls, and if you'd like a photo of a stall or a person, ask first. A smile and a simple gesture usually do the trick. If you can, buy something, even something small, like a drink or a bite at one of the counters. It's the easiest way to be a welcome guest rather than a spectator. Finally, look up at the windows and balconies around the square. Those are real homes, where people are cooking, resting and getting on with their day. Keep your voice low as you wander, and leave the square as quiet as you found it.","new_facts":["Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century as a grid of narrow streets beside the port."]},"architecture":{"short":"Look up. That bold roof seems to float above the old market, as if it had drifted in from the sea. It was added in 2007, fitted with solar panels, above the market's old iron structure. Walk around the outside and spot the two layers: old iron below, new roof above.","normal":"Look up. That bold roof seems to float above the old market, as if it had drifted in from the sea and settled there. Beneath it is the old iron structure of a market that has served this fishermen's neighbourhood since the 19th century. When the building was renovated in 2007, the architects kept the iron and set this striking new roof above it, fitted with solar panels. Walk slowly around the outside and notice the two layers, the old iron frame and the new roof, telling two different chapters of the same story.","long":"Look up. That bold roof seems to float above the old market, as if it had drifted in from the sea and settled there. Beneath it stands the old iron structure of a market that has served this fishermen's neighbourhood since the 19th century. Iron was a modern building material back then, widely used for market halls because it could span wide, open spaces and let in air and light. When the building was renovated in 2007, the architect Josep Miàs kept that iron frame instead of sweeping it away, and set this striking new roof above it, fitted with solar panels. So the roof isn't only a gesture; it also turns sunlight into energy. Now walk slowly around the outside, letting your eyes move between the two layers. Below, look at the older iron frame and the steady rhythm of its supports. Above, see how the new roof breaks away from that order and reaches out beyond the old building. Try a few different angles. From some spots the roof seems to hover; from others it looks as if it's stretching toward the surrounding streets. Those streets are part of the story too. Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century as a tight grid of narrow lanes, so an open square like this one really stands out. Notice how the building sits among the homes around it, and keep your voice low; people live in those flats. The old iron and the new roof tell two chapters of the same story, written for the people who still shop here every day.","new_facts":["The 2007 renovation was designed by the architect Josep Miàs.","In the 19th century iron was widely used for market halls because it could span wide open spaces and let in air and light.","Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century as a tight grid of narrow lanes."]}},"tip":"Most Barcelona markets close on Sundays and many stalls close in the afternoon."},"mercat-concepcio":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This iron-and-glass market has served the right side of the Eixample since the late nineteenth century. Look at the flower stalls outside, spilling colour onto the street. Inside, locals buy fruit, meat, fish and cheese, with hardly a tourist in sight. It's a real neighbourhood market, so browse quietly.","normal":"Built in the late 19th century, this iron-and-glass market serves the right side of the Eixample. It's especially known for its flower stalls, which spill colour onto the street outside. Inside, it's a real neighbourhood market: fruit, meat, fish and cheese, with very few tourists. A good place to see how locals in the Eixample really shop.","long":"You're standing at Mercat de la Concepció, one of the neighbourhood markets that keeps the Eixample fed. It opened in 1888, designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias, and it has served the right side of the Eixample ever since. Before you go in, take a moment with the flower stalls outside. Their buckets and bunches spill colour right onto the pavement, and they're what this market is best known for. You may see people stop on their way past and pick up a bunch for the table as casually as a loaf of bread. Now look up at the building itself. The roof is iron and glass, the materials of its age, letting daylight fall across the counters below. Step inside and you'll find a working market rather than a showpiece. There's fruit stacked in careful piles, butchers at their counters, fresh fish laid out, and cheese stalls with wheels and wedges on display. Listen for a moment. Most of the conversations around you are between stallholders and regulars, and very few of the voices belong to tourists. That's what makes this place worth a visit. It's one of the best spots to see how people in the Eixample really shop. If you'd like to join in, buying a piece of fruit or a little cheese is a welcome way to do it. Otherwise, keep to the side of the aisles, give way to anyone carrying full bags, and ask before photographing stallholders. You're a guest in someone's everyday routine here.","new_facts":["The market opened in 1888.","It was designed by the architect Antoni Rovira i Trias."]},"food":{"short":"Step under the iron-and-glass roof and follow your nose. Since the late nineteenth century, these counters of fruit, fish, meat and cheese have fed the right side of the Eixample. Watch what shoppers choose so carefully, because it's tonight's dinner. Keep your voice low and your path clear.","normal":"Step under the iron-and-glass roof and follow your nose. This market has fed the right side of the Eixample since the late nineteenth century, and what's on these counters is what local kitchens will cook tonight. Look for bright piles of fruit, fresh fish, butchers' counters and cheese stalls. Watch which ingredients shoppers pick out so carefully, and you'll learn more about everyday Barcelona cooking than any menu can tell you. Keep your voice low and your path clear: people here are doing their real daily shopping, not posing for photos.","long":"Step under the iron-and-glass roof and let your nose lead the way. This market has fed the right side of the Eixample since the late nineteenth century, and the counters around you hold what local kitchens will be cooking tonight. Start with the fruit. Look at how bright the piles are, and how neatly they're stacked. Then drift toward the fish counters, where customers lean in for a close look before deciding. Next come the butchers, and the cheese stalls with their wheels and wedges. Each counter is its own small world, with its own regulars and its own rhythm. The real lesson here isn't on any label. It's in the shoppers. Watch how carefully they choose. A piece of fruit gets turned over in the hand, someone asks the stallholder a quick question, and there's a nod when the right piece is found. Notice too which ingredients keep ending up in people's bags, because that's the shape of tonight's dinners across the neighbourhood. Spend a few minutes simply observing and you'll understand more about everyday Barcelona cooking than any restaurant menu can tell you, because this is food for home tables, not for show. If something tempts you, buying a little fruit or a piece of cheese is a fine way to taste the neighbourhood. Just remember that everyone else here is doing their real daily shopping. Keep your voice low, keep your path clear of the queues, and ask before you photograph anyone's stall.","new_facts":[]},"local":{"short":"Notice something: hardly anyone around you is a tourist. This is where people from the right side of the Eixample do their everyday shopping. Look at the flower stalls outside, their colours spilling onto the street. Step aside for anyone with full bags, keep your voice soft, and simply watch the neighbourhood.","normal":"Notice something here: hardly anyone around you is a tourist. Mercat de la Concepció is where people from the right side of the Eixample do their everyday shopping, and you're standing in the middle of it. Look at the flower stalls outside, their colours spilling onto the street, then glance under the iron-and-glass roof built in the late nineteenth century. Neighbours come in for fruit, meat, fish and cheese. Step aside for anyone with full bags, keep your voice soft, and enjoy simply watching a real neighbourhood go about its day.","long":"Notice something here: hardly anyone around you is a tourist. Mercat de la Concepció is where people from the right side of the Eixample do their everyday shopping, and you're standing right in the middle of it. The market fills the block between Carrer d'Aragó and Carrer de València, tucked into the grid of ordinary apartment streets. Start outside, with the flower stalls. Their colours spill onto the street, and you may see someone pause to pick up a bunch on the way home. Then glance up at the iron-and-glass roof, built in the late nineteenth century. It has sheltered the same daily routine for generations. Now look at the people beneath it. You might see neighbours with shopping trolleys, someone dashing in on a lunch break, or a regular chatting with a stallholder. They're here for fruit, meat, fish and cheese, the ordinary things that fill a kitchen. This isn't a show put on for visitors. It's simply how the neighbourhood lives, and that's exactly what makes it worth a few quiet minutes. Find a spot out of the flow and just watch the rhythm of the place: the weighing, the wrapping, the small talk. If you'd like to take part, buy something small and say thank you. Step aside for anyone with full bags, keep your voice soft, and ask before photographing people or their stalls. Enjoy watching a real neighbourhood go about its day.","new_facts":["The market occupies the block between Carrer d'Aragó and Carrer de València."]}},"tip":"Buy something if you linger: the stalls are people's livelihoods, not a museum."},"mercat-abaceria":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This is Gràcia's biggest market, and since the end of the nineteenth century local families have come here for their fruit, fish and bread. After a long renovation, the stalls may be in a temporary space nearby. Either way, watch the trolleys and the regulars: this is everyday Gràcia, far from the tourist routes.","normal":"This is Gràcia's biggest market and one of the busiest neighbourhood markets in the city, serving local families since the end of the 19th century. It has gone through a long renovation, so the stalls may be in a temporary space nearby. Either way, it's where Gràcia buys its fruit, fish and bread, far from the tourist routes.","long":"You're standing at the Mercat de l'Abaceria Central, Gràcia's biggest market and one of the busiest neighbourhood markets in all of Barcelona. It has been feeding local families since the end of the nineteenth century. Even the name tells you what it's for. An abaceria is an old Catalan word for a grocer's shop, a place for everyday provisions, and that is exactly what this still is. The market has been through a long renovation, so depending on when you visit, the stalls may be working out of a temporary space nearby. Don't let that put you off. A market is really its stallholders and its regulars, not its roof. Take a moment to look before you walk in. Notice who is coming and going: older residents with wheeled shopping trolleys, parents with a child in tow, someone carrying a loaf under one arm. This is where Gràcia buys its fruit, its fish and its bread, far from the tourist routes, and many regulars here know their stallholders by name. Like the city's other municipal markets, it's part of daily routine rather than a sight to tick off. So if you go in, move gently, keep to the side of the aisles, and ask before you photograph anyone. And if something looks good, buy it. A piece of fruit eaten on a bench nearby is one of the simplest ways to feel, for a few minutes, like you live here.","new_facts":["\"Abaceria\" is an old Catalan word for a grocer's or provisions shop.","It is one of Barcelona's municipal (city-run) markets."]},"food":{"short":"Look at what's piled highest on the stalls: that's usually what's best right now, because the displays follow the seasons. Gràcia has bought its fruit, fish and bread here since the end of the nineteenth century. Pick up a ripe piece of fruit to enjoy outside, and keep the aisles clear.","normal":"Look around at what fills the stalls, because this is where Gràcia buys its fruit, its fish and its bread, and it has done so since the end of the nineteenth century. It's the neighbourhood's biggest market and one of the busiest local markets in the whole city. Notice how the displays follow the seasons. Whatever is piled highest is usually what's best right now. For a taste, buy a ripe piece of fruit or a little bread and enjoy it outside. People here are doing their real shopping, so keep the aisles clear.","long":"Look around at what fills the stalls, because this is where Gràcia buys its fruit, its fish and its bread, and it has done so since the end of the nineteenth century. It's the neighbourhood's biggest market and one of the busiest local markets in the whole city. The name itself is a clue: an abaceria is an old Catalan word for a grocer's shop, a place for everyday provisions. Start with the fruit and vegetable stalls. The displays follow the seasons, so the mix changes through the year, and whatever is piled highest is usually what's best right now. Notice how the stallholders build those piles by hand, the best pieces turned to face you. Prices are usually marked on small cards stuck into the produce. Then find the fish counters. Mornings tend to be busiest, and the fish usually lies whole on crushed ice, so you can judge it by its bright eyes and shine. Locals often ask for it cleaned or cut on the spot, and you'll hear quick chats about how it will be cooked. Bread is the easiest thing to enjoy straight away. Buy a small loaf or a ripe piece of fruit, say thank you, and take it outside to eat. If the stalls are in a temporary space nearby because of the long renovation, the same applies. People here are doing their real shopping, so keep the aisles clear, let trolleys pass, and wait your turn.","new_facts":["\"Abaceria\" is an old Catalan word for a grocer's or provisions shop."]},"local":{"short":"Watch the wheeled trolleys and the quick chats between stallholders and regulars: this is Gràcia's biggest market, where families have shopped since the end of the nineteenth century. At a busy stall, ask \"Qui és l'últim?\", meaning \"Who's last?\", then wait behind that person. Speak softly and ask before photographing anyone.","normal":"Watch the shoppers for a moment. Notice the wheeled trolleys, the reusable bags, and the quick, easy chats between stallholders and their regulars. This is Gràcia's biggest market. Local families have shopped here since the end of the nineteenth century, far from the tourist routes. There's one ritual worth learning. When you reach a busy stall, ask \"Qui és l'últim?\" That means \"Who's last?\" Then simply wait your turn behind that person. Speak softly, step aside for trolleys, and ask before photographing anyone. This is a real neighbourhood doing its everyday shopping.","long":"Before you go in, stand to one side and watch the shoppers for a moment. Notice the wheeled trolleys, the reusable bags, and the quick, easy chats between stallholders and their regulars. The pace is unhurried but purposeful. This is Gràcia's biggest market, and local families have shopped here since the end of the nineteenth century, far from the tourist routes. Even the name is homely: an abaceria is an old Catalan word for a grocer's shop. The market has been through a long renovation, so you may find the stalls in a temporary space nearby. The habits stay the same wherever the stalls are. There's one ritual worth learning. When you reach a busy stall, ask \"Qui és l'últim?\" That's Catalan for \"Who's last?\" Someone will raise a hand or nod. Remember that person, and when they've been served, it's your turn. You'll also hear the Spanish version, \"¿Quién es el último?\", and either is perfectly fine. Use it even if you're only buying one apple. A simple \"bon dia\", good morning, as you reach a stall goes a long way. And remember whose space this is. Speak softly, step aside for trolleys, and ask before photographing anyone. This is a real neighbourhood doing its everyday shopping, and the kindest thing you can be here is an easy, unobtrusive guest.","new_facts":["\"Abaceria\" is an old Catalan word for a grocer's or provisions shop.","The Spanish equivalent of \"Qui és l'últim?\" is \"¿Quién es el último?\"","\"Bon dia\" means \"good morning\" in Catalan."]}},"tip":"Check where the stalls are on the day you visit: the market has been under renovation."},"mercat-ninot":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan, and this market is named after a carved wooden figurehead from a ship that once hung outside a nearby tavern. The building in front of you is modern, rebuilt in the 2010s. Step inside and look for the fish and seafood counters locals love.","normal":"Ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan. The market takes its name from a carved wooden figurehead from a ship that once hung outside a nearby tavern. Today the market has a modern building, rebuilt in the 2010s, and it's a favourite with local food lovers for its fish, seafood and good-quality produce.","long":"Ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan, and it's a charming name for a food market. It comes from a carved wooden figurehead, the kind that once sat on the bow of a sailing ship. That figurehead once hung outside a nearby tavern, and the market took its name from it. Take a moment to look at the building itself. What you see is modern, rebuilt in the 2010s. Now glance at the streets around you. This is the Eixample, the grid of blocks planned by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà in the nineteenth century, and you'll notice the corners of the blocks are cut off at an angle rather than square. That gives the crossings their open, roomy feel, and it's one of the easiest ways to know you're in this part of the city. Stand on one of those corners and you get a wide view of the whole block. If you head inside, you'll find out why the market has its reputation. Local food lovers come here above all for the fish and seafood, laid out fresh on the counters, and for good-quality produce. Notice how people shop: many chat with the stallholders, ask what's best today, and buy just what they need. If you'd like to buy something, a simple 'bon dia', good morning in Catalan, goes a long way. Remember this is a working neighbourhood market, not a showpiece. Keep to the side of the aisles, ask before taking close-up photos of stalls or people, and let the regulars get on with their shopping.","new_facts":["The Eixample district was planned by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà in the nineteenth century.","Eixample blocks have corners cut off at an angle (chamfered corners).","'Bon dia' means good morning in Catalan."]},"food":{"short":"This market is named after a ship's figurehead: ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan, and a carved wooden one once hung outside a nearby tavern. Head inside and look toward the fish and seafood counters, where the catch is laid out on ice. Point, ask, and let regulars go first.","normal":"Here's a market named after a ship's figurehead. Ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan, and the name comes from a carved wooden figurehead that once hung outside a nearby tavern. Today the building is modern, rebuilt in the 2010s, but the real draw is inside. Look toward the fish and seafood counters, where the catch is laid out on ice. Local food lovers come here for that, and for good-quality produce. This is a working neighbourhood market, so shop like a local: point, ask, and let the regulars go first.","long":"Here's a market named after a ship's figurehead. Ninot means a little figure or doll in Catalan. The name comes from a carved wooden figurehead, the kind that once rode on the bow of a sailing ship, which hung outside a nearby tavern. It's a fitting name for a place loved for its seafood. The building you see is modern, rebuilt in the 2010s, but the real draw is inside. Make your way toward the fish and seafood counters. The catch is laid out on ice, and it's worth slowing down to take in the variety of shapes, sizes and colours. Local food lovers come here for exactly that, and for good-quality produce on the fruit and vegetable stalls. Watch how regulars shop: they ask what's best today, chat with the stallholder, and choose carefully. Step back outside for a moment and look at the streets. This is the Eixample, the grid planned by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà in the nineteenth century, with corners cut off at an angle. This is a working neighbourhood market, so shop like a local. Point, ask, and let the regulars go first. If you're buying, a simple 'bon dia', good morning in Catalan, is a friendly start. Keep to the side of the aisles when you stop to look, and ask before taking close-up photos of the stalls or the people working them. And if you're staying somewhere with a kitchen, this is a lovely place to pick up something to cook tonight.","new_facts":["The Eixample district was planned by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà in the nineteenth century.","Eixample blocks have corners cut off at an angle (chamfered corners).","'Bon dia' means good morning in Catalan."]}},"tip":"Mornings are best: that's when the stalls are full and the neighbourhood does its shopping."},"bunkers-carmel":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"From this hilltop you can see almost the whole city, from the sea to the mountains. That view is why anti-aircraft guns stood here in the Spanish Civil War. Families with nowhere else to live later built shacks among the ruins and stayed until the late 1980s. Those concrete platforms are what remains.","normal":"From this hilltop you can see almost the whole city, from the sea to the mountains. That's exactly why anti-aircraft guns were placed here during the Spanish Civil War, to defend Barcelona from bombing raids. After the war, families who had nowhere else to live built shacks among the ruins, and they lived here until the late 1980s. The concrete platforms are what remains.","long":"You're standing on top of the Turó de la Rovira, and from here you can see almost the whole city, from the sea to the mountains. Take a moment to trace it. Find the Mediterranean shining along the horizon. Then turn toward the wooded hills of Collserola, with the church of Tibidabo at the top. Off to one side, Montjuïc sits low beside the port, and closer in you may spot the spires of the Sagrada Família rising above the rooftops. That wide view is exactly why anti-aircraft guns were placed here during the Spanish Civil War. Barcelona was bombed again and again from the air, and from this hilltop gunners could watch the sky and defend the city below. Look at the concrete platforms around you. Some of them held the guns, and you can still make out their shape. The story didn't stop when the war ended. In the hard years that followed, families who had nowhere else to live built shacks among the ruins, using the old military structures as walls and floors. People cooked, raised children and made a community up here, and they lived on this hilltop until the late 1980s. Today the concrete platforms are what remains, and the site is looked after as part of the city's history museum. As you sit and take in the view, remember that the slopes below are still a living neighbourhood. El Carmel is full of people's homes, so keep your voice gentle on the way down.","new_facts":["The hill is called the Turó de la Rovira.","The wooded hills of Collserola and the church on Tibidabo are visible from here.","Montjuïc and the port are visible from here.","The spires of the Sagrada Família are visible from here.","Barcelona was bombed repeatedly from the air during the Spanish Civil War.","The families who built shacks here used the old military structures as walls and floors.","The site is cared for as part of Barcelona's city history museum (MUHBA)."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Turn slowly in a full circle. Almost the whole city spreads below you, from the shining sea to the green mountains. Look across the rooftops for the spires of the Sagrada Família. Then sit on the concrete platforms, feel the breeze, and enjoy the view softly, because these slopes are home to real families.","normal":"Turn slowly in a full circle. From this hilltop almost the whole city spreads out below you, from the shining line of the sea to the green mountains behind. Let your eyes travel across the rooftops and see if you can pick out the spires of the Sagrada Família. Find a spot on the concrete platforms, sit, and just watch the light move over the streets. The breeze up here feels a world away from the busy centre. And remember, the houses on these slopes are home to real families, so enjoy the view softly.","long":"Turn slowly in a full circle and let the whole city arrive. From this hilltop, the Turó de la Rovira, almost all of Barcelona spreads out below you, from the shining line of the sea to the green mountains behind. Start with the water. Follow the Mediterranean along the horizon and you'll notice the ships and the cranes of the port. Then let your eyes travel back across the rooftops. Somewhere in that sea of buildings you can pick out the spires of the Sagrada Família, still growing after more than a century. To one side, the hill of Montjuïc rises beside the port. Keep turning, and the wooded ridge of Collserola fills the skyline, with the church of Tibidabo perched on its highest point. Now find a spot on the concrete platforms and sit for a while. These slabs are the remains of anti-aircraft positions from the Spanish Civil War, which is exactly why they sit somewhere with such a wide view. Watch how the light moves over the streets. As clouds pass, whole neighbourhoods dim and brighten, and the grid of the Eixample, with its regular blocks, becomes easier to read from up here. Notice the breeze too. It feels a world away from the busy centre, even though the centre is right there below you. As the day goes on, more people come up to share the view, especially near sunset. And remember, the houses on these slopes are home to real families, so enjoy it softly, and take the quiet with you when you leave.","new_facts":["The hill is called the Turó de la Rovira.","The port, with its ships and cranes, is visible from here.","The Sagrada Família has been under construction for more than a century.","Montjuïc rises beside the port and is visible from here.","The ridge of Collserola and the church of Tibidabo, on its highest point, are visible from here.","The grid of the Eixample district can be seen from here.","The viewpoint gets busier near sunset."]},"history":{"short":"Look down at the concrete under your feet. Anti-aircraft guns stood here in the Spanish Civil War, defending Barcelona from bombing raids. Afterwards, families with nowhere else to live built shacks among the ruins and stayed until the late 1980s. Picture kitchens and children's games here. Below, El Carmel is still full of homes.","normal":"Look down at the concrete under your feet. During the Spanish Civil War, anti-aircraft guns stood on this hilltop. It was chosen because from here you can see almost the whole city, from the sea to the mountains, making it a lookout to defend Barcelona from bombing raids. But the story didn't end with the war. Families with nowhere else to live built shacks among the ruins, and people made their homes right here until the late 1980s. Picture kitchens and children's games where you stand now. Today El Carmel below is still a living neighbourhood, full of people's homes.","long":"Look down at the concrete under your feet. During the Spanish Civil War, anti-aircraft guns stood on this hilltop, the Turó de la Rovira. It was chosen because from here you can see almost the whole city, from the sea to the mountains, making it a lookout to defend Barcelona from bombing raids. Those raids were real and terrifying. The city was bombed heavily from the air, much of it by Italian aircraft flying in from Mallorca across the sea. Turn toward the water and imagine scanning that horizon, waiting for the sound of engines. Now look closely at the platforms. Some are the bases where the guns were mounted, and nearby you can find the remains of rooms used by the soldiers who served here. But the story didn't end with the war. In the hard years that followed, Barcelona was short of housing. Families with nowhere else to live built shacks among the ruins, using the military concrete as foundations and walls. Over time this became a real settlement, and people made their homes right here until the late 1980s. Picture kitchens, washing hung out to dry, and children's games where you stand now. Look carefully and you may notice traces of those homes too, like patches of old floor tiles in the ground. Today the site is cared for as part of the city's history museum, and El Carmel below is still a living neighbourhood, full of people's homes. Keep that in mind as you head back down its streets, and keep your voice low.","new_facts":["The hill is called the Turó de la Rovira.","Much of the bombing of Barcelona was carried out by Italian aircraft flying from Mallorca.","Remains of rooms used by the soldiers who served at the battery survive near the gun platforms.","Barcelona faced a housing shortage in the years after the war.","The shacks used the military concrete as foundations and walls.","Traces of the shack homes, such as floor tiles, can still be seen on the site.","The site is cared for as part of Barcelona's city history museum (MUHBA)."]}},"tip":"Neighbours have suffered from crowds and noise up here: come at quiet times, and check the opening hours."},"port-vell":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This is Barcelona's old port, where the city met the sea for centuries. The wooden walkway in front of you, the Rambla de Mar, actually swings open to let boats pass. Look back towards the city and find the Columbus monument standing at the end of the Rambla.","normal":"This is Barcelona's old port, where the city met the sea for centuries. The wooden walkway in front of you, the Rambla de Mar, swings open to let boats pass. Look back towards the city: the Columbus monument stands at the end of the Rambla. Since the 1992 Olympics, this old working harbour has become a promenade for walking, cycling and watching the boats.","long":"This is Port Vell, Barcelona's old port, where the city met the sea for centuries. For most of that time this was a place of work, with ships loading and unloading along the quays. Right in front of you is the Rambla de Mar, a wooden walkway stretching out over the water. It isn't fixed in place: it swings open to let boats pass, so keep an eye on it and you might catch it moving. Follow it with your eyes and you'll see it leads across the harbour to the Maremagnum shopping centre on the far side. Now turn and look back towards the city. At the end of the Rambla stands the Columbus monument, a tall column with a statue of Christopher Columbus on top. It was built for the Universal Exhibition of 1888 and rises around sixty metres. Notice his raised arm: he's pointing out to sea, across the Mediterranean. Near the foot of the monument, look for the long stone halls of the Drassanes, the medieval royal shipyards where galleys were once built. Today they house the city's Maritime Museum. Since the 1992 Olympics, this old working harbour has become a promenade for walking, cycling and watching the boats. Along the waterfront lies Barceloneta, the old fishermen's neighbourhood, and rising above the harbour is the green hill of Montjuïc. Before you move on, stand still for a moment and watch the masts sway, just as people have watched them here for centuries.","new_facts":["The Rambla de Mar leads across the harbour to the Maremagnum shopping centre.","The Columbus monument was built for the 1888 Universal Exhibition.","The Columbus monument is around sixty metres tall.","The Columbus statue points out to sea, across the Mediterranean.","The Drassanes near the monument are the medieval royal shipyards where galleys were built.","The Drassanes now house Barcelona's Maritime Museum.","Barceloneta, the old fishermen's neighbourhood, lies along the waterfront.","The hill of Montjuïc rises above the harbour."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Under your feet, the wooden Rambla de Mar swings open to let boats pass, so for a moment you're standing on a bridge over the harbour. Then turn towards the city and find the Columbus monument at the end of the Rambla. Share it gently: neighbours live here too.","normal":"Under your feet, the wooden walkway of the Rambla de Mar swings open to let boats pass, so for a moment you're standing on a bridge over the harbour. Pause and listen to the water against the hulls, and notice how the light glints between the moored boats. Then turn back towards the city, where the Columbus monument rises at the end of the Rambla. Since the 1992 Olympics, Barcelona's old working harbour has become open space for walking, cycling and breathing sea air. Share it gently: neighbours live and work right here too.","long":"Under your feet, the wooden walkway of the Rambla de Mar swings open to let boats pass, so for a moment you're standing on a bridge over the harbour. Feel the boards beneath you and look ahead: the walkway carries on across the water to the Maremagnum centre on the far side. Now pause and listen. You'll hear the water slapping gently against the hulls, maybe the creak of ropes and the clink of rigging against masts when the breeze picks up. Notice how the light glints between the moored boats, changing as the clouds move and the sun shifts over the water. Then turn back towards the city. At the end of the Rambla, the Columbus monument rises above the traffic, a tall column with the explorer on top, his arm stretched out towards the sea. It was built for the Universal Exhibition of 1888 and stands around sixty metres high. Close by, the long stone halls of the Drassanes, the medieval royal shipyards, now house the Maritime Museum. Let your eyes drift along the skyline. On one side, the green hill of Montjuïc rises above the harbour. Along the waterfront the other way lies Barceloneta, the old fishermen's neighbourhood. Since the 1992 Olympics, Barcelona's old working harbour has become open space for walking, cycling and breathing sea air. Take a deep breath of it. And share it gently: neighbours live and work right here too, from those who work on the boats to the families in Barceloneta, so keep to one side and leave room for everyone.","new_facts":["The Rambla de Mar leads across the harbour to the Maremagnum centre.","The Columbus statue's arm points out towards the sea.","The Columbus monument was built for the 1888 Universal Exhibition.","The Columbus monument is around sixty metres tall.","The Drassanes nearby are the medieval royal shipyards and now house the Maritime Museum.","The hill of Montjuïc rises above the harbour.","Barceloneta, the old fishermen's neighbourhood, lies along the waterfront."]}},"tip":"It's a lovely place at sunset: find a bench and watch the boats come in."},"parc-estacio-nord":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This park sits on old railway land beside the former North Station. Look at the ground: those long, wave-like forms covered in blue ceramic are Fallen Sky, a land-art work by the American artist Beverly Pepper. Children climb it and neighbours read beside it. Here, art is something you walk on.","normal":"This park was created on old railway land next to the former North Station. Look at the ground: the long, wave-like forms covered in blue ceramic are a land-art work by the American artist Beverly Pepper, called Fallen Sky. Children climb them, dogs run around them and neighbours read in the sun. Art here is something you walk on, not just look at.","long":"You're standing on old railway land. Trains once ran across this ground beside the former North Station, and later the space became a park for the neighbourhood. The big station building is still there, and today it's Barcelona's main long-distance bus station. Now look at the ground. Those long, wave-like forms covered in blue ceramic are a land-art work by the American artist Beverly Pepper, called Fallen Sky, or Cel Caigut in Catalan. It's as if a piece of the sky had dropped into the grass. Step a little closer and look at the surface: it's a mosaic of broken ceramic pieces in many shades of blue, a technique Barcelona knows well from Gaudí. Notice how the colour shifts when clouds pass, and how the curves seem to rise and sink as you walk slowly around them. Pepper also designed another piece in this park, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral, so keep an eye out for it as you wander. Most of all, watch how the people who live here use this space. Children climb the ceramic waves, dogs run around them and neighbours read in the sun. Nobody treats it like a museum piece. Sit on a bench for a minute and simply watch. Art here is something you walk on, not just look at. If you join in, do it gently, the way the locals do, because this is their everyday park.","new_facts":["The work's Catalan name is Cel Caigut.","The former North Station building is now Barcelona's main long-distance bus station.","The surface is a mosaic of broken ceramic pieces in many shades of blue, a technique associated in Barcelona with Gaudí.","Beverly Pepper also created a second work in the park, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral (Espiral Arbrat)."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Trains once rumbled across the ground where you're standing. Now it's open lawn and wide sky. Look down at the long, curving shapes covered in blue ceramic: they're called Fallen Sky, as if a piece of the sky had landed here. Neighbours read and children climb, so enjoy it gently.","normal":"Trains once rumbled across the ground where you're standing. This was railway land beside the old North Station, and today it's open lawn and wide sky. Look down at the long, curving shapes rising from the grass, covered in blue ceramic. They're called Fallen Sky, as if a piece of the sky had landed in the park. Find a bench or a patch of grass nearby and watch the light play on the tiles. Around you, neighbours read, children climb and dogs run, so enjoy it gently, the way the locals do.","long":"Trains once rumbled across the ground where you're standing. This was railway land beside the old North Station, and the station building still stands nearby; today it's Barcelona's main long-distance bus station, so you may see coaches coming and going. Where tracks once ran, there's now open lawn and wide sky, a rare thing in the tightly built streets of the Eixample. Look down at the long, curving shapes rising from the grass, covered in blue ceramic. They're a work by the American artist Beverly Pepper called Fallen Sky, as if a piece of the sky had landed in the park. Up close, you'll see the surface is a mosaic of broken tiles in many shades of blue. Watch how the colour shifts with the light, softer under clouds and brighter when the sun comes out. Pepper also shaped another piece here, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral, so as you wander, see if you can spot it. Then find a bench or a patch of grass nearby and settle in for a moment. Watch the light play on the tiles and listen to the park around you. Neighbours read, children climb the ceramic waves and dogs run across the lawn. For the people who live around here, this is their back garden, so enjoy it gently, the way the locals do, and leave it just as you found it.","new_facts":["The former North Station building is now Barcelona's main long-distance bus station.","Fallen Sky is by the American artist Beverly Pepper.","The surface is a mosaic of broken tiles in many shades of blue.","Beverly Pepper also created a second work in the park, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral (Espiral Arbrat)."]},"art":{"short":"The artwork is under your feet. Those long, wave-like forms in blue ceramic are Fallen Sky, a land-art piece by the American artist Beverly Pepper. Instead of standing on a plinth, it rises out of the earth. Walk slowly around it and watch the curves change as you move.","normal":"Look at the ground, because the artwork is under your feet. Those long, wave-like forms covered in blue ceramic are a piece of land art by the American artist Beverly Pepper, called Fallen Sky. Instead of standing on a plinth, the sculpture rises out of the earth itself and invites you in. Notice how the curves change as you walk slowly around them. Watch the neighbours who use this park every day: children climb it, others read beside it. Here, art is lived in, not just looked at.","long":"Look at the ground, because the artwork is under your feet. Those long, wave-like forms covered in blue ceramic are a piece of land art by the American artist Beverly Pepper, called Fallen Sky, or Cel Caigut in Catalan. Land art works with the earth itself, and that's exactly what happens here: instead of standing on a plinth, the sculpture rises out of the ground and invites you in. Step closer and look at the surface. It's a mosaic of broken ceramic pieces in many shades of blue, from pale to deep. In Barcelona that technique is famously linked with Gaudí, and here it gives an earthwork the shimmer of sky and water. Now walk slowly around the forms. Notice how the curves change as you move: from one side they seem to swell like waves, from another they sink back into the grass. Pepper made a second work in this park, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral. It's worth looking for as you leave. Finally, watch the neighbours who use this park every day. Children climb Fallen Sky and slide down its curves, others sit and read beside it, and dogs race around its edges. The work only really comes alive with people on it. Here, art is lived in, not just looked at, so if you step onto it yourself, do it gently and leave room for the locals who call this park their own.","new_facts":["The work's Catalan name is Cel Caigut.","The surface is a mosaic of broken ceramic pieces in many shades of blue, a technique famously linked with Gaudí in Barcelona.","Beverly Pepper also created a second work in the park, a spiral of trees called Wooded Spiral (Espiral Arbrat)."]}},"tip":"A quiet, little-known park: perfect for a break away from the crowds."},"jardins-palau-robert":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Just off busy Passeig de Gràcia, step into a hidden garden of tall palm trees. Look up at their fronds, then at the elegant palace behind them, built at the turn of the twentieth century. Today it's a public information centre about Catalonia, and the garden is free to enter.","normal":"Just off the busy Passeig de Gràcia hides a small garden with tall palm trees, behind an elegant palace built at the turn of the 20th century. Today the palace is a public information centre about Catalonia, and the garden is free to enter. It's a surprising pocket of calm only a few steps from one of the city's busiest shopping streets.","long":"You've just stepped off Passeig de Gràcia, one of Barcelona's busiest shopping streets, and into something you might never guess was here: a small garden shaded by tall palm trees. Take a moment and listen. The traffic and the crowds are only a few steps away, yet the sound already feels softer, as if the garden walls were holding the city at arm's length. Look up at the palms first. Their trunks rise high above the paths, and their fronds catch the light and move with the slightest breeze. Then lower your eyes to the building behind them. This elegant palace was built at the turn of the twentieth century as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat, and it still carries his name. Notice how it sits back from the avenue, with a garden of its own. In the Eixample, the carefully planned grid of blocks laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà, private space like this was a rare luxury. Today the palace serves the public. It's an information centre about Catalonia, where you can pick up ideas for travelling beyond Barcelona and often visit free exhibitions. The garden, too, is free to enter, open to anyone who needs a pause. You'll likely share it with people who live and work nearby, reading or simply sitting in the shade. They come here for the same quiet you do, so keep your voice low, find a spot under the palms, and let the city slow down for a minute.","new_facts":["The palace was built as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat, and is named after him.","The Eixample's grid was laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà.","The information centre offers travel information about Catalonia beyond Barcelona and often hosts free exhibitions."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Close your eyes and listen: the traffic of Passeig de Gràcia is only a few steps away, yet in here it softens. Now look up at the tall palm trees, their fronds catching the light high above the paths. Neighbours come here for quiet too, so keep voices low.","normal":"Close your eyes. Can you still hear the traffic of Passeig de Gràcia? It's only a few steps away, yet in here the sound softens. Now look up at the tall palm trees, their fronds catching the light high above the paths. This small garden sits behind an elegant palace from the turn of the twentieth century, and it feels like a secret courtyard in the heart of the Eixample. Pause in the shade and let the city slow down. People who live and work nearby come here for quiet too, so keep voices low and enjoy the calm.","long":"Close your eyes for a moment. Can you still hear the traffic of Passeig de Gràcia? It's only a few steps away, yet in here the sound softens into a distant hum. Take a slow breath, then open your eyes and look up. Tall palm trees rise above the paths, their fronds catching the light high overhead and shifting with every breeze. Follow one trunk down to the ground and notice how its shade falls across the paths around you. This small garden sits behind an elegant palace from the turn of the twentieth century, built as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat. A garden of your own was a real luxury in the tightly planned grid of the Eixample, the district laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà. Today the palace is a public information centre about Catalonia, and the garden is free for anyone to enter, which makes it feel like a secret courtyard shared with the whole city. Walk a little deeper in and listen again to how the noise of the avenue fades. Pause in the shade and let the city slow down. Watch the light move across the palace walls, and notice the people around you. Many live and work nearby and come here for a quiet break, just as you have. Keep your voice low, give them space, and enjoy the calm a little longer before you step back into the crowds.","new_facts":["The palace was built as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat.","The Eixample district was laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà."]},"architecture":{"short":"Turn and face the palace behind the palm trees. Built at the turn of the twentieth century as a grand private home with its own garden, it has a calm, symmetrical stone facade with classical details, a formal contrast to the avenue's Modernista curves. Please admire it quietly.","normal":"Turn and face the palace behind the palm trees. Built at the turn of the twentieth century, it was a grand private residence set back from Passeig de Gràcia, with its own garden, a real luxury in the tightly planned grid of the Eixample. Notice the calm, symmetrical stone facade and its classical details. While some neighbours on this avenue chose bold Modernista curves, this house looks back to older, more formal European models. Today it's a public information centre about Catalonia. Admire it quietly; this is a real neighbourhood where people live and work.","long":"Turn and face the palace behind the palm trees. It was built at the turn of the twentieth century, between 1898 and 1903, as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat, and its design is credited to the French architect Henri Grandpierre. Notice first where it stands. Instead of rising straight from the pavement like most buildings on Passeig de Gràcia, it sits back from the avenue behind its own garden. In the tightly planned grid of the Eixample, laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà, that kind of space was a real luxury, and a clear sign of wealth. Now let your eyes travel across the facade. It's built of stone, calm and symmetrical, one side mirroring the other. Look for the classical details, and notice how orderly and measured everything feels, with nothing twisting or bulging. While some neighbours on this avenue chose bold Modernista curves, colour and fantasy, this house looks back to older, more formal European models. Its style is usually described as neoclassical, and it would not look out of place on a boulevard in Paris. Hold that contrast in mind as you walk on, because Passeig de Gràcia is one of the best places in the city to compare these two very different ideas of what a grand house should be. Today the palace is a public information centre about Catalonia. Admire it quietly; this is a real neighbourhood where people live and work.","new_facts":["The palace was built between 1898 and 1903.","It was built as the home of Robert Robert i Surís, a Catalan aristocrat.","Its design is credited to the French architect Henri Grandpierre.","The Eixample grid was laid out by the engineer Ildefons Cerdà.","The palace's style is described as neoclassical."]}},"tip":"The garden is free: take a break here before going back into the crowds on Passeig de Gràcia."},"platja-sant-sebastia":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This is one of Barcelona's oldest beaches. In the 19th century, when sea bathing became fashionable, the city's first bathing clubs opened here, and some historic swimming clubs still stand at the edge of the sand. Now look to the far end: that sail-shaped hotel marks where the city ends and open sea begins.","normal":"This is one of the oldest beaches in the city: in the 19th century, Barcelona's first bathing clubs opened here, when sea bathing became fashionable. Today some of the city's historic swimming clubs still stand at the edge of the sand. Look to the end of the beach: the sail-shaped hotel marks where the city now ends and the open sea begins.","long":"This is one of the oldest beaches in Barcelona, and it holds a small story about how the city learned to enjoy the sea. In the 19th century, sea bathing became fashionable, and Barcelona's first bathing clubs opened right here on this sand. Picture the first bathers wading into these same waves, trying out a fashion that was brand new. Today, some of the city's historic swimming clubs still stand at the edge of the beach. One of them, the Club Natació Barcelona, was founded in 1907, and it's one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain. Notice how these low buildings sit between the sand and the city, with their pools and terraces, still busy with members, just as they've been for generations. They're private clubs, so enjoy them from the beach. Just inland is La Barceloneta, a neighbourhood laid out in the 18th century in a grid of long, narrow streets, where many people live. Now look to the very end of the beach. That tall, sail-shaped building is a hotel, designed by the architect Ricardo Bofill and opened in 2009. Its glass front reflects the sky, and on a bright day it can look almost like a sail filling with wind. It marks the point where the city now ends and the open sea begins. Take a moment to hold it all in one view: the old clubs, the new tower, and between them the same stretch of sand where Barcelona first went bathing.","new_facts":["The Club Natació Barcelona, one of the historic swimming clubs on this beach, was founded in 1907 and is one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain.","The club has pools and terraces and is private, for members.","La Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century in a grid of long, narrow streets.","The sail-shaped hotel was designed by the architect Ricardo Bofill.","The sail-shaped hotel opened in 2009.","The hotel has a glass front that reflects the sky."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Take a breath and look out to sea. You're on one of Barcelona's oldest beaches, where the city's first bathing clubs opened in the 19th century. Follow the sand to its far end, to the sail-shaped hotel: that's where the city stops and the open Mediterranean begins. Then just listen to the waves.","normal":"Take a breath and look out to sea. You're standing on one of the oldest beaches in Barcelona, where the city's first bathing clubs opened in the 19th century, when sea bathing became fashionable. Let your eyes follow the sand to its far end, where a sail-shaped hotel rises. That's where the city stops and the open Mediterranean begins. If you'd like a pause, find a quiet spot near the water's edge, listen to the waves, and simply watch the light move across the sea.","long":"Take a slow breath and look out to sea. Feel the breeze, notice the smell of salt, and let the sound of the waves settle around you. You're standing on one of the oldest beaches in Barcelona. In the 19th century, when sea bathing became fashionable, the city's first bathing clubs opened here, and people came to this shore to try the new fashion for themselves. Some of the city's historic swimming clubs still sit along the edge of the sand today, low buildings facing the same water. Now let your eyes travel along the beach to its far end, where a tall, sail-shaped hotel rises above the shore. It was designed by the architect Ricardo Bofill, and its glass front changes with the weather, silver on a cloudy morning, bright blue on a clear afternoon. That's where the city stops and the open Mediterranean begins. Beyond it there's nothing but water and horizon. Notice how the colour of the sea shifts as it moves away from you, paler near the sand and deeper further out. Watch the small waves folding onto the shore, one after another. If you'd like a pause, find a quiet spot near the water's edge and sit for a while. Listen to the waves, and simply watch the light move across the sea. There's nowhere you need to be for the next few minutes.","new_facts":["The sail-shaped hotel was designed by the architect Ricardo Bofill.","The hotel has a glass front that reflects the sky and changes with the weather."]},"local":{"short":"Look along the edge of the sand: some of these buildings are Barcelona's historic swimming clubs, still very much in use. For many neighbours this is simply their local beach. La Barceloneta, just inland, is a real neighbourhood where people live and sleep, so keep your voice low in its narrow streets.","normal":"Look along the edge of the sand. Some of the buildings here are Barcelona's historic swimming clubs, and they're still very much in use. Their story goes back to the 19th century, when the city's first bathing clubs opened on this beach as sea bathing became fashionable. For many neighbours, this is simply their local beach: a swim, a walk with friends, a chat by the water. La Barceloneta, just inland, is a real neighbourhood where people live and sleep, so keep your voice low as you head back through its narrow streets.","long":"Look along the edge of the sand. Some of the buildings you can see here are Barcelona's historic swimming clubs, and they're still very much in use. One of them, the Club Natació Barcelona, was founded in 1907 and is one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain. These aren't museums: members come here to train, swim and meet, and they're private, so enjoy them from the beach rather than wandering in. Their story goes back to the 19th century, when the city's first bathing clubs opened on this beach as sea bathing became fashionable. For generations since, locals have made this shore part of their daily routine. For many neighbours, this is simply their local beach: a swim, a walk with friends, a chat by the water. Look around and you may spot them: someone with a towel over the shoulder heading for the clubs, older friends sitting together on the promenade, people walking at an easy pace. Give them space, and if you take photos, keep residents out of the frame unless they're happy to be in it. La Barceloneta, just inland, is a real neighbourhood where people live and sleep. It was laid out in the 18th century in a regular grid of long, narrow streets, with homes close together and washing often hanging from the balconies. Sound carries easily between those buildings, so keep your voice low as you head back through them. A quiet walk is the best thanks you can give the people who call this place home.","new_facts":["The Club Natació Barcelona, one of the historic swimming clubs on this beach, was founded in 1907 and is one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain.","The swimming clubs are private, for members.","La Barceloneta was laid out in the 18th century in a regular grid of long, narrow streets.","In La Barceloneta, homes stand close together and washing often hangs from the balconies."]}},"tip":"Wear a top when you walk back into the neighbourhood streets: it's a local request, and the law."},"font-canaletes":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This iron fountain at the top of the Rambla holds a legend: drink from it and you'll always come back to Barcelona. It's also where Barça fans gather after every big win, singing late into the night. Look closely and you may see a neighbour simply filling a bottle.","normal":"This iron fountain at the top of the Rambla carries two traditions. The first is a legend: whoever drinks from it will always come back to Barcelona. The second belongs to football. For decades, Barça fans have gathered right here to celebrate every big win, singing and waving flags until late at night. On an ordinary day it's just a fountain where neighbours fill a bottle; on a title night, the whole Rambla turns blue and red.","long":"You're standing at the top of the Rambla, beside one of Barcelona's best-loved street fixtures: the Font de Canaletes, a slender iron fountain that carries two very different traditions. Just up the slope lies Plaça de Catalunya, the big square at the heart of the city, and ahead the Rambla runs downhill under its plane trees toward the old port and the sea. The first tradition is a legend. People say that whoever drinks from this fountain will always come back to Barcelona. It's a gentle promise, and you'll often see visitors bend down for a sip with a half-embarrassed smile, as if they don't quite believe it but don't want to take any chances. The second tradition belongs to football. For decades, FC Barcelona supporters have gathered right here to celebrate every big win. On a title night, this quiet corner fills from wall to wall. Fans sing, wave flags and hold their scarves high, and the celebration carries on late into the night. The whole Rambla turns into a river of blue and red, the colours locals call blaugrana. On most days, though, the fountain is simply useful. Neighbours stop to fill a bottle on their way home, and the windows above the Rambla belong to people who live here. So if you'd like to test the legend, wait your turn, take your sip, and step aside for the next person. That small courtesy is part of the tradition too.","new_facts":["Plaça de Catalunya is right beside the top of the Rambla, at the heart of the city","The Rambla runs downhill toward the old port and the sea","The Rambla is lined with plane trees","Barça's blue and red colours are called blaugrana"]},"sports":{"short":"Look at this slender iron fountain. It seems modest, but it's the place Barça fans claim as their own. After every big win, supporters stream up here, singing and waving flags late into the night, until the whole Rambla is washed in blue and red.","normal":"Look at the slender iron fountain in front of you. On a quiet afternoon it seems almost too modest for its fame, yet this is the place Barça fans claim as their own. For decades, after every big win, supporters have streamed up to this very spot at the top of the Rambla, singing and waving flags late into the night. Picture the whole street around you washed in blue and red, scarves held high, voices echoing under the trees. Tonight it may be calm, but this fountain is always waiting for the next title.","long":"Look at the slender iron fountain in front of you. On a quiet afternoon it seems almost too modest for its fame, just a metal column at the top of the Rambla, a few steps from Plaça de Catalunya. Yet this is the spot FC Barcelona's fans claim as their own. The club's stadium is far from here, across the city, but for decades this is where supporters have come to celebrate. After every big win, they stream up the Rambla and in from the side streets toward this very fountain. Picture it. The plane trees overhead, the street packed shoulder to shoulder, scarves held high, flags waving, and the whole crowd singing late into the night. If a match is on, you may even hear cheers drifting out of nearby bars. The colours are blue and red, which locals call blaugrana, and on a title night they seem to wash over everything around you. Now look again at how small the fountain really is. It doesn't need to be grand. What matters is the habit, kept up for generations, of meeting in the same place to share the same joy. Remember, too, that people live in the buildings above, and on ordinary nights this street is simply their neighbourhood. So enjoy the calm while it lasts. Tonight it may be just a fountain where someone fills a bottle on the way home, but it is always waiting for the next title.","new_facts":["The fountain is a few steps from Plaça de Catalunya","FC Barcelona's stadium is far from here, across the city","The Rambla is lined with plane trees","Barça's blue and red colours are called blaugrana"]},"local":{"short":"Watch who comes to this iron fountain. Often it's a neighbour filling a bottle on the way home. Locals say anyone who drinks here will always return to Barcelona. If you'd like a sip, wait your turn, drink, and leave room for the next person. The windows above are real homes.","normal":"Stand back for a moment and watch who comes to this iron fountain. Often it isn't a visitor at all, but a neighbour filling a bottle on the way home. People here say that anyone who drinks from it will always come back to Barcelona, so a sip becomes a small, quiet ritual. Notice how people pause, drink and move on without fuss. The windows above the Rambla belong to real homes. If you'd like a taste, wait your turn, take your sip, and leave room for the next person.","long":"Stand back for a moment, out of the flow of people, and watch who comes to this iron fountain. Often it isn't a visitor at all. It's a neighbour on the way home, filling a bottle before heading into one of the side streets. This is the very top of the Rambla, where it meets Plaça de Catalunya, one of the busiest crossroads in the city, and yet for the people who live nearby it is still a place of small everyday errands. People here say that anyone who drinks from this fountain will always come back to Barcelona. You'll see how that turns a sip into a quiet little ritual. Notice how people pause, drink and move on without any fuss. Nobody makes a show of it. Look up at the windows above the Rambla, with their balconies. Those are real homes, and the people inside hear the crowds below every day, and on football nights, when Barça fans gather here to celebrate a big win, the singing often goes on late. That's worth remembering as you walk this street. Keeping your voice down and stepping aside on the pavement are small kindnesses that people here notice. If you'd like a taste of the legend, wait your turn behind whoever is already there, take your sip, and leave room for the next person. Then, if you can, linger a little longer and simply watch. The fountain is at its most interesting when you see it through the eyes of the people who use it every day.","new_facts":["The top of the Rambla meets Plaça de Catalunya, one of the busiest crossroads in the city","The buildings above the Rambla have balconies"]},"history":{"short":"Notice this iron fountain at the top of the Rambla. A legend says drink from it and you'll always return to Barcelona, and for decades Barça fans have gathered right here after every big win. Generations have stood where you're standing, making their own small promise to return.","normal":"Notice the iron fountain here at the top of the Rambla. It has quietly gathered two kinds of memory. One is a legend, still told today: drink from it and you will always return to Barcelona. The other grew over decades, as football supporters made this exact spot their meeting place for every big Barça victory. So a simple street fountain became a landmark where private wishes and shared celebrations meet. Look around you: generations of locals and travellers have stood just where you are standing now, making their own small promise to return.","long":"Notice the iron fountain here at the top of the Rambla. It looks like an ordinary piece of street furniture, but it has quietly gathered two kinds of memory. Start with where you are. The Rambla below you was once a seasonal stream bed beside the medieval city walls, and today it runs down under the plane trees toward the old port and the sea. Just up the slope lies Plaça de Catalunya, the great square that links the old city with the newer grid of the Eixample. A fountain at this meeting point was bound to see a lot of life. The first memory is a legend, still told today: drink from this fountain and you will always return to Barcelona. Nobody needs to believe it completely. It survives because people keep repeating it, to friends, to children, to visitors like you. The second memory grew over decades, as FC Barcelona supporters made this exact spot their meeting place after every big victory. Here the club's blue and red spill out onto the street. So a simple street fountain became a landmark where private wishes and shared celebrations meet. One person's quiet sip and a whole singing crowd can belong to the same place, years apart or even on the same night. Look around you now: generations of locals and travellers have stood just where you are standing, many of them making their own small promise to return. And the people who live above the Rambla share this spot with all of them, every single day.","new_facts":["The Rambla was once a seasonal stream bed beside the medieval city walls","The Rambla is lined with plane trees and runs down toward the old port and the sea","Plaça de Catalunya links the old city with the grid of the Eixample"]}},"tip":"On match nights this spot gets packed: if you come to celebrate, respect the people who live on the Rambla."},"gimnas-sole":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Nothing about this narrow street suggests history, yet FC Barcelona was born here. On 29 November 1899, the Swiss Joan Gamper, who had advertised in a newspaper for players, met fellow fans at the Gimnàs Solé, a gym on this street, and founded the club. Look for the plaque that recalls that evening.","normal":"Nothing about this narrow street suggests history, yet FC Barcelona was born here. On 29 November 1899, a group of football enthusiasts led by the Swiss Joan Gamper met at the Gimnàs Solé, a gymnasium in this street, and founded the club after Gamper had placed an advert in a sports newspaper looking for players. Today a plaque recalls that evening. From a small gym in the Raval to one of the most famous clubs in the world.","long":"Nothing about this narrow street suggests history, yet FC Barcelona was born here. You're on Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme, in the Raval, and on 29 November 1899 a gymnasium on this street, the Gimnàs Solé, hosted a meeting that would change the city's sporting life. The man behind it was Joan Gamper, a Swiss football enthusiast who had been born Hans Gamper and settled in Barcelona. Football was still a fairly new game here, and he wanted a team. So he placed an advert in a sports newspaper, Los Deportes, asking for players. Those who answered came together in this gym that evening. They were a mixed group of Swiss, English and Catalan enthusiasts, united by a love of a game that many people in the city had barely seen. That night they founded the club. Take a moment to find the plaque that recalls the meeting. It's modest and easy to walk past, and that suits the story. There was no stadium, no crowd and no famous kit yet, just a handful of people in a small gym deciding to play together. Gamper stayed at the heart of the club for years, playing for it and later serving as its president more than once. Today his name lives on in the Joan Gamper Trophy, the pre-season match the club hosts every summer. Look along the street as it is now, with its balconies and front doors. People live here, so keep your voice low and leave doorways clear. From a small gym in the Raval to one of the most famous clubs in the world.","new_facts":["The street is Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme.","Joan Gamper was born Hans Gamper.","The sports newspaper with his advert was Los Deportes.","The founders included Swiss, English and Catalan members.","Football was still a fairly new game in Barcelona at the time.","Gamper played for the club and later served as its president more than once.","The Joan Gamper Trophy, named after him, is a pre-season match the club hosts every summer."]},"sports":{"short":"Every trophy, every chant of FC Barcelona leads back to this narrow street. On 29 November 1899, Joan Gamper, a Swiss fan who had advertised in a sports newspaper for players, met fellow enthusiasts at the Gimnàs Solé and founded the club. Look for the plaque, and remember people live here.","normal":"Every trophy, every chant, every celebration of FC Barcelona leads back to a small gym on this narrow street. Look for the plaque that marks the spot. A Swiss football enthusiast, Joan Gamper, had placed an advert in a sports newspaper looking for players. On 29 November 1899, he and a group of fellow fans met here, at the Gimnàs Solé, and that evening they founded the club. It's a humble starting line for one of the most famous clubs in the world, tucked into a Raval street where people still live today.","long":"Every trophy, every chant, every celebration of FC Barcelona leads back to a small gym on this narrow street. Look for the plaque that marks the spot. Here, on Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme, stood the Gimnàs Solé. The story starts with Joan Gamper, a Swiss football enthusiast, born Hans Gamper, who had made Barcelona his home. He wanted to play, but he needed teammates, so he placed an advert in a sports newspaper, Los Deportes, looking for players. On 29 November 1899, he and a group of fellow fans met here, and that evening they founded the club. Picture that first group of Swiss, English and Catalan players, a mixed bunch brought together by one advert and a shared passion. There was no stadium, no supporters and no anthem, just a gym and a plan. Gamper didn't only organise. He pulled on the shirt himself in the early years, and later served as the club's president more than once. His name is still honoured every summer with the Joan Gamper Trophy, the pre-season match the club hosts. So the next time you hear a stadium roar, think of this quiet street. It's a humble starting line for one of the most famous clubs in the world, tucked into a Raval street where people still live today. Have a look at the balconies and doorways around you and take your photo of the plaque. Then keep your voice down and leave the entrances clear for the neighbours.","new_facts":["The street is Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme.","Joan Gamper was born Hans Gamper.","The sports newspaper with his advert was Los Deportes.","The founders included Swiss, English and Catalan members.","Gamper played for the club in its early years and later served as its president more than once.","The Joan Gamper Trophy, named after him, is a pre-season match the club hosts every summer."]},"history":{"short":"Notice how ordinary this street looks. Yet on the evening of 29 November 1899, a small group led by Joan Gamper, a Swiss who had advertised for players in a sports newspaper, gathered at the Gimnàs Solé here and founded FC Barcelona. Find the plaque that remembers them.","normal":"Notice how ordinary this street looks. Yet on the evening of 29 November 1899, a small group gathered in a gymnasium here, the Gimnàs Solé. Their leader was Joan Gamper, a Swiss football enthusiast, who had placed an advert in a sports newspaper asking for players. The people who met that evening founded FC Barcelona. Find the plaque that remembers them. History doesn't always happen in palaces or grand squares. Sometimes it starts in a modest gym in the Raval, a neighbourhood where everyday life carries on all around you.","long":"Notice how ordinary this street looks. This is Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme, in the Raval, and on the evening of 29 November 1899 a small group gathered in a gymnasium here, the Gimnàs Solé. Their leader was Joan Gamper, a Swiss football enthusiast, born Hans Gamper, who had settled in Barcelona. At the time, football was still a fairly new game in the city, known to relatively few people. Gamper had placed an advert in a sports newspaper, Los Deportes, asking for players. The people who answered, Swiss, English and Catalans among them, met that evening and founded FC Barcelona. Find the plaque that remembers them. It's easy to miss, and that is part of its charm. There was no ceremony and no grand building, just a gym in a working neighbourhood. Gamper went on to shape the club for years, first as a player and later as its president more than once, and today the Joan Gamper Trophy, played every summer, carries his name. Think about how much has grown from that one meeting, and then look around at the street itself. The Raval has long been a place where people arriving in the city made their lives, and Gamper was a newcomer too. History doesn't always happen in palaces or grand squares. Sometimes it starts in a modest gym in the Raval, a neighbourhood where everyday life carries on all around you, so please keep your voice low and leave doorways clear.","new_facts":["The street is Carrer de Montjuïc del Carme.","Joan Gamper was born Hans Gamper.","Football was still a fairly new game in Barcelona at the time.","The sports newspaper with his advert was Los Deportes.","The founders included Swiss, English and Catalan members.","Gamper played for the club and later served as its president more than once.","The Joan Gamper Trophy, named after him, is played every summer.","The Raval has long been home to people arriving in the city."]}},"tip":"It's a residential street: look for the plaque quietly and don't block doorways."},"nou-sardenya":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Barcelona has another historic football club, and this is its home. CE Europa was founded here in Gràcia in 1907. Look at how its little stadium is squeezed between apartment blocks: neighbours can almost watch matches from their balconies. To its loyal fans, Europa is part of Gràcia itself.","normal":"Barcelona has another historic football club, and this is its home. CE Europa was founded in 1907 in Gràcia, and in 1929 it played in Spain's very first national league, alongside the biggest clubs in the country. Its small stadium is squeezed between apartment blocks, so neighbours can almost watch matches from their balconies. Europa is a true neighbourhood club, with loyal, passionate fans who see it as part of Gràcia's identity.","long":"Barcelona has another historic football club, and you're standing at its home. This is Nou Sardenya, the ground of CE Europa. The name is easy to unpack: nou is Catalan for new, and Sardenya is the street the stadium sits on. Europa was founded right here in Gràcia in 1907, not long after Gràcia stopped being a town of its own and became part of Barcelona. That independent spirit still runs through the neighbourhood, and through this club. In 1923, Europa reached the final of the Spanish Cup. Then, in 1929, it played in Spain's very first national league, one of just ten clubs, alongside the biggest names in the country. Few people walking past today would guess that this modest ground belongs to one of the clubs from that very first season. Take a look at the stadium itself. It's small, and it's squeezed between apartment blocks so tightly that neighbours can almost watch matches from their balconies. Glance up and imagine a match day: the shouts from the stands, the flags, perhaps a resident leaning over the railing with a coffee, catching a free view of the game. There are no grand approaches here, just the ordinary streets of Gràcia running right up to the gates. Europa is a true neighbourhood club. Its loyal, passionate fans see it as part of Gràcia's identity, as much a part of local life as the squares and corner bars nearby. Remember, these are people's homes, so enjoy the view quietly before you move on.","new_facts":["The stadium is named Nou Sardenya; 'nou' means 'new' in Catalan and Sardenya is the street it stands on.","Gràcia was a separate town before becoming part of Barcelona, shortly before 1907.","CE Europa reached the final of the Spanish Cup in 1923.","The first Spanish national league in 1929 had ten clubs."]},"sports":{"short":"Here's a surprise: Barcelona has another historic football club, and you're standing at its home. CE Europa, founded in Gràcia in 1907, played in Spain's very first national league in 1929. Now look at the stadium, tucked tightly between apartment blocks. Football here feels close and personal.","normal":"Here's a surprise: Barcelona has another historic football club, and you're standing at its home. CE Europa was founded in Gràcia in 1907, and in 1929 it played in Spain's very first national league, sharing that stage with the biggest clubs in the country. Now look at the stadium itself. It's small, tucked tightly between apartment blocks, nothing like the giant arenas you might expect. That's the charm. Football here feels close and personal, and the fans are loyal and passionate, carrying more than a century of history on their shoulders.","long":"Here's a surprise: Barcelona has another historic football club, and you're standing at its home. CE Europa was founded here in Gràcia in 1907, and it was never just a local side making up the numbers. In 1923 it reached the final of the Spanish Cup. Then, in 1929, it took its place in Spain's very first national league. Only ten clubs made up that first season, and Europa shared the stage with the biggest names in Spanish football, including its city neighbours FC Barcelona, who went on to win that first title. Think about that for a moment: one of the original ten, playing out of this corner of Gràcia. Now look at the stadium itself, Nou Sardenya. It's small, tucked tightly between apartment blocks, nothing like the giant arenas you might expect in a city famous for football. There are no towering stands visible from across town. Instead, the ground slots into the streets like any other building on the block. That's the charm. Football here feels close and personal. In a ground this size, the noise of the crowd stays near, and the game feels almost within touching distance. The fans are loyal and passionate, carrying more than a century of history on their shoulders. Whatever the table says in any given season, that history is what keeps them coming back. Enjoy the moment, and keep it neighbourly, because people live right around this ground.","new_facts":["CE Europa reached the final of the Spanish Cup in 1923.","The first Spanish national league season in 1929 had ten clubs.","FC Barcelona won the first Spanish league title in 1929.","The stadium is called Nou Sardenya."]},"local":{"short":"Look up at the balconies around you. From some, neighbours can almost watch the matches, because this small stadium, Nou Sardenya, home of CE Europa, is squeezed right in among their homes. For locals, the club is part of Gràcia's identity. People live all around here, so please keep your voice low.","normal":"Look up at the balconies on the apartment blocks around you. From some of them, neighbours can almost watch the matches, because this small stadium is squeezed right in among their homes. This is Nou Sardenya, home of CE Europa, a club founded here in Gràcia in 1907. For many locals, Europa isn't just a team. It's part of the neighbourhood's identity, followed by loyal, passionate fans. Remember that people live, sleep and work all around this ground, so keep your voice low as you wander these streets, just as a good neighbour would.","long":"Look up at the balconies on the apartment blocks around you. From some of them, neighbours can almost watch the matches, because this small stadium is squeezed right in among their homes. Imagine hearing a goal celebrated just beyond your kitchen window. This is Nou Sardenya, home of CE Europa, a club founded here in Gràcia in 1907. The stadium takes its name from the street it stands on, Carrer de Sardenya, and nou simply means new in Catalan. To understand why the club matters so much, it helps to know that Gràcia was once a separate town, only absorbed into Barcelona at the end of the nineteenth century. Many people here still feel a strong sense of being a place apart, with its own squares, its own festivals and its own character. For many locals, Europa isn't just a team. It's part of that neighbourhood identity, followed by loyal, passionate fans who carry the club's long history with them. Take a moment to notice how ordinary the surroundings are: shutters, balconies, front doors, the everyday life of a residential street, with a football ground right in the middle of it. That closeness is what makes this place special, and it's also why your behaviour matters here. Remember that people live, sleep and work all around this ground, perhaps on a night shift, perhaps with a baby asleep upstairs. So keep your voice low as you wander these streets, take photos without pointing your camera into windows, and move along as a good neighbour would.","new_facts":["The stadium is named after Carrer de Sardenya, the street it stands on; 'nou' means 'new' in Catalan.","Gràcia was a separate town until it was absorbed into Barcelona at the end of the nineteenth century.","Gràcia has its own squares and festivals."]}},"tip":"If there's a home match, buying a ticket is a great way to see local football up close."},"club-natacio":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Picture Christmas Day in the port: brave swimmers jumping into the cold winter sea while crowds cheer from the quay. That's the Copa Nadal, organised by this club since its early years. Look at the club here on Barceloneta beach, founded in 1907 and one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain.","normal":"This club on Barceloneta beach was founded in 1907 and is one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain. It has produced generations of swimmers and water polo players, a sport Barcelona has always been strong at. Every Christmas Day since its early years, the club has organised a traditional swim in the port, the Copa Nadal: brave swimmers jump into the cold winter sea while crowds cheer from the quay.","long":"You're standing at Club Natació Barcelona, right on the edge of Barceloneta beach. It was founded in 1907, which makes it one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain, and for more than a century it has been a home for people who love the water. Take a moment to look at the club and the beach around it, and imagine this shoreline more than a hundred years ago.\n\nOver the generations, the club has produced swimmers and water polo players, a sport Barcelona has always been strong at. It's a tough game. Players can't touch the bottom, so they tread water for the whole match while passing and shooting. When Barcelona hosted the Olympic Games in 1992, water polo was part of the programme, so the world got to see the sport in this city.\n\nBut the club's most famous tradition happens in winter. Every Christmas Day since the club's early years, it has organised a swim in the port called the Copa Nadal, which simply means Christmas Cup in Catalan. Brave swimmers jump into the cold winter sea while crowds gather on the quay to cheer them on. Look toward the port and picture the scene. People are bundled up in coats and scarves along the edge, cheering as the swimmers brave the water and race for the finish.\n\nBarceloneta itself is a real neighbourhood, laid out in the eighteenth century, where locals still swim and train. So enjoy the view, keep your voice low, and give them room.","new_facts":["Barcelona hosted the Olympic Games in 1992, and water polo was part of the programme.","In water polo, players cannot touch the bottom and tread water throughout the match.","\"Copa Nadal\" means \"Christmas Cup\" in Catalan.","The Barceloneta neighbourhood was laid out in the eighteenth century."]},"sports":{"short":"Imagine plunging into the port on Christmas Day while crowds cheer from the quay. That's the Copa Nadal, organised by this club since its early years. Look at the club here on Barceloneta beach: founded in 1907, it's trained generations of swimmers and water polo players. Locals train here, so give them room.","normal":"Imagine plunging into the port on Christmas Day while crowds cheer from the quay. That's the Copa Nadal, a winter swim this club has organised every Christmas Day since its early years. Look at the club right here on the edge of Barceloneta beach. Founded in 1907, it's one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain, and generations of swimmers and water polo players have trained here, in a sport Barcelona has always excelled at. Barceloneta is a real neighbourhood where locals still swim and train, so enjoy the view quietly and give them room.","long":"Imagine plunging into the port on Christmas Day while crowds cheer from the quay. That's the Copa Nadal, the Christmas Cup, a winter swim this club has organised every Christmas Day since its early years. It's a race, but it's also a test of nerve. The sea is cold in December and the swimmers go in anyway, to applause from friends, families and curious passers-by.\n\nNow look at the club itself, right here on the edge of Barceloneta beach. Founded in 1907, it's one of the oldest swimming clubs in Spain. Among its founders was Bernat Picornell, a swimmer whose name you'll also find on the big outdoor pools up on Montjuïc hill.\n\nGenerations of swimmers and water polo players have trained here, in a sport Barcelona has always excelled at. If you've never watched water polo, it's brutally demanding. Players can't touch the bottom, so they tread water for the entire match while fighting for position, passing and shooting with one hand. When Barcelona hosted the 1992 Olympic Games, water polo was on the programme. Four years later, Spain's men's team won Olympic gold in Atlanta. Clubs like this one are where that kind of strength is built, one training session at a time.\n\nBarceloneta is a real neighbourhood where locals still swim and train, so enjoy the view quietly and give them room. If you see someone heading for the water with a towel over their shoulder, you're watching a love of the sea that this club has shared for more than a century.","new_facts":["\"Copa Nadal\" means \"Christmas Cup\" in Catalan.","Bernat Picornell was among the founders of Club Natació Barcelona.","The Bernat Picornell pools on Montjuïc are named after him.","In water polo, players cannot touch the bottom and may handle the ball with only one hand.","Barcelona hosted the 1992 Olympic Games, which included water polo.","Spain's men's water polo team won Olympic gold at the 1996 Atlanta Games."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Look out at the sea and imagine it in midwinter. Every Christmas Day, swimmers from this club leap into the chilly port while people cheer from the quay. Today, just enjoy it from dry land: watch the light on the waves and feel the breeze. People live here, so keep voices soft.","normal":"Look out at the sea in front of you and imagine it in midwinter. Every Christmas Day, swimmers from this club leap into the chilly water of the port while people cheer from the quay, a tradition called the Copa Nadal. Today you can simply enjoy the sea from dry land. Notice how the light plays on the waves, feel the breeze, and listen to the water. The club, founded in 1907, sits right on Barceloneta beach, in a neighbourhood where people live, so keep voices soft and find a calm spot to pause.","long":"Look out at the sea in front of you and imagine it in midwinter. Every Christmas Day, swimmers from this club leap into the chilly water of the port while people cheer from the quay. The tradition is called the Copa Nadal, which means Christmas Cup in Catalan. Picture the crowd in coats and scarves, the splash as the swimmers go in, and the shouts of encouragement from the edge.\n\nToday you can simply enjoy the Mediterranean from dry land. Take a slow breath and notice how the light plays on the waves. Depending on the time of day, the water can look silver, deep blue or almost green near the shore. Feel the breeze on your face and listen for a while. You might hear the rhythm of the waves, the gulls overhead, and perhaps the creak of boats in the port nearby.\n\nThe club itself is close by, sitting right on Barceloneta beach. It was founded in 1907, so for well over a century its members have been heading out into this same sea. The neighbourhood around it was laid out in the eighteenth century, and its streets of homes run right down toward the water.\n\nBarceloneta is a place where people live, not just a view, so keep voices soft and find a calm spot to pause. Sit on a bench or at the edge of the sand if there's room. Give yourself a minute with nothing to do but watch the sea.","new_facts":["\"Copa Nadal\" means \"Christmas Cup\" in Catalan.","The sea here is the Mediterranean.","The Barceloneta neighbourhood was laid out in the eighteenth century."]}},"tip":"The club is for members, but the beach in front of it is open to everyone."},"port-olimpic":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This whole neighbourhood was built for the 1992 Olympic Games, on land once covered by old factories and railway lines. Look up at Frank Gehry's giant golden fish, shining in the sun above the beach. The Games changed Barcelona for ever: they opened the city to the sea.","normal":"This whole neighbourhood was built for the 1992 Olympic Games. Old factories and railway lines were cleared to create the Olympic Village, where the athletes lived, and this port, where the sailing events were based. Look up at the two towers rising over the beach and at Frank Gehry's giant golden fish sculpture shining in the sun. The Games changed Barcelona for ever: they opened the city to the sea.","long":"This whole neighbourhood was built for the 1992 Olympic Games. Before that, this stretch of coast was industrial land, with old factories and railway lines running between the city and the shore. People in Barcelona could live a short walk from the Mediterranean and still barely reach it. For the Games, all of that was cleared away. In its place came the Olympic Village, where the athletes lived, and this port, where the sailing events were based. Take a moment to look around the harbour. The boats tied up at the moorings float along a shoreline that, not so long ago, you couldn't easily reach from the streets behind you. Now look up at the two towers rising over the beach. One is the Hotel Arts and the other is the Torre Mapfre, an office building, and they stand at the same height, like a gateway to the seafront. Near the foot of the towers, find Frank Gehry's giant golden fish. Up close you can see it isn't solid at all: it's made of strips of metal woven together like a lattice, so the light passes through it and it seems to shimmer as the sun moves. From some angles it looks as though it's swimming along the promenade. The Games changed Barcelona for ever: they opened the city to the sea. The athletes left long ago, and the streets behind you are now a real neighbourhood where people live and work, so enjoy the view and keep your voice down as you wander through.","new_facts":["The two towers are the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre.","The Torre Mapfre is an office building.","The two towers are the same height.","Gehry's fish is made of woven metal strips forming a lattice that light passes through."]},"sports":{"short":"Imagine this harbour in the summer of 1992, filled with racing sailboats. This port was built as the base for the Olympic sailing events. Look out at the moorings and picture the crews preparing their boats and heading out to sea. Today this is a real neighbourhood, so enjoy it quietly.","normal":"Imagine this harbour in the summer of 1992, filled with racing sailboats. This port was built as the base for the Olympic sailing events, and the streets behind you were the Olympic Village, where athletes from around the world lived during the Games. Look out at the moorings and picture the crews preparing to head out to sea. Then look up at Frank Gehry's giant golden fish, glinting above the waterfront. Today the athletes are long gone and this is a real neighbourhood, so enjoy the scene quietly as residents go about their day.","long":"Imagine this harbour in the summer of 1992, filled with racing sailboats. This port was built as the base for the Olympic sailing events, and the streets behind you were the Olympic Village, where athletes from around the world lived during the Games. Before any of that, this was a strip of old factories and railway lines, and the shore was hard to reach from the city. It was all cleared to make room for the Games. Look out at the moorings and picture the crews getting ready: checking ropes, rigging sails, studying the wind before heading out to the open sea. This harbour was where each day on the water began and ended. Feel the breeze on your face, the same kind of sea wind the sailors would have been reading as they left. Then look up at Frank Gehry's giant golden fish, glinting above the waterfront. It's made of strips of metal woven like a lattice, so it catches the light and seems to change as you move. Behind it rise two towers of the same height, the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre. The Games left Barcelona with a seafront it had never really had before. Today the athletes are long gone and the old village is a real neighbourhood, with homes and everyday life. So enjoy the scene quietly as residents go about their day, and if you're tempted to stroll through the streets behind you, remember that this is their home.","new_facts":["Gehry's fish is made of woven metal strips forming a lattice.","The two towers are the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre.","The two towers are the same height."]},"outdoors":{"short":"Notice how the light bounces off the water, then catches Frank Gehry's giant golden fish shining above the beach. This open seafront was once hidden behind factories and railway lines, cleared for the 1992 Olympic Games. Pause, feel the breeze, and remember people live just behind you.","normal":"Notice how the light bounces off the water here, and then catches Frank Gehry's giant golden fish, shining in the sun above the beach. It's hard to believe this open seafront was once hidden behind old factories and railway lines. They were cleared for the 1992 Olympic Games, and the city gained a way down to the sea. Find a spot to pause, feel the breeze, and watch the boats bob in the port. Look up at the two towers rising over the sand. Remember that people live just behind you, so keep things calm and relaxed.","long":"Notice how the light bounces off the water here, and then catches Frank Gehry's giant golden fish, shining in the sun above the beach. Look a little closer and you'll see the fish isn't a solid shape at all. It's made of strips of metal woven together like a lattice, so the sky shows through it and its colour shifts with the time of day and the clouds passing over. It's hard to believe this open seafront was once hidden behind old factories and railway lines. They were cleared for the 1992 Olympic Games, and the city gained a way down to the sea. Find a spot to pause, perhaps on the edge of the promenade, and feel the breeze coming in off the Mediterranean. Listen for the small sounds of the port: ropes tapping against masts, water slapping gently on the hulls. Watch the boats bob at their moorings, and notice how the harbour walls keep the water inside calm while the open sea beyond keeps moving. Now look up at the two towers rising over the sand. They're the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre, and they stand at the same height, so from down here they frame the sky like a pair of giant posts. Turn slowly and take in the whole sweep of the coast, the beach stretching away on either side. Remember that people live just behind you, in the streets of the old Olympic Village, so keep things calm and relaxed, and leave the seafront as peaceful as you found it.","new_facts":["Gehry's fish is made of woven metal strips forming a lattice that the sky shows through.","The two towers are the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre.","The two towers are the same height."]},"history":{"short":"Picture old factories and railway lines cutting the city off from its own shore. All of it was cleared for the 1992 Olympic Games. Look up at the two towers rising over the beach. Barcelona opened itself to the sea, and the athletes' village became a neighbourhood where people still live.","normal":"Stand here and picture a very different scene: old factories and railway lines cutting the city off from its own shore. All of that was cleared away for the 1992 Olympic Games. In its place came the Olympic Village, built to house the athletes, and this port, base for the sailing events. Look up at the two towers rising over the beach, and at Frank Gehry's golden fish shining in the sun. The Games were a turning point: Barcelona opened itself to the sea. Once the athletes left, the village became a real neighbourhood, and people still live here today.","long":"Stand here and picture a very different scene: old factories and railway lines cutting the city off from its own shore. This part of the city had grown up around industry, and the neighbouring district of Poblenou was so full of factories that it became known as the Catalan Manchester. All of that was cleared away for the 1992 Olympic Games. In its place came the Olympic Village, built to house the athletes, and this port, base for the sailing events. The planners did more than build for a few weeks of sport: they reshaped the whole relationship between the city and its coastline. Look around and try to imagine rail tracks and factory walls where people now stroll and cycle along the seafront. Then look up at the two towers rising over the beach. They are the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre, built to the same height, and they became one of the most recognisable images of the new waterfront. Below them, find Frank Gehry's golden fish shining in the sun, a sculpture of woven metal strips that seems to ripple as the light changes. The Games were a turning point: Barcelona opened itself to the sea. Once the athletes left, the village became a real neighbourhood, with the athletes' apartments turned into ordinary homes. People still live here today, so as you take in the history around you, keep your voice low and give residents the same respect you'd want on your own street.","new_facts":["The neighbouring district of Poblenou was so industrial it was nicknamed the Catalan Manchester.","The two towers are the Hotel Arts and the Torre Mapfre.","The two towers are the same height.","Gehry's fish is made of woven metal strips.","The athletes' apartments became ordinary homes after the Games."]}},"tip":"Walk along the promenade towards Barceloneta for one of the best seafront strolls in the city."},"liceu":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Behind this modest façade on the Rambla hides one of Europe's great opera houses, opened in 1847. It has burned down twice, in 1861 and 1994, and each time it came back. Look at that plain front and picture the red and gold auditorium glowing just behind it.","normal":"Behind this modest façade on the Rambla hides one of Europe's great opera houses, opened in 1847. Its story is dramatic: it burned down in 1861, and in 1893 an anarchist threw bombs into the audience during a performance. In 1994 another fire destroyed it again, and the whole city helped rebuild it. It reopened in 1999, and its red and gold auditorium is once again the heart of opera in Barcelona.","long":"Take a look at the building in front of you. From the Rambla it hardly stands out, just a modest façade slipped into the row of buildings. Yet behind it hides one of Europe's great opera houses, the Gran Teatre del Liceu, opened in 1847. Unusually for a grand European opera house, it wasn't built by a king or a court. It was paid for by private shareholders, Barcelona families who bought their boxes, and it rose on the site of a former convent. Its story is as dramatic as anything performed on its stage. In 1861 fire gutted it, and it was rebuilt within about a year. In 1893 came the darkest night: an anarchist threw two bombs from the upper tiers into the audience during a performance of Rossini's William Tell. Only one exploded, but it killed around twenty people. Then, in 1994, a spark during repair work set the stage curtain alight, and the theatre was destroyed again. This time the whole city helped rebuild it, and it reopened in 1999, larger behind the scenes but with its red and gold horseshoe auditorium recreated. Before you move on, glance down at the pavement a little further along the Rambla, towards the market. The colourful circular mosaic set into the ground there is by Joan Miró, and most people walk over it without noticing. And remember, this is a lived-in neighbourhood, so give a little space to the residents passing by on their way home.","new_facts":["The Liceu was funded by private shareholders who bought boxes, not by royalty or a court","It was built on the site of a former convent","After the 1861 fire it was rebuilt and reopened about a year later (1862)","The 1893 attack took place during a performance of Rossini's William Tell","Two bombs were thrown from the upper tiers; only one exploded","The 1893 bombing killed around twenty people","The 1994 fire started when sparks during repair work set the stage curtain alight","The reconstruction made the theatre larger behind the scenes while recreating the horseshoe auditorium","A circular pavement mosaic by Joan Miró lies on the Rambla a short way from the Liceu, towards the Boqueria market"]},"culture":{"short":"Look at this plain façade on the Rambla. Behind it lies one of Europe's great opera houses, home to Barcelona-born stars like Montserrat Caballé and Josep Carreras. Picture the red and gold auditorium hushed before the overture, and leave room on the pavement for neighbours passing by.","normal":"Stand back and look at this rather plain façade on the Rambla. Would you guess that behind it lies one of Europe's great opera houses? Since 1847 this stage has belonged to singers, and Barcelona has given it some of its brightest stars, including Montserrat Caballé and Josep Carreras, both born in this city. Picture the red and gold auditorium on a performance night, the hush before the overture. Opera here isn't only for visitors. Locals have filled these seats for generations, so as you linger, leave room on the pavement for neighbours passing by.","long":"Stand back for a moment and look at this rather plain façade on the Rambla. Would you guess that behind it lies one of Europe's great opera houses? Since 1847 this stage has belonged to singers, and the theatre's very name points to its roots: it grew out of a music school, the Liceu, which still trains musicians in the city today. Barcelona has given opera some of its brightest stars. Montserrat Caballé and Josep Carreras were both born here, and so was the soprano Victoria de los Ángeles, and all three sang on this stage. The city also has a famous passion for Wagner. When the restriction keeping Parsifal at Bayreuth expired, the Liceu was among the very first theatres to stage it, at the start of 1914. Now picture the red and gold auditorium on a performance night, the tiers of boxes curving in a horseshoe, the hush just before the overture. Inside the same building is the Cercle del Liceu, a private club whose rooms hold paintings by the modernista artist Ramon Casas. Opera here isn't only for visitors, though. Locals have filled these seats for generations. It's a lovely reminder that this is a working neighbourhood, not a stage set. Ticket holders, staff and residents all share this stretch of pavement, so as you linger, leave room for the neighbours passing by.","new_facts":["The theatre's name comes from the Liceu music school from which it grew, and the Conservatori del Liceu still trains musicians today","Soprano Victoria de los Ángeles was born in Barcelona","Montserrat Caballé, Josep Carreras and Victoria de los Ángeles all sang at the Liceu","Barcelona has a strong Wagner tradition","The Liceu was among the first theatres outside Bayreuth to stage Parsifal, at the start of 1914, when the restriction expired","The auditorium has a horseshoe of tiered boxes","The Cercle del Liceu, a private club inside the building, holds paintings by Ramon Casas"]},"history":{"short":"This quiet doorway has outlived more drama than most operas sung inside. Opened in 1847, the Liceu burned in 1861, suffered an anarchist bomb attack on its audience in 1893, and burned again in 1994. The city rebuilt it, reopening it in 1999. Look at the façade and imagine all it has survived.","normal":"This quiet doorway has survived more drama than most of the operas sung inside. The Liceu opened in 1847, and just fourteen years later, in 1861, fire gutted it. In 1893 came something darker: an anarchist threw bombs into the audience during a performance. Then, in 1994, flames destroyed the theatre again. This time the whole city rallied to rebuild, and in 1999 the curtain rose once more. Look at the façade in front of you and imagine all it has outlasted, right here on the Rambla, in a neighbourhood people still call home.","long":"This quiet doorway has survived more drama than most of the operas sung inside. The Liceu opened in 1847, built not by a royal court but by private shareholders, on land that had once belonged to a convent. Just fourteen years later, in 1861, fire gutted it. The people of Barcelona were quick to respond, and the rebuilt theatre opened its doors again the following year. In 1893 came something far darker. During a performance of Rossini's William Tell, an anarchist named Santiago Salvador threw two bombs from the upper tiers down into the stalls. Only one exploded, but it killed around twenty people. For the city's wealthy audience, the attack became a symbol of the deep social tensions of those years, and the anarchist was later executed. Then, in 1994, during repair work, sparks set the stage curtain alight, and within hours the theatre was destroyed again. This time the whole city rallied. Public bodies and private donors came together, the theatre expanded into neighbouring buildings to gain modern backstage space, and in 1999 the curtain rose once more on a recreated red and gold auditorium. Look at the façade in front of you and imagine all it has outlasted. Then notice the life around it: the flow of people up and down the Rambla, the Liceu metro entrance nearby, the market a short walk further along. This is still a neighbourhood people call home, so keep a little space for the residents on their way through.","new_facts":["The Liceu was funded by private shareholders rather than a royal court","It was built on land that had belonged to a convent","After the 1861 fire it reopened the following year (1862)","The 1893 bomber was the anarchist Santiago Salvador","The attack happened during Rossini's William Tell","Two bombs were thrown from the upper tiers; only one exploded","Around twenty people were killed in the 1893 bombing","Santiago Salvador was later executed","The 1994 fire was started by sparks during repair work that set the stage curtain alight","The rebuilding was funded by public bodies and private donors","The rebuilt theatre expanded into neighbouring buildings for modern backstage space","There is a Liceu metro station on the Rambla next to the theatre"]},"architecture":{"short":"Here's the trick: from the Rambla this building barely announces itself. Yet behind this modest façade opens a red and gold auditorium curving in a horseshoe of tiers. Rebuilt after the 1994 fire, it reopened in 1999. Pause at the doorway, step aside for neighbours, and picture that glowing hall.","normal":"Here's the trick of this building: from the Rambla it barely announces itself. The façade in front of you is modest, slipped into the line of the street, yet behind it opens one of Europe's great opera houses, a red and gold auditorium curving in a classic horseshoe of tiers. After fire destroyed the theatre in 1994, Barcelona chose to rebuild it, recreating the historic hall while giving it modern stage technology. It reopened in 1999. So take a moment at the doorway, step aside for neighbours on the pavement, and picture that glowing interior hidden just steps away.","long":"Here's the trick of this building: from the Rambla it barely announces itself. The façade in front of you is modest, slipped into the line of the street like any of its neighbours. The theatre went up in 1847 on the site of a former convent, designed by the architect Miquel Garriga i Roca and paid for by private shareholders rather than a royal court. Unlike many European opera houses, it has no grand square of its own to show off from. Yet behind this frontage opens one of Europe's great opera houses: a red and gold auditorium curving in a classic horseshoe, tier upon tier of boxes rising towards the ceiling, with seating for well over two thousand people. After fire destroyed the theatre in 1994, Barcelona decided to recreate the historic hall rather than build something new. The flames spared the façade and the elegant Hall of Mirrors, so what you're looking at is the real nineteenth-century front. The reconstruction, led by the architect Ignasi de Solà-Morales, kept the familiar horseshoe and its gilded decoration, but the theatre also took in neighbouring buildings, giving it a far larger, modern stage and backstage machinery hidden out of sight. It reopened in 1999. So the building you see is really two buildings in one: a nineteenth-century face on the street, and a modern theatre working quietly behind it. Take a moment at the doorway, step aside for neighbours on the pavement, and picture that glowing interior hidden just steps away.","new_facts":["The theatre was built on the site of a former convent","The original architect was Miquel Garriga i Roca","It was funded by private shareholders rather than a royal court","The theatre has no square of its own in front of it","The auditorium seats well over two thousand people","The 1994 fire spared the façade and the Hall of Mirrors","The reconstruction was led by the architect Ignasi de Solà-Morales","The rebuilt theatre took in neighbouring buildings to gain a larger modern stage and backstage"]}},"tip":"Guided tours run most days, and last-minute tickets can be surprisingly affordable."},"teatre-romea":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This theatre on Carrer de l'Hospital has staged plays since 1863, and for much of that time it was the home of theatre in Catalan. Actors kept the language alive on this stage even when it was pushed out of public life. Look at the posters: the shows are still mostly in Catalan.","normal":"This theatre on Carrer de l'Hospital has been staging plays since 1863, and for much of its history it was the home of theatre in Catalan. Generations of Catalan actors and playwrights made their names on this stage, even in years when the language was pushed out of public life. Today it still presents modern productions, mostly in Catalan, for a local audience.","long":"You're standing on Carrer de l'Hospital, in front of the Teatre Romea, where plays have been staged since 1863. The street takes its name from the old Hospital de la Santa Creu, whose Gothic buildings stand nearby on this same street. The theatre itself is named after Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century. For much of its history, the Romea was the home of theatre in Catalan. Generations of Catalan actors and playwrights made their names on this stage, and they kept performing in their own language even in years when it was pushed out of public life. Under the Franco dictatorship, for example, Catalan was heavily restricted in schools, the press and official life, so a stage where it could still be heard mattered a great deal to people here. Now look at the entrance and the posters beside the doors. They tell you what's on right now, because the Romea is a working theatre, not a museum. It still presents modern productions, mostly in Catalan, for a local audience. If you pass by in the evening before a performance, you may see people from the neighbourhood and across the city greeting friends before the doors open. Notice, too, how modestly the building sits in the everyday street front of El Raval. People live all around here, so as you take it in, keep your voice low and leave the doorway clear for them.","new_facts":["Carrer de l'Hospital is named after the Hospital de la Santa Creu, whose Gothic buildings stand nearby on the same street.","The theatre is named after Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century.","Under the Franco dictatorship, Catalan was heavily restricted in schools, the press and official life."]},"culture":{"short":"Look at the entrance of the Teatre Romea here on Carrer de l'Hospital. Plays have filled this stage since 1863, and for much of that time it was the home of theatre in Catalan. Glance at the posters by the door: shows are still mostly in Catalan, watched by Raval neighbours.","normal":"Stand here on Carrer de l'Hospital and look at the entrance of the Teatre Romea. Behind these doors, plays have been performed since 1863, and for much of that time this was the home of theatre in Catalan. Picture the generations of Catalan actors and playwrights who stepped into the lights on this stage and made their names. Now glance at the posters by the door. The Romea still presents modern productions, mostly in Catalan, for a local audience. Come back for a show and you'll share the seats with Raval neighbours on their night out.","long":"Stand here on Carrer de l'Hospital and look at the entrance of the Teatre Romea. Behind these doors, plays have been performed since 1863, and for much of that time this was the home of theatre in Catalan. The theatre carries the name of Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century. Picture the generations of Catalan actors and playwrights who stepped into the lights on this stage and made their names, playing to audiences who came to hear their own language spoken back to them. Even in years when Catalan was pushed out of public life, including the long Franco dictatorship, this stage kept it in the air. Now glance at the posters by the door. Read the titles and names, and you'll see the Romea is no museum piece. It still presents modern productions, mostly in Catalan, for a local audience. If you don't speak Catalan, it can still be a memorable evening, so ask at the box office about what's playing. While you're here, look along the street. The old Hospital de la Santa Creu, a Gothic complex that gave Carrer de l'Hospital its name, stands just nearby, a reminder of how long this street has been part of the city's daily life. Come back for a show and you'll share the seats with Raval neighbours on their night out, so treat it as their evening too.","new_facts":["The theatre is named after Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century.","Catalan was pushed out of public life during the Franco dictatorship.","The Hospital de la Santa Creu, a Gothic complex nearby, gave Carrer de l'Hospital its name."]},"history":{"short":"Notice how modestly this theatre sits on Carrer de l'Hospital. Yet the Teatre Romea, staging plays since 1863, helped carry Catalan through hard times: even when the language was pushed out of public life, actors kept it alive here. Look quietly at the facade, and keep your voice low for residents.","normal":"Notice how modestly this theatre sits along Carrer de l'Hospital, part of the everyday street front of El Raval. Yet this building helped carry a language through difficult times. The Teatre Romea has been staging plays since 1863, and for much of its long life it was the home of theatre in Catalan. Even in years when Catalan was pushed out of public life, generations of actors and playwrights kept it alive on this stage. Take a quiet moment to look at the facade. This is a lived-in neighbourhood, so please keep your voice low as you move on.","long":"Notice how modestly this theatre sits along Carrer de l'Hospital, part of the everyday street front of El Raval. Nothing about it shouts for attention, yet this building helped carry a language through difficult times. The Teatre Romea has been staging plays since 1863, and for much of its long life it was the home of theatre in Catalan. It takes its name from Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century. Over the decades, Catalan was repeatedly pushed out of public life. Under the Franco dictatorship in particular, it was heavily restricted in schools, the press and official business. Even in those years, generations of actors and playwrights kept it alive on this stage, giving the language a public voice. The street around you has deep roots too. Carrer de l'Hospital is named after the Hospital de la Santa Creu, a Gothic complex that stands nearby and served the city's sick for centuries. So as you stand here, you're among some of the oldest layers of Barcelona's daily life. Take a quiet moment to look at the facade and the entrance. Today the Romea still presents modern productions, mostly in Catalan, for a local audience, so the story you're hearing is not finished; it carries on every evening the doors open. Remember that this is a lived-in neighbourhood, where people are going about their own day around you. Please keep your voice low as you move on.","new_facts":["The theatre is named after Julián Romea, a celebrated Spanish actor of the nineteenth century.","Under the Franco dictatorship, Catalan was heavily restricted in schools, the press and official business.","Carrer de l'Hospital is named after the Hospital de la Santa Creu, a Gothic complex nearby that served the city's sick for centuries."]}},"tip":"Check the programme: some productions offer surtitles."},"el-molino":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Look up at the windmill on the façade. This is El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret, inspired by the Moulin Rouge in Paris and open since the early 20th century. The Paral·lel around you was once the city's Broadway. Most of its music halls closed, but El Molino still puts on shows today.","normal":"Look for the windmill on the façade: this is El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret, open since the early 20th century and inspired by the Moulin Rouge in Paris. The Avinguda del Paral·lel around it was once the city's Broadway, lined with theatres, music halls and cafés where workers came to laugh, sing and forget a hard week. El Molino survived when most of them closed, and it still puts on shows today.","long":"Find the windmill on the façade above you. Its sails mark El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret, open since the early twentieth century and inspired by the Moulin Rouge in Paris, the Montmartre cabaret that opened in 1889 under its own red windmill. Barcelona borrowed the idea and made it its own. Now turn and look along the avenue. This is the Avinguda del Paral·lel, and its name has a neat story behind it: the street roughly follows a line of latitude, a parallel, across the map of the city. A century ago it was Barcelona's Broadway. Picture it lined with theatres, music halls and cafés, lights glowing, doors open, crowds spilling onto the pavement. Most of the audiences were working people, who came here after a hard week to laugh, sing along and forget their troubles for a few hours. Over the decades, most of those halls closed, one after another, and the avenue changed around them. El Molino survived. It still puts on shows today, so the windmill above you isn't just a souvenir of the past but the sign of a working stage. Take a moment, too, to notice the green slopes of Montjuïc hill close by. The neighbourhood at its foot is Poble-sec, home to families who have lived here for generations. So as you explore, keep your voice low on the residential side streets, and enjoy the avenue the way the locals do: slowly, and without any rush.","new_facts":["The Moulin Rouge in Paris is in Montmartre and opened in 1889 with a red windmill on its façade","The Avinguda del Paral·lel takes its name from the line of latitude (parallel) it roughly follows","The surrounding neighbourhood, Poble-sec, lies at the foot of Montjuïc hill and is a long-established residential area"]},"culture":{"short":"Look up at the windmill, a nod to the Moulin Rouge in Paris. This is El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret, where singers, comedians and dancers have traded cheeky songs and quick jokes with packed rooms. Along the Paral·lel, once the city's Broadway, most stages went dark. This one still performs.","normal":"Look up at the windmill on the façade. It's a nod to the Moulin Rouge in Paris, and it marks El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret. Imagine what this stage has seen: singers, comedians and dancers trading cheeky songs and quick jokes with a packed room. All along the Avinguda del Paral·lel, once called the city's Broadway, theatres and music halls competed for audiences every night. Most of them went dark, but El Molino kept performing, and it still puts on shows today. Glance down the avenue and you'll spot other stages carrying the tradition on.","long":"Look up at the windmill on the façade. It's a nod to the Moulin Rouge in Paris, the Montmartre cabaret that opened in 1889 beneath its own red windmill, and here it marks El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret. Now imagine what this stage has seen. Singers, comedians and dancers, working close enough to the crowd to catch every laugh, trading cheeky songs and quick jokes with a packed room. Cabaret like this was never stiff, formal theatre; it was close, cheeky and full of energy. Look along the avenue around you. This is the Avinguda del Paral·lel, named because it roughly follows a line of latitude, a parallel, across the city map. Once called Barcelona's Broadway, it was packed with theatres and music halls that competed for audiences every night. Most of those stages went dark over the years. El Molino kept performing, and it still puts on shows today, so the songs and jokes you're imagining aren't only history. Glance down the avenue and you'll spot other stages carrying the tradition on; the Teatre Victòria and the Teatre Condal both stand on this same avenue. If you come back in the evening, you'll feel the avenue wake up as audiences gather outside the doors. Just remember that people live in the flats above and in the streets behind, so enjoy the buzz, and let the neighbourhood rest when the show is over.","new_facts":["The Moulin Rouge in Paris is in Montmartre and opened in 1889 with a red windmill on its façade","The Avinguda del Paral·lel takes its name from the line of latitude (parallel) it roughly follows","The Teatre Victòria and the Teatre Condal are theatres on the Avinguda del Paral·lel","People live in the flats above and the streets around the avenue"]},"local":{"short":"Notice the windmill above you, then the people around you. This is Poble-sec, where families have lived for generations, right beside Barcelona's most famous cabaret. Do as neighbours do: wander a block into the quieter streets, take a seat at a terrace, and enjoy an evening drink, unhurried and friendly.","normal":"Notice the windmill above you, and then notice the people around you. This is Poble-sec, a real neighbourhood where families have lived for generations, right beside Barcelona's most famous cabaret. A century ago, workers came to the Paral·lel after a hard week to laugh and sing. Today neighbours still meet friends in the side streets for an evening drink or a few tapas. If you'd like to join them, wander a block into the quieter streets, find a seat at a terrace, and enjoy it the local way: unhurried, friendly, and never in a rush.","long":"Notice the windmill above you, the sign of El Molino, Barcelona's most famous cabaret. Now notice the people around you. This is Poble-sec, a real neighbourhood where families have lived for generations, tucked between the Avinguda del Paral·lel and the slopes of Montjuïc hill. The name is Catalan and translates as 'dry village'. Look up at the balconies nearby, where you may spot laundry drying, plants in pots, or someone leaning out to chat with a neighbour below. That's everyday life carrying on right beside the lights of a cabaret. A century ago, workers came to the Paral·lel after a hard week to laugh and sing in its theatres and music halls. That habit of meeting up after work has never really gone away. Today neighbours still meet friends in the side streets for an evening drink or a few tapas. If you'd like to join them, wander a block or two into the quieter streets, away from the traffic on the avenue. Find a seat at a terrace, order something small, and let the evening take its time. A few small courtesies go a long way here. Say a simple 'bon vespre', good evening in Catalan, and you'll often get a smile back. Keep your voice down near doorways and balconies, because people are sleeping, studying or putting children to bed just above your head. Enjoy it the local way: unhurried, friendly, and never in a rush.","new_facts":["Poble-sec lies between the Avinguda del Paral·lel and the slopes of Montjuïc hill","The name Poble-sec is Catalan and translates as 'dry village'","'Bon vespre' means 'good evening' in Catalan"]},"history":{"short":"Stand back and look at that windmill. It has watched over the Paral·lel for more than a century. El Molino opened in the early twentieth century, modelled on Paris's Moulin Rouge, when this avenue was Barcelona's Broadway. Nearly all its halls closed; El Molino survived and still stages shows.","normal":"Stand back and look at that windmill. It has watched over the Paral·lel for more than a century. El Molino opened in the early twentieth century, borrowing its look from the Moulin Rouge in Paris. Back then this avenue was Barcelona's Broadway, crowded with theatres, music halls and cafés. Their audiences were mostly working people, who came to laugh, sing and forget a hard week. As the decades passed, nearly all those halls closed. El Molino is one of the great survivors, still staging shows today, a living piece of the avenue's past.","long":"Stand back and look at that windmill. It has watched over the Paral·lel for more than a century. El Molino opened in the early twentieth century, borrowing its look from the Moulin Rouge in Paris, which had opened in Montmartre in 1889 under its own red windmill. Paris set the fashion, and Barcelona answered with a windmill of its own. Now look along the avenue. Its name, the Avinguda del Paral·lel, comes from the line of latitude, the parallel, that it roughly follows across the city. In El Molino's early years, this was Barcelona's Broadway, crowded with theatres, music halls and cafés. Try to picture the evenings: crowded pavements, open doors, music drifting out into the street. The audiences were mostly working people, who came to laugh, sing and forget a hard week. For a few hours, the Paral·lel let them leave their worries at the door. As the decades passed, nearly all those halls closed. A few theatres still stand along the avenue, among them the Teatre Victòria and the Teatre Condal, but El Molino is the one that kept the old cabaret spirit alive. It is one of the great survivors, still staging shows today, a living piece of the avenue's past. When you walk on, look at the buildings with fresh eyes, and remember that behind many of these façades, ordinary residents live their lives in the middle of all that history.","new_facts":["The Moulin Rouge in Paris is in Montmartre and opened in 1889 with a red windmill on its façade","The Avinguda del Paral·lel takes its name from the line of latitude (parallel) it roughly follows","The Teatre Victòria and the Teatre Condal are theatres on the Avinguda del Paral·lel","Residents live in many of the buildings along and around the avenue"]}},"tip":"People live above the theatres on the Paral·lel: keep it calm when shows let out."},"filmoteca":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This bold concrete building, opened in 2012, is home to Catalonia's film archive. Look at its bare concrete walls and imagine the cinemas inside, screening classics and rare films every day at low prices. It came to this corner of the Raval to bring culture and new life to an old, crowded neighbourhood.","normal":"This bold concrete building, opened in 2012, is home to Catalonia's film archive. Inside, cinemas show classics, retrospectives and rare films every day at low prices, and the archive looks after decades of Catalan cinema. Its arrival in this corner of the Raval was part of a plan to bring culture and new life to one of the city's oldest and most crowded neighbourhoods.","long":"Take a moment to look at this bold concrete building in front of you. It opened in 2012, and it is home to the Filmoteca de Catalunya, Catalonia's film archive. It was designed by the architect Josep Lluís Mateo, and from here you can see how he worked with heavy, bare concrete, letting large surfaces and openings shape the building. Notice how the building opens onto the square, Plaça de Salvador Seguí. Inside, there are two cinemas, and every day they show classics, retrospectives and rare films at low prices, so a night at the movies here won't stretch your budget. There is also a library for anyone curious about film, and exhibitions about cinema and its history. Behind all of this, the archive looks after decades of Catalan cinema, keeping films that might otherwise be lost. Now look around the square itself. The Filmoteca's arrival in this corner of the Raval was part of a plan to bring culture and new life to one of the city's oldest and most crowded neighbourhoods. The Raval has narrow streets, tall old apartment buildings and a long history of working people and newcomers making their homes here, as many still do today. As you take it in, remember that these balconies and doorways belong to residents, so enjoy the view quietly. And if you have an evening free, check the programme. A screening here is a lovely way to end a day in the neighbourhood.","new_facts":["The building was designed by the architect Josep Lluís Mateo.","It stands on Plaça de Salvador Seguí.","It has two cinemas.","It includes a film library.","It hosts exhibitions about cinema.","The Raval has long been home to working people and newcomers to the city."]},"culture":{"short":"Look up at this slab of bare concrete. Since 2012, its cinemas have shown classics and rare films every single day, while decades of Catalan cinema are looked after inside. Imagine those stories flickering just metres away. People live all around here, so linger gently and keep your voice low.","normal":"Look up at this slab of bare concrete. Behind those walls, decades of Catalan cinema are carefully looked after in Catalonia's film archive. Since the building opened in 2012, its cinemas have shown classics, retrospectives and rare films every single day. Imagine all those stories flickering in the dark, just metres from where you stand. The Filmoteca arrived here as part of a plan to bring culture and new life to one of Barcelona's oldest neighbourhoods. The Raval is still home to many people, so linger gently and keep your voice low.","long":"Look up at this slab of bare concrete. It was designed by the architect Josep Lluís Mateo, and it opened in 2012. Let your eyes travel across its surfaces, the way the light catches the grey and the shadows fall into its openings. Behind those walls, decades of Catalan cinema are carefully looked after in Catalonia's film archive. Think of everything that means: faces, streets and voices from years gone by, kept safe so they can be seen again. Inside there are two cinemas, and since the building opened they have shown classics, retrospectives and rare films every single day. There is a library too, where students and film lovers come to read and research, and spaces for exhibitions about cinema. Imagine all those stories flickering in the dark, just metres from where you stand, while the square outside, Plaça de Salvador Seguí, carries on with its day. Two worlds side by side, the old films inside and the everyday life of the neighbourhood outside. The Filmoteca arrived here as part of a plan to bring culture and new life to one of Barcelona's oldest neighbourhoods. The Raval has long been a place where working families and people arriving from elsewhere made their homes, and its narrow streets and tall buildings still hold that life today. Notice the balconies and shutters around you. These are people's homes. So linger gently and keep your voice low. If you can, come back one evening for a screening, and let the neighbourhood's own cinema welcome you in.","new_facts":["The building was designed by the architect Josep Lluís Mateo.","It stands on Plaça de Salvador Seguí.","It has two cinemas.","It includes a film library.","It hosts exhibitions about cinema.","The Raval has long been home to working families and people arriving from elsewhere."]}},"tip":"Tickets are cheap: catching a classic film here is a lovely local evening."},"placa-orwell":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This small square is named after George Orwell, the English writer who came to Barcelona in 1936 to fight for the Republic in the Spanish Civil War. He told the story in Homage to Catalonia. Look at the terraces and chatting neighbours around you: a long way from the barricades he described.","normal":"This small square carries the name of the English writer George Orwell. In 1936 he came to Barcelona to fight for the Republic in the Spanish Civil War, and he described the city in those intense months in his book Homage to Catalonia. Today the square is a lively meeting point in the Gothic Quarter, with terraces and neighbours chatting, a long way from the barricades Orwell wrote about.","long":"You're standing in a small square that carries the name of an English writer, George Orwell. Orwell was a pen name; he was born Eric Blair. In late December 1936 he came to Barcelona to fight for the Republic in the Spanish Civil War. He joined a militia linked to a small party called the POUM and was sent to the Aragon front. Months later, back in the city on leave, he lived through the street fighting of May 1937, when groups on the same Republican side turned their guns on each other, and barricades went up across central Barcelona. Not long after, back at the front, a sniper's bullet hit him in the throat. He survived, and he and his wife Eileen escaped Spain that summer. In 1938 he published Homage to Catalonia, his account of those intense months. Now take a slow look around you. Notice the terraces, the tables close together, the neighbours stopping to chat. Look up at the balconies, where people live their everyday lives above the square, and keep your voice gentle for them. Notice how the narrow streets of the Gothic Quarter feed into this open space, the kind of streets that were easy to block in 1937. The Rambla, where Orwell kept watch from a rooftop during that fighting, is only a few minutes' walk away. Today this is a lively meeting point, a long way from the barricades he wrote about.","new_facts":["George Orwell was a pen name; he was born Eric Blair.","He arrived in Barcelona in late December 1936.","He joined a militia linked to the POUM party.","He was sent to the Aragon front.","He was in Barcelona on leave during the street fighting of May 1937 between groups on the Republican side.","He was shot in the throat by a sniper at the front and survived.","He and his wife Eileen escaped Spain in the summer of 1937.","Homage to Catalonia was published in 1938.","During the May 1937 fighting Orwell kept watch from a rooftop on the Rambla.","The Rambla is a few minutes' walk from the square."]},"culture":{"short":"Find the name sign on the corner wall. George Orwell came to Barcelona in 1936 as a volunteer, not a tourist, and wrote Homage to Catalonia about it. He describes waiters refusing tips and strangers calling each other comrade. Picture that city as you look around these terraces.","normal":"Look for the name sign on the corner wall: this square honours a writer who arrived in Barcelona as a volunteer, not a tourist. George Orwell came in 1936 to fight for the Republic, and turned what he saw into Homage to Catalonia. In its pages he describes a city where waiters refused tips and strangers called each other comrade. Notice the terraces around you and imagine reading those lines here. It's a fine place to open the book, quietly, among the neighbours who call these narrow streets home.","long":"Look for the name sign on the corner wall. This square honours a writer who arrived in Barcelona as a volunteer, not a tourist. George Orwell, whose real name was Eric Blair, came in 1936 to fight for the Republic, and turned what he saw into Homage to Catalonia, published in 1938. In its opening pages he describes a city that felt turned upside down. Buildings were draped with the red and black flags of the anarchists, walls were covered in revolutionary posters, waiters refused tips, and strangers called each other comrade. He admits he didn't understand all of it, but he felt it was worth fighting for. The book sold poorly at first, and only found a wide readership years later, after Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four had made his name. Many readers see Spain as the place where the ideas behind those books took shape. Now look around you. Notice the terraces, the café tables, the mix of languages drifting across the square. Look at the walls of the surrounding buildings and imagine them covered in posters, then notice how ordinary and peaceful they are today. These are homes, so glance up at the balconies and keep your voice low for the people living above. It's a fine place to open the book, quietly, and read a page or two among the neighbours who call these narrow streets home. Orwell came here as an outsider who wanted to understand the city. That's a good spirit to carry with you as you walk on.","new_facts":["Orwell's real name was Eric Blair.","Homage to Catalonia was published in 1938.","In the book Orwell describes buildings draped with the anarchists' red and black flags and walls covered in revolutionary posters.","Orwell wrote that he did not understand everything he saw but considered it worth fighting for.","Homage to Catalonia sold poorly at first and became widely read only years later.","Orwell later became famous for Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four.","His experience in Spain is widely seen as shaping the political ideas behind those later books."]},"history":{"short":"Notice how calm these terraces feel, then picture these Gothic Quarter streets blocked by barricades. George Orwell came here in 1936 to fight for the Republic, and returned from the front to street fighting between factions on the same side. Look at the name sign on the wall: the city remembers him.","normal":"Notice how calm these terraces feel, then picture the same Gothic Quarter streets blocked by barricades. In 1936 George Orwell, the English writer, arrived in Barcelona to fight for the Republic in the Spanish Civil War. He joined a militia, went to the front, and came back to a city sliding into street fighting between factions on the same side. He told it all in Homage to Catalonia. Look at the name sign on the wall: that's a city remembering him. Today it's simply a neighbourhood square, where people live and meet.","long":"Notice how calm these terraces feel. Now picture the same Gothic Quarter streets blocked by barricades. In late December 1936, George Orwell, the English writer, arrived in Barcelona to fight for the Republic in the Spanish Civil War. He joined the militia of the POUM, a small anti-Stalinist Marxist party, and went to the Aragon front, where for months the war was mostly cold, boredom and waiting. When he came back to the city on leave in May 1937, it was sliding into street fighting between factions on the same side. On one side were the anarchists and the POUM; on the other, government forces and the Communists. For several days, Barcelona's centre was split by barricades, and Orwell spent them on guard on a rooftop above the Rambla, a few minutes' walk from here. Soon after, back at the front, he was shot in the throat by a sniper and survived. Then the POUM was outlawed, and he and his wife Eileen had to flee Spain. He told it all in Homage to Catalonia, published in 1938. Look at the name sign on the wall: that's a city remembering him. Now look at the narrow streets opening onto this square and imagine how easily they could be closed with piled-up paving stones, the way Orwell saw barricades built. Then come back to the present. Notice the people at the tables, the neighbours crossing on their way home, the balconies above. Today it's simply a neighbourhood square, where people live and meet, so enjoy it gently.","new_facts":["Orwell arrived in Barcelona in late December 1936.","He joined the militia of the POUM, a small anti-Stalinist Marxist party.","He served on the Aragon front, where for long periods there was little fighting.","He was back in Barcelona on leave during the street fighting of May 1937.","The fighting set the anarchists and the POUM against government forces and the Communists.","The fighting lasted several days.","Orwell spent those days on guard on a rooftop above the Rambla.","The Rambla is a few minutes' walk from the square.","Back at the front, Orwell was shot in the throat by a sniper and survived.","The POUM was outlawed, and Orwell and his wife Eileen fled Spain.","Homage to Catalonia was published in 1938.","Orwell describes barricades built from paving stones."]}},"tip":"Neighbours live around the square: enjoy the terraces, but keep the noise down at night."},"arc-teatre":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"Step under this arch off the Rambla and you enter a novel. In Carlos Ruiz Zafón's The Shadow of the Wind, a young boy is led to a secret library, the Cemetery of Forgotten Books, hidden behind a door in this very passage. It's fictional, but look along the doors and imagine which one.","normal":"Step under this arch off the Rambla and you enter the world of a novel. In The Shadow of the Wind, by Carlos Ruiz Zafón, one of the best-selling Spanish books ever, a young boy is led through these streets to a secret library called the Cemetery of Forgotten Books, hidden behind a door in this very passage. The place is fictional, but readers from all over the world come to imagine which door it might be.","long":"Step under this arch off the Rambla and you leave the busy boulevard behind for the world of a novel. The Shadow of the Wind, by Carlos Ruiz Zafón, is one of the best-selling Spanish books ever, and it was written by a man born here in Barcelona. It came out in 2001 and became the first of four novels built around the same secret place. That place is the Cemetery of Forgotten Books, a vast hidden library where books the world has forgotten are kept safe. At the start of the story, in 1945, a young boy called Daniel Sempere is taken early one morning by his father, a bookseller, and led through these streets to a door in this very passage. Inside, he is allowed to choose one book and promise to protect it, and that choice sets the whole mystery in motion. Now walk a few steps in and let the lane close around you. Feel how quickly the noise of the Rambla fades, how the walls rise on either side, how the light narrows to a strip of sky above. It's easy to see why a writer would hide a secret here. The library itself is fictional, and no door in this lane leads to it. Still, readers from all over the world come to stand where you are and imagine which one it might be. As you look, remember that these doors belong to people's homes, so enjoy the story quietly and let the neighbours go about their day.","new_facts":["Carlos Ruiz Zafón was born in Barcelona.","The Shadow of the Wind was published in 2001.","It is the first of four novels connected to the Cemetery of Forgotten Books.","The story begins in 1945.","The boy is named Daniel Sempere, and he is brought to the library by his father, a bookseller.","In the library, Daniel chooses one book and promises to protect it."]},"culture":{"short":"Look up at the arch above you, framing this narrow lane like a stage curtain drawn back. In Carlos Ruiz Zafón's The Shadow of the Wind, a secret library, the Cemetery of Forgotten Books, hides behind a door here. It never existed. These doorways are real homes; the mystery lives only on the page.","normal":"Look up at the arch above you, framing a narrow lane like a stage curtain drawn back. For readers of one of the best-selling Spanish novels ever, this lane holds a secret. In The Shadow of the Wind, Carlos Ruiz Zafón has a young boy led through these streets to the Cemetery of Forgotten Books, a secret library hidden behind a door in this very passage. It never existed, yet readers worldwide come to imagine it. Notice the doorways: real homes in a real neighbourhood, where the mystery lives only on the page.","long":"Look up at the arch above you. It frames this narrow lane like a stage curtain drawn back, as if the street beyond were a set waiting for its actors. For readers of The Shadow of the Wind, one of the best-selling Spanish novels ever, that's exactly what it is. Its author, Carlos Ruiz Zafón, was born in Barcelona, and he filled his books with the city's shadowy corners. In the novel, published in 2001 and set in the years after the Spanish Civil War, a young boy named Daniel is led through these streets by his father, a bookseller, to the Cemetery of Forgotten Books. It's a secret library hidden behind a door in this very passage, a labyrinth of shelves where books nobody remembers are kept alive. Daniel chooses one, and the novel he picks pulls him into a mystery that shapes the rest of his life. Now lower your eyes from the arch and notice the lane itself. See how the buildings press close, how the walls rise and the sky narrows to a thin strip, how a single doorway can look like the start of a story. The library never existed, yet readers worldwide come here to imagine which door it might hide behind. Take a moment to do the same, but notice too what's really in front of you: real homes in a real neighbourhood, where people live their everyday lives. The mystery belongs to the page; the doorways belong to them, so keep your voice low and your camera kind.","new_facts":["Carlos Ruiz Zafón was born in Barcelona.","The Shadow of the Wind was published in 2001.","The novel is set in the years after the Spanish Civil War.","The boy is named Daniel, and his father, who leads him there, is a bookseller.","The Cemetery of Forgotten Books is described as a labyrinth of shelves.","Daniel chooses a book there that draws him into the novel's mystery."]}},"tip":"It's a narrow residential street: please don't knock on doors looking for the library."},"la-central":{"stories":{"base":{"short":"This bookshop on Carrer d'Elisabets lives inside a former chapel. Where an altar once stood, tables are piled with novels, art books and essays. Look up at the old stone vaults arching over the shelves. Locals come here to browse slowly, and it shows how the Raval's old religious buildings found new lives.","normal":"This bookshop lives inside a former chapel on Carrer d'Elisabets. Instead of an altar, there are tables piled with novels, art books and essays under the old stone vaults. It's one of the city's favourite independent bookshops, a place where locals come to browse slowly, and it shows how the Raval's old religious buildings have found new lives as places of culture.","long":"You're standing on Carrer d'Elisabets, outside one of Barcelona's favourite independent bookshops, and it lives somewhere unexpected: inside a former chapel. The chapel once belonged to the Casa de la Misericòrdia, an old charitable institution of the Raval. Step to the doorway and let your eyes travel upward. The stone vaults still arch overhead, just as they did when this was a place of worship. But instead of an altar, there are tables piled with novels, art books and essays. Notice how the quiet of the old building suits its new use. People here tend to browse slowly, picking up a book, reading a few pages, putting it back, coming round again. The shop is part of La Central, a small group of independent Barcelona bookshops, and this branch is loved by locals as much as by visitors. Now look along the street. The Raval is full of buildings like this one, convents, chapels and charitable houses built over centuries, when this part of the old city was home to hospitals, monasteries and institutions caring for the poor. Just around the corner, the old Convent dels Àngels is now part of the MACBA, Barcelona's contemporary art museum, and the former Casa de la Caritat houses the CCCB cultural centre. Old religious buildings, reborn as places of culture: it's a story the neighbourhood tells again and again. If you go in, browse as the locals do, quietly and without hurry.","new_facts":["The bookshop occupies the former chapel of the Casa de la Misericòrdia, a charitable institution.","The shop is part of La Central, a small group of independent Barcelona bookshops.","The Raval historically housed many convents, hospitals and charitable institutions for the poor.","The former Convent dels Àngels nearby is now part of the MACBA, Barcelona's contemporary art museum.","The former Casa de la Caritat houses the CCCB cultural centre."]},"culture":{"short":"Imagine walking into a chapel and finding the altar replaced by stacks of novels. Peek through the doorway on Carrer d'Elisabets and look up: old stone vaults still arch over tables of fiction and art books. Locals linger here before choosing, so browse gently; this shop is part of everyday neighbourhood life.","normal":"Imagine walking into a chapel and finding the altar replaced by stacks of novels. That's what's happened here on Carrer d'Elisabets. Peek through the doorway and look up: the old stone vaults still arch overhead, but beneath them sit tables of fiction, art books and essays. Readers in Barcelona count this among their favourite independent bookshops, a spot where people linger over a page before deciding. It's a small story the Raval tells again and again, old religious buildings reborn as places of culture. Remember, this is a living neighbourhood, and the shop is part of its everyday life.","long":"Imagine walking into a chapel and finding the altar replaced by stacks of novels. That's exactly what's happened here on Carrer d'Elisabets. Peek through the doorway and look up. The old stone vaults still arch overhead, the same curves that once echoed with prayers, but beneath them now sit tables of fiction, art books and essays. This was the chapel of the Casa de la Misericòrdia, one of the charitable houses that once filled this part of the city. Today it belongs to La Central, a small family of independent Barcelona bookshops, and readers here count it among their favourites. Watch the people inside for a moment. Nobody seems in a hurry. Someone turns a page, then another, before deciding; someone else drifts from one table to the next and back again. The hush of the old building does half the work. Now step back and take in the street itself. The Raval tells this small story again and again. Just around the corner, the old Convent dels Àngels has become part of the MACBA, the contemporary art museum, and the former Casa de la Caritat is now home to the CCCB, a busy cultural centre. Chapels, convents and charity houses, built for prayer and for caring for the poor, have been reborn as places of culture. Remember, though, that this is a living neighbourhood, and the shop is part of its everyday life. If you slip inside, move as the regulars do: quietly, curiously, and with time to spare.","new_facts":["The bookshop occupies the former chapel of the Casa de la Misericòrdia, a charitable institution.","The shop is part of La Central, a small group of independent Barcelona bookshops.","The Raval historically housed many convents and charitable houses caring for the poor.","The former Convent dels Àngels nearby is now part of the MACBA, the contemporary art museum.","The former Casa de la Caritat is now home to the CCCB cultural centre."]}},"tip":"Browse quietly, as you would in a library, and support the shop if you find something you like."}},"activities":null};
